      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>Ride a</p>
        <p>THE ALL-STEEL BiCYCLE and keep. fit mentally and physically. • GUARANTEED .FOR EVER Fitted with Dunlop Tyres and Sturmey - Archer Tr-Coaster. Prices from £7 10. to 216 163 Send a Post Card for "The Boolz of the Raleigh.'</p>
        <p>Widnes: C. Bingham, 7a, Vic- toria-road. Runcorn H. Smith and Son, 22, Greenway-road. St. Helens W. Cook, 107,</p>
        <p>Church-street. Earlestown : J. A. Churchill &amp; Co., Cross-lane. itshton-in-Makerfield: S. Reeves, Warrington-road. . Lowton St. Mary: Birkenhead Bros., 128/146, Newton-road.</p>
        <p>RALE;GH CYCLE CO., LTD., NOTTINGHAM. 'Cycling for, Health and Points ,for Cyclists,' .by Sir Frank Bowden,-Bart., lop pp,</p>
        <p>Frtan Agenis and Bookst;ills.</p>
        <p>il'A'.„' . ' • 0</p>
        <p>is concerned. With a coach-built sidecar par- ticular care has to be taken in the cleaning, however. If you try to rub off the dirt when it is dry you will inevitably scratch -and, line the paint and quite ruin it. Even dust should not be rubbed off dry, as it is gritty stuff and will score a number of 'fine lines., Both dust and mud should be thoroughly soaked off, then swished with a sopping sponge. After sponging, go over the paint with a leather wrung out in clean water until Practically dry. A dab of car polish, applied with a soft rag and finished off with a clean, dry leather will give a fine' shine. A spot of paraffin in the first bucket of water will remove nattd that has been allowed to dry hard on .the coach work, but plenty of fresh water will be needed afterwards to remove the resulting Smears. The only thing to remove tar stains is a spot of fresh butter (not salt) and plenty of elbow grease, applied immediately you get home.</p>
