      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CYCLING AB MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AB MOTORING.</p>
        <p>TRUING SLIGHTLY OVAL WHEELS.</p>
        <p>After a slight accident, it will frequently be found that, while the wheel is true sideways, it will have become slightly oval, or egg-shaped. If it is badly 'out of truth in this direction, of course, it will require rebuilding, but if only slight correction is necessary it is,a very simple matter to put things to rights. The first thing it to find the spots at which the wheel bulges. The simplest way to do this is to remove the tyre, replace the wheel in the forks, strejch a piece .of wire ,across the' forks and fix it just above, and as close as possible to, the rim, and spin the wheel. The rim will touch the .wire at the spots where it bulges, and these spots should be marked with_ chalk. Again turn the wheel slowly, ascertain the low spots in the rim, and mark ;these with, a different sort- of chalk mark. Having found out all the pieces at which the wheel is untrue, take your spoke adjuster first loosen the spokes at the low places and then tighten up those nearest the bulges. Test the rim' frequently during the adjustments or probably you will make matters worse than they- were originally. For the same reason, only screw up or unscrew each spoke a half-turn at a time. Your aim must be to have all the spokes-'of the rim at the same tension, and this you can gauge approximately by feeling them with your fingers. Don't forget to file down the ends of any screwed-up spokes that are projecting beyond the rim.</p>
        <p>HOW TO CARRY YOUR CAPE. Even on a brightsummer's day it nnsafe</p>
        <p>• to start out on anything approaching a long -Aide withont a waterpnoof of some sort or 'another, so ficklei.A..onio. eliula*,tA is the best form Of protection. Nit This note is not to discuss which is the best covering, hut rather to point out that the active service life of the average 'waterproof is cut down to half what it ought to be by the method usually adopted of .squeezing it into a tight sausage-like roll and strapping it beneath the saddle, where • it catches much of the mud that sprays up from , the wheel. ,The most careful cyclist I know • wraps his cape—a three-year-old by the way, and still going strong—in 'oil skin and secures it by, means of two straps to the handlebar. Tightly rolling the "cape does more to destroy the weather resisting qualities of a waterproof than anything else.</p>
        <p>CLEANING A SIDECAR.</p>
        <p>The whole art of keeping a sidecar "as new" as they say in the advertisements, is not 'to let the dust and mud remain .on the coach-work a moment longer than can be helped—in fact, let "do it now" he your motto so far as this job</p>
        <p>• SPEED GEAR g,„k g .vity Fon CYCLES AND MOTOR CYCLLS 5T1r2.14EY-ARCITER GAS ?..7" NOTTINGHAM '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>fIEA</head>
        <p>fIEA</p>
        <p>AN</p>
        <p>You have</p>
        <p>idea</p>
        <p>to why your Motor Cycle is not running</p>
