      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SOOTEED SPARKING PLUGS.</head>
        <p>SOOTEED SPARKING PLUGS.</p>
        <p>Many motor cycles, especially two-stroke engines are seriously affected in their' running powers owing to the fact that the sparking Plugs have become sooted. So many of the two-stroke engines are. both oil and air cooled, and thus it is not surprising that a certain amount of plug trouble should be experietnced. It is quite an easy patter successfully to overcome the diffi- culty. As. a general -rule' the plug, is at right angles to .e 4 cylinder; and in this case a single point plug should be obtained and washers fixed between the plug and the cylinder in such a manner that the Point is upright. This causes the oil -to drain away from the point, thus eliminating in a large measure the sooting of the plug. When the magneto allows it, a plug should be used with a large 'gap, as this makes it practically impossible for the oil to bridge the points. It is a-Nery good plan oceasionally to remove the plug and soak it in petrol all .nlght-: This removes any burnt caoon that there may be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TO CLEAN YOUR CAR.</head>
        <p>TO CLEAN YOUR CAR.</p>
        <p>Many motorists are nowadays looking after their own cars, their chauffeurs having either enlisted or become engaged on .munitions. In the pre-war days—how long ago they seem !—the fact that the car was always bright and clean seemed only natural,- and few, people dreamt how much labour had been expended to have it alwakys spick and span. • Personally I have never possessed a .chaiiffeur, and so the new order of things has hardly affected me. But there are .a few hints that, if followed, will greatly facilitate the maintenance of the smart appearance .of your car. 'grease marks from dirty lingers are a common form of disfigure- ment. Ordinary washing will not remove..thern, and, of course, the varnish suffers consider#y if either paraffin or benzoic!, is used. A good soap lather is the best thing to use. Afterwards swilling from the hose will comtdete the work. The hood can be cleaned by "scrubbing with soap and water, but it should be left up until dry. When the cushions become wet, they should be dried slowly: in moderate heat. Linseed oil is a capital agent to employ for taking off spots left by the mud drying on the body work. ; Waddidg is a good material for applying the oil. The same liquid will remove rain stains off the bonnet. If the woodwork is occasionally polished with some of the special preparations sold for the purpose it adds to the appearance of the car.</p>
        <p>SPOKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>FUERAL OF THE: LATE r 7, . 3. PLumPTorq.</head>
        <p>FUERAL OF THE: LATE r 7, . 3. PLumPTorq.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the funeral took place at the Daresbury Churchyard of the late Mr. Stephen, Plumpton, /managing director of' Messrs. George Plumpton, Ltd., Albion Works, Warrington. The funeral was largely attended. The Vicar of Daresbury officiated. The principal mourners were: 3lrs: Plumpton (widow), Messrs. Herbert, ,George and Frank Plumpton (brothers),. Tom 'and Sam Plumpton (nephews), W. Taylor (brother-in-law), Birken- head;- Mrs. F. Plumpton (Sister-in-law), Mr. E. A. Bradbury (cousin), Mrs. Taylor (mother-in- law), Birkenhead; - and Messrs. H. Cosgrove, C. S. Burgess, W. Parsons and John Moss. Others presenf were Messrs. C. ,Gough, George Wal- lington and,' T. F. Wallington. The firm was represented by Mr. George Plumpton (chairman of the company). The hymns sung were "For ever with the Lord" and "On . the resurrection morning," both favourites of Mr. Plumpton. . Mr. John Wright was at the organ, and pri9r to the com- mencement of the service he played "0 rest in, the Lord," and at the conclusion the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>•. Wreaths- Were sent by the following :—The widow' and family,- "Herbert and Lucy and family," Messrs. Herbert, George and:Frail-1k Plimpton, the employes - of George Plumpton, Ltd.„ Mr. Harry. Milling, Mr. and Mrs. Wood- ward., Mr. and Mrs. J. L. Jones (Manor Farm, Moore), Miss Bessie Dutton, "Annie, and Emmib," Mr. and Mrs. Frank Dumford, "Doris, Gladys and Evelyn," Mr. and Mrs. E. A. Bradbury, Miss E. Taylor, Mr. Cosgrove, Mr. and Alm Taylor, and the Laneashirii Tool Company. / -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ROYAL ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>"The Royal Academy Illustrated" (Walter Judd, Ltd., price 25.) isgued by authority, con- tains over 200 admirable reproductions,in photo- gravure of the pictures and sculptar.n in this year's exhibition, which will be notable for many fine works that the war has inspired. The reproductions include the work of 146 R.A.'s and A.R.A.'s. The book is the first hook of its kind issued by authority of the Royal Academy. It gives one a general conception of the spirit underlying the best work in this year's exhilli- tion, and forms a most interesting souvenir for all who desire a permanent record of the prin- cipal exhibits and for all those who cannot visit the Academy. Local interest in the book is based upon the picture of' a bronze bust of the late John Cros. field. by Henry Pegram, that was recently placed in the Warrington Town Hall by the members of his family. There are several of the works of Sir Luke Fildes, and portraits of Lord Derby ,ad Sir W. H. Lever. s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Barber-ous.</p>
        <p>The barber loomed large in last week's issue, .and I. have somewhat to write respecting him. The very employment of, the common term ha.,,1 caused searching of heart. However it may be with the younger generation in this indispen- sable trade, members of the old school were apt to resent it. I knew one at Elland, in the West Riding (he had gone there from Wharfedale a‘ a young man) in the far4Af days, when that 'district was included in pay itinetary, who, over- hearing the word " barber " in the waiting customers' conversation, would draw .himself up in offended" dignity and interposO the protest., " ' Barber,' sir! 'Hairdresser,' please. My apprenticeship indentures distinctly stated that I was bound to "learn the business of a- hair- dresser; the word ' barber ' never occurred." But perhaps my Yorkshire acquaintance was an exception—like that of the learAdVossius, whose barber, according to Steele, "used to comb his head in iambics." Yes, I think he must have been exceptional, for he ended by cutting his throat with one of his own razors, whicii is. as rare as fora sailor to commit suicide by drown- ing or a chemist by poison. Modern Innovation.</p>
        <p>can well imagine how scandalised I hat poor old fellow would have been could he had foreseen the advent of the lady barber-4 mean hair- dresser,- with apologies to his shade. I note that at last week's local Tribunal an insurance agent obtained temporary exemption on the Plea that he iyas instructing his wife in the business. I have seen such lady agents already at work. • I have just' come• across a remarkable case more to my present point. The other • clay I accepted a friend's invitation to a motor run.- It was a 'hurried call, and taking train to what used to be known.aS "th' w.eyver's seeahpport," I joined hitn at-Littleborough, whence we had a joy ride over Blackstoncdge. We -deseended by way of 'Cragg Vale, and at 'llytholmroyd I called a halt for ,a 'shave. ' The operation was deftly per- formed by the tradesman's wife, and I learned that he, too, had gained a short respite from military service in order to teach her to use 'razor and scissors, and so keep the business going I until his return. She had proved a smart pupil, and when I called 'she was in sole 'charge. Of course, lady, hairdressers have been known for years in some of the great cities. but this is a noteworthy bit of war service. More power to the brave woman's elbow. Plainly, she deserv.ts the encouragement and active support of the • A Bad Spoc. _ _</p>
        <p>I knew one in that same trade who made an unprofitable change just before the war. He ana three or four friends arranged to embark on a hut. building sveculaiion in Canada, and he was so confident of immediate .and permanent success that in winding up his affairs he often made the mistake, in the hearing of customers, cf counting his chickens before thy were hatched. In a word, the scheme turned out a disastrous failure, and the once so boastful and now chastened barber is already back in this country and engaged in his former occupation, though not in the same lOcality.</p>
        <p>Business Improving. " Vigilant's" bit of "Gossip" to the effect that the hot weather was " dtEmping men into hairdressers' shops" and that barbers confess that such weather conditions practically double their business (which must-be particularly wel- come now so many men are away), reminded me of a "Punch" tit-bit which appeared in a similar.li'eek to. last :quite a genetation.-ago. "How'd you like it, sir?" asked the hairdreiiser, as he flourished the scissors above the head of the exhausted customer. "Phew !" was the response of the desperate one, "cut it to the bone."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Nicknames.</head>
        <p>Nicknames.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse hears with Professed in- difference of some calling him "Old Sam," and indeed he is in good company. A pliblic man may wIP feal,flattcred when‘suclt familiar per- sonal refereneerbeCOnie common--as, for ex- ample, "Bobby" Lowe and "Jimmy" Low- ther. After all, to be strict, these are not nick- names; though by no mean,s so nice, that of "Soapy. Sam" for the' famdus Bishop Wilber= force, approximates more nearly thereto. I have come across more amusing instances, but .I fear-I have 'previously given at least some of these, and, burdened with a treacherous memory, one' naturally desires to avoid repeti- tion as far as possible. - r Empire Day. _ _</p>
        <p>• AS I contemplated Wednesday's celebratiOn, my mind went back to a letter ,addressed to the Press in view of a previous festival of the kind. ,It was from the hon. secretary of the School Peace League, and was. signed "Carl Heath." Teachers were thereby invited to °"impress upon the children the humaner and more civilised aspects o British rule as an organised advance in pacification rather than as development of mere force and power." "Empire Day," pur- sued the writer in elaboration of his argument, "as a glorification of an Empire separated in its sympathies from and antagonistic to the rest of the world; Empire Day as a: flag-waving sen- timentalism,- has no iust place in the schools of a civilised nation. Empire Day as a day for the consideration of the duties to humanity of a community of peoples having splendid oppor- tunities for the Oromotion of human liberty, peace and justicevis a matter that may be vital to us all." Ah, well, well ! " Little Willie."</p>
        <p>The "Stage and, Kinema " item concerning Miss Lilian Maitland's debut as "Little Willie" in "East Lynne," sent me afresh to my num- ber of the current "Printers' Pie." Many readers of this column promptly obtained copies, and, with deference to • the superior judgment of more competent critics, I venture to submit that, if not just as original as some, one of the best jokes therein is "The doctor's dileMma." The contributor, H. M. Brock, shows in the surgery a Scottish laddie, having by the hand a younger one in tears, and accompanied by a little sister. Says, the sthall boy: "If ye please, doctor, we've coora to pay the bill. Here's half-a-crown in siller, twa shillings in copper, eighteenpence in stamps; and saxpence in Wullie, and will ye get it oot the noo, because we're late for school ?" The rendering of the, dialect is not impeccable, ye ken, but as a bit of "child study" the sketch is—well, simply immense, to use a convenient modern expression. Snobbery.</p>
        <p>The performers mentioned last week as appear- ing at the " Hip " here and nightly returning. to Manchester for bed and board, set me think- ing of others I have met, actresses and singers especially, whg have treated after that manner hospitable towns whose inhabitants flocked to patronise the entertainment, and . who felt rather snubbed when they discovered that their leading hotel, on which they were wont to pride themselves, was beneath the notice of the dainty creature who, had condescended to give them a. series of turns extending over a week. Once in those parts we had actually a County Court ' Judge made of that sort of stuff. His home was in London. and when he was Ai circuit nothing inferior to Manchester would serve—and his Honour had the extremely bad taste to tell us so in a tactless speech, from the Bench. Warrington may not be good enough for such people as a nermanent abode, bytt. in the absence tof any explanation, one might have sunnoseA they Could have endured a first-class local hotel for a week's engagement.</p>
        <p>Military Etiquette. Should a wounded soldier sporting a panama raise it or salUte on meeting a. lady acquaint- ance? The point is raised by " Critic "in Lymm "Facts and Fancies." Even assuming it to be put as a serious proposition, and that my brother scribe will. not laugh at me for my pains i my own experience were hardly likely to help to a wlution. I have known a soldier re- fuse to reffiove his hat in a Civil Court, and stoutly defend his attitude when challenged; on the other hand. I have known one, on being shown into the editorial sanctum, uncover at once and show the utmost deference to the pen which is said to be mightier than the sword. On the whole, I incline to the opinion that. sup- posing him to be native and not. Colonial, the wearer of the non-military headgear might so far condescend as to " tip " the panama "just a wee."</p>
        <p>A great deal of talent is lost to the world for the want of a little courage.—Sydney Smith</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>£1 FOR 15s. 6D.</head>
        <p>£1 FOR 15s. 6D.</p>
        <p>The Working Men's Chance To Become Successful Investors.</p>
        <p>\\R SAVINGS CERTIFICATE.</p>
        <p>The proposal to institute War Savings Associations in Warrington is one which is deserving of support from all truly patriotic individuals, not only from the standpoint of the immediate national need, but because of the salutary effect that it promises tO have upon people who as yet have never learned the real meaning of saving.. .</p>
        <p>Speaking generally, the opportunity now pre- sents itself to the workers to rally to the support of the Exchequer ,in providing the money s.) very necessary for carrying on this great war and ensuring our ability to,stay the course to the end. The people whose good fortune it is to be in receipt of over £3OO per annum would gladly invest £5OO in this exceedingly reintinerative Government security if they were allowed to do so. Those unfortunate People in receipt of a fixed wage or -salary who have tht.), foresight to appreciate the necessity for saving and, who would gladly take advantage, of the present offer, are, alas, in most cases, so heavily hit by the increased cost of living and the multitude of calls upon their Very diminished sovereign, that they are unable to invest to any great ex- tent in War Savings Certificates. " Take for instance a municipal officer, say a clerk or a married assistant master in an elementary school. AS things are at the present moment, through no fault of his .employers, the wage is hardly to be regarded . as more than a ',lying wage. In fact, ir almost every case reliance has to be placed on ' some other source of income or on past savings in order to meet the calls upon him, and to keep up the appearance which the very natur, ' of his employment and social standing demands. The likelihood of any extensive investment among this class of people is very small. Where then are the people to' whom our local com- mittee must look for the succesSful miming of the associations?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WORK AND MONEY PLENTIFUL.</head>
        <p>WORK AND MONEY PLENTIFUL.</p>
        <p>The people in our large. works are the fruitful field, among whom we must hope to gather.ihe harvest of war savings. Agreed 'immediately, that all engineers are not earning £5 a week, and that many are not in receipt of a much better wage, as far as purchasing power. goes, than they were in pre-war days, still there are large number—a very considerable nuniber— of families in the town where the income has been very materially increased. 'Evidences of this are innumerable. There are woinen folk who have gone out to work who. previously stayed at home; boys and. girls o'n work that_is very highly paid—too highly 'paid,, unless there is the good sense accompanying the earning lo set aside some 6f the " extra " for a possible "rainy day." Work was never better nor money more plentiful among the working classes, than it is to-day, despite the very high cost of living. But this has not called for that farsighted bolicy of saving, that one would faia see among these people. The fact is , the majority of people in every "class," rich, inter- mediath 'and poor, have no idea of the value of saving.</p>
        <p>With -working class folk this is perhaps due to a lack of opportunity; more probably to the want of an object lesson to bring it home te them. Often if a man save it is not for a remote contirlgency, such as old age, sickness or, uneinPloyment. These are allowed to take care of themselves. There must' be a definite object in view; a cup final, a walking day out- ing, or, in a few cases, a "house of furniture." Some stimulus muSt be there to .proauce the desire to save. And this is not real saying after all, but a holding back of money for a short time to spend it in a lump sum, frequently for no lasting benefit. The number of working class people who "set up housekeeping" without a halfpenny to fall back on is really astour4. rk,: and to the handi- cap of a hire-pnrchase debt are added the in- creasing responsibilities which these people so easily call down upon themselves. "It is good for a man that he bear the yoke in his youth" is a true saying as far as saving is concerned. It is then to the young people in our w‘orks that attention should be given by the local Central War Savings Committee at the present time. The sixpences aid shillings so easily coaxed from them by the football match, the theatre and the cinema can be diverted into channels far more useful and much more valuable. Apart from the national gain as far as the war is concerned, the future result will be even more far-reaching and beneficial. 'The saving habit once formed is not easily lost, and now is the time to cultivate it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>REPORTING PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>REPORTING PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Too much emphasis, however, Cannot be laid upon the need to'keep the investors alive as to their financial position as the months go by. It is not enough to assure them that the money is "safe," and that interest will be added each year and (after the first 12: months) each month: .The local Central Committee or the secretary should prepare a list each quarter for a general meeting of associates, giving clearly the position of each member. The fact that it tells them on the back of a 'War Savings Book how they stand is not sufficknt. 'This' demands too much " faith " for people not used to investment. Their savings must have a living reality for them. Perhaps this attitude of Mind is strangq to many, but it is a very important factor in their minds. In fact do we not find it among every section? The ,morning meal is very often enjoyed more because of a rise of 3d. or 6d. in the particular shares held,. not as a speculation, but as a permanent investment by the business man. He likes to see how. things are, although he has no thought of selling out.. To part 'with money and never to hear of it for five years is too much for the average .-rorking man. , . Special- emphasis also must be laid upon the fact that the certificates can be cashed im- mediately for their face value if a time of neces- sity demands it. By applying to the association secretary the 15s: 6d. is returnable, and the /certificate then becomes the property of the association. I.ctily people are content to receive a very small rate of interest or even ,to forfeit interest altogether if the principal is " get-at- able " easily: Of course, one of the great educativt sides of. these associations will, be to encourage the real saving habit.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OPPORTUNITY TO EDUCATE;</head>
        <p>OPPORTUNITY TO EDUCATE;</p>
        <p>A tactful secretary can soon achieve this very necessary piece of social work and thus prevent the calamitous poverty which - reveals itself.. among the wage-earners during periods of trade depression or labour 'disputes. IV is not many years • since the :pitiable spectacle was seen in• Warrington of applicants for free meals at .soup kitchans being fed by ellow-Workmen who had been in receipt of less wages Apart' froth the ' injustice to the careful an more, intelligent man it was a reflection upon the people who ate readiest to cry out for a living wage. The opportunity to educate these. well-meaning . but thoughtless citizens, now. presents itself in the: form of a very. necessary and patriotic scheme for raising a people's war loan. For the sake of the future of' our nation -t. is to be 'hoped' that suceegs -will attend the efforts of the War- rington Central War Savings Committee. S. J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CONSCIENCES.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENCES.</p>
        <p>The Bishop Of Liverpool On The Duties • Of Citizenship.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool having been asked by a conscientious objector to military service what is his "attitude toward such objectors, and if he will make it public, has sent the follow- ing reply:— The Palace, Liverpool, May 22. 1916.</p>
        <p>, Dear Mr. . ,—I have - very decided vpinions indeed about conscientious objectors fo military service. I think that if they decline to fight for their country and are content to let, other people do •it for them, their duty is to leave their own shores and to ta,ke up their resi- dence in some other land where they will not be called upon to bear arms. Freedom of con- science does not mean believing anything that you like, even though it be to the detriment of your neighbours, and declining to perform your duties as Christian men and citizens. This is certainly not the freedom of conscience for which our forefathers kontended, and for Vhich, if alive, they w9121d hae contended to-day. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WORKERS' PATRIOTISM.</head>
        <p>WORKERS' PATRIOTISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon Says Labour must Be Rewarded.</p>
        <p>NATION,LISE RAILWAYS AND SHIPPING.</p>
        <p>Having agreed that England is worth fighting for, as, indeed, all right-thinking people have (as "The Spectator" would say), supporters of ,the National Workers' League held a meet- ing in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday evening to advocate schemes whereby after the war England shall also be more of a place' worth living in for the workers. Their particular desires are :—The national ownership and control of vital industries; the establishment on a democratic basis of defences adequate to the Empire's security, the federation of BritAh Free States, the modification of our international commercial policy, educational reform, agricultural reform, and' adult suffrage. Mr. Stephen °Walsh, M.P. for l.nce, and Councillor Sexton, of Liverpool, had been advertised among the list of sp-...akers, but they were unable to be present. Mr. Jr Elwell (the chairman) .and Mr. J. A. Seddon, -,therefore, held the attention of the audience. Mr. Seddon, it will be remembered with pride, has been 'a Government missionary in France land the United States, explaining to the people of these. countries our view,point. in' the war, and what we are doing to,gainsuccess. When in Los Angeles, in Decenlber,-.1914, he recalled, he met a gentleman wearing clerical garb, who had been ministering to English-speak- ing Catholics in Berlin at the time war was declared. His position gave him access to many influential quarters, and on the 3rd August, he said, he was sitting in the crack cavalry club hi Berlin with two high officers, one of whom ceased . conversing .to go to the tape machine. Coming back to. them he said,, "England has joined with France and Russia. We are beaten, 'I but we will bankrupt Europe, and when. that has been consummated we will all, start off the same mark, and the real Victor of the war will be tlie nation with the greatest recuperative power, and that will be Germany,. because she is the most scientific country in the world." 'Germany, comthented Mr. Seddon with in- tense seriousness, is fighting a" lost cause in the hope that When Europe has been bled white she 'will recover most quickly and then beat the world, but we are not going to let her do- it. War is no rose-water, but nations have decreed that certain devilish. instruments ‘. shall be tabooed.' and, yet, in defiance- of all this, Ger- many has broken, the rules. I have always tried. to avoid a feeling of revenge, but when I saw in France MY own countiymen broken in body and many of them with their minds gone because of being helpless victims of poison gas and, liouid flame. I asked myself, Ts there no nenaltv against them for this? (Applause.) Her fighting at sea hi,s been cheracterised by the same outrage of the prin- ciples of naval warfare, whereas we have always fought fairly and -humanely. No one can say of our men that they have left.men to drown if they 'could ,be saved. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Confidence In Jack.</head>
        <p>Confidence In Jack.</p>
        <p>I won't forget, in a hurry crossing the Atlantic after the' war had beEn in progress about six; months, and the whole- of the German raiders had not been tied' up. As we got beyond the three-mile limit outside New York two 'British sentinels came to see that there were no enemies on beard and,, having ;left us, we then resumed: our 3,000-nile run. All the way there was abiding confidence in the British Navy, and when I. asked an American lady how it was that she had such-great' faith:•inx the Navy she, replied, "Well, you know, ybu have got, a tradition. However brutal soldiers may be, the British Navy have ,alays proved them- selves to be gentlemen of the first water." (Applause.) This was not an ordinary fight of nations' against nations, the speaker continued. It was one of ideals against ideals. Let Germany pre- dominate and our institution y would Melt away -like snow befere _the storm. We, must 'put up with no inconclusive peace, for' that would mean that the inenaCe of Another war would. hang over us. At present we were probably at tin most i_.ritical stage in the struggle', and in the next six months the fate of Europe during the next hundred years would be decided. Some- one had said that the differences between the Allies and the enemy were not 'worth the sacri- fice of one human life. 'That person must be dead to the .tremendous issues involved. (Hear, hear.) "We of the British Workers' League, say that this war is a righteous war," deelared Mr. Seddon, adding .that after the war work must be found for everyone. There must be no unemployment, and everyone must have a living wage. • '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Nationalise Railways And Shipping.</head>
        <p>Nationalise Railways And Shipping.</p>
        <p>, Going further into the aim,s of the Lague, the speaker said that the railways had been taken over by the Government, and gs far as the League as concerned they were going. to stop where they were. (Applause.) The control of railway' in war time had been an absolute necessity, and they must be the ,same in peace time. (Applause.) They could not allow a monopoly to strangle trade, ad pay starVation wages. They were not going to rob the share- holders, but instead .of them having script from the railway companies they would have it from the Government.. The same applied to shipping. If the Mercantile marine had our Navy ,to defend them then we ought to have the. ship- ping. - (Ap'plause.) •,. Another thing they wanted was a reconsidera- tion *of their fiscal sr-,tem, the education system (whereby, lads of ability could win through to the ~Universities) and an alteration in the franchise. Every man who had fought for Eng- land should have a vote. (Applause.) Thy 14ere not out to destroy the Labour party, but to give it life and substance. They were not out for a class war; but for the tunity of the nation. •</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Elwell. who presided, said the war was teaching us that the freedom we possessed could only be maintained by the British Empire using its whole strolgth, and,• therefore, they believed that all who enjoyed its advantages should share in its responsibilities.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ffodson, the Secretary pro. tern, called upon Alderman. Bennett; who was. sitting in the auditorium, to propise a vote of thanks to Mr. Seddon for. his address. The Alderman responded, remarking that he hoped justice [would be done to labour after the war. .Mr. Hodson seconded, and the Motion was •carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>, NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>, NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>\ "The European Anarchy." . By E. Lows Diel4nson. --- London: George Allen and Tinwin, Ltd. 28. 6d: net.._ _ _ , •</p>
        <p>I I Mr, Lewes Dickinson has Written a number of pamphlets dealing' with the present inter- national criSis since the ,war broke out, and his I point of view is fairly well-known. In theiPfie- , sent book he gives a short account of the policies .of the diifferent belligerent countries with the object of showing that European anarchy is the cause,of the war, lie does not dispute the I fact that 'Germany pretipitated the outbreak: he frankly recognises this, but regards it as only the superficial cause, the real cause lying far deeper, among the conflicting ambitions and .mutual suspicions of the different European nations. . He devotes considerable space to an, examination of the policy of Germany, in which the shows admirable impartiality and fairness. lie rejects the theory that the war is the result of a predetermined policy on the part of Ger- many as improbable. The section of , his book in which Mr. Lowes Dickinson discusses "Opinion in Germany " is extremely interesting and valuable, as is also the section dealing with "Opinion about Etermany." • Mr. LOWeS 'Dickinson supports the views he puts forward by cogent argument and quota- tion. He makes great use of the opinions ex- pressed at various dates anterior to the war by various Belgian diplomats, and published since the war comAienced, as to the direction in which events were moving during the last few years! Whether the time is yet ripe for the solution of Present troubles which he puts forward we may at least be permitted to question. But that there is a not inconsiderable amcunt of truth in much that he urges, and that it is desirable that our people should recognise thiS, we do not for one moment doubt: We can very cordially recommend the book as being one of the most dispassionate and judicial works published on the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE MILK SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H, Green, of Willesborough, in a letter to the " Times " points out that the dis- tributhig companies in London, as a rule, insist upon ,their contracts beginning and ending in spring, when everybody's cow has a ,calf. when cost of production is reduced,. and when milk is plentiful. The result is that once a farmer has been unwise enough to enter into this arrangement he is for ever in the hands of the middlemen. He suggests that farmers should make their contracts to begin and end at Michaelmas. Here lies excellent ground f(•r combination on :the part of farmers to throttic. a growing evil completely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PIGS FOR PROFIT.</head>
        <p>PIGS FOR PROFIT.</p>
        <p>The. old, old question, "Do pigs pay ?" is not in fashion just now, when prime small bacon' pigs are fetching nearly is. per lb., says "Farm and Home." The, actual average price is 12s. 9d. per stone, and in at least one market (Derby) the full _shilling has been recorded. At present values there is no doubt at all about the profit,. notwithstanding the bigh cost of food, but it would obviously be foolish to found. an., argument in favour of bacon production generally, on •results at • such a time as. this.. Recent events have brought the pig into •great prominence, for in Germany the large stock always -kept in time of peace has proved an enormous advantage, while we in England are suffering for our. past neglect, :but all that,,,is beside -the question as to whether a good , fair prOfit can always' be made Thy .skilful -manage- ment. I think the answer is decidedly " But it is not a business that can ,be successfully. conducted by haphazard treatment. Public trials • have recently shown that t acon can at the present 'moment be produced at gd. per lb. up to a certain age and weight, but that beyond that limit the cost rapidly increases. That is a most important point to bear in mind, and an unanswerable argument in 'favour %bf early maturity and marketing. Th.en 'there is the question of breed and that of the selection of foods.. in both of which may be founl a mar-, gin of difference between profit and. loss. Prob- ably the food is the, more potent factor of the two. To be at all sparing with the rations is a fatal mistake.' The pig should always have as much as he can clear up-, and, as regards quality the best is the cheapest. A. combination which cannot be beaten is one of barley meal, potatoes, tlnd. 'skim milk, but the potatoes should be, steamed and mot boiled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>E. 640 FOR A SINGLE BEAST.</head>
        <p>E. 640 FOR A SINGLE BEAST.</p>
        <p>Efforts are to be made at the .gift sale which' is to be held for. the Agricultural Relief;iof .Allies frund'. at the Royal. Show at Manchester next month, with a view to setting up' a new price record for a single beast. The record at present is the £B4O which Lord Northbrook, act- ing as auctioneer at a gift sale at' Basingstoke, secured for a blue grey steer. Previously his Lordship had sold, for the same fund, a heifer for £529 at Reading, while the Duke, of Port- land, who is president of the fund, sold a beast at a Mansfield gift sale for £355. The Agri.' cultural Relief, of Allies Committee, has raised £BO,OOO for the relief of the ruined peasants in allied countries.' Of this amount Lancashire and Cheshire together have 'contributed close on £lO,OOO, and head the list of counties. York- shire occupies twelfth place with £1,795.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SUMMER PASTURES.</head>
        <p>THE SUMMER PASTURES.</p>
        <p>We, may take it ler granted that all . classes of stock are no 7 at grass or mainly dePendent upon green food, though: with heavy rainfalls and a low temperature it can scarcely be said the month. has been as favourable ag one could wiSh, •</p>
        <p>-Wherever sourness of pasture prevails—a state, of affairs easily discernible by noticing the rank appearance of the grass—it is best to run the scythe over such patches, and us the cdarse fodder thus obtained for toiving haystacks, and so. forth. By adopting this course there is a possibility of making the land really productive, instead of being a source of loss. Another way to increaSe our yield of stock food from on-v; summer pastures is by scattering the dropping as frequently as possible. When they are allowed to remain untouched it means the loss of almost a square yard of grass fer the reinain- der of -the. summeri for it will net be until the autumn, 'when the early frosts have sweetened the rank herbage, that the grass Obtained from this overdose of manure will become .accept- able. On the other band, if, . when • tb., pasture has been closely fed, it is the rule to knock the manure ,about, the .;ilighter dressing thereby obtained will''leave no unpleasant effects; the sward will remain as even :is a lawn, and there will be an absence of the rank, tufts which so often disfigure an ,uneveilly-gravd pasture. The top-dressing business'—if one may describe it as such-,--is suitable w.prk for June,. .when the .first tush, of grass ins 'ref;ri• off.—Staffe:...lshire,' in "Farm aud -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>EAR_LY POTATOES.</p>
        <p>'As we are not yet out of the v.-ood as" requrds frost it will be advisable where the .hrtalm of potatoes is just peering through the soil to give this some protection .by drawing \a \littlesil over it, and ,thus it maybe save the er.2p. We have also with dd,S.antdge used i little loose litter, evergreen boughs, or hc4s coverediwith canvas. '</p>
        <p>THINNiNG THE BLOOMS ON FRUIT TREES.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine who, last Autumn, had planted a number of fruit trees, including apples, pears, plums, &amp;c., was telling me how delighted he was 'on seeing so many flower buds on. the trees. Far too many owners of fruit trees think they have made a. very !bad' bargain' if the trees they have just nlanted do not bear fruit the first season after planting. • I told my friend, that if he desired the trees to do well in coming years he ought to remove all the flower bads,- except in cases where. he was anxious to verify the name of any particular variety—evltn then only leaving sufficient- for the' purpose. The great point as regards newly-planted trees is to encourage. them to make free growth the first year, and this is impossible if the trees ore allowed to 'carry a crop. By removing the Rower buds we conAentrate the whole energy of the tree into the g‘rowth of branches and roots. The more freely a tree grows in its younger stages the greater arc the prospects of realising a full crop in years to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>STAKING PEAS.</head>
        <p>STAKING PEAS.</p>
        <p>Early staking no doubt helps the young plants in many ways, as it protects them, and also checks the attack of sparrows and wood pigeons,, Which, in wooded districts;,' do great damage to the young .plants if left unstaked long after they are through the ground. Before staking it is a good' plan to 'draw up a little ridge of., soil along' each side of the row—not close to the row—as this will help to retain any rain- that may . fall or, any-, artificially-applied water. All Too often we see the stick's so placed that the tops meet, 'with the inevitable result that by the time the plants reach their full height they become a tangled .masS. If the sticks- are set wider apart at the top than at the bottom the plants have room to branch out, thus increasing their bearing. All pea sticks should have 'abundance of spray on them, and be long ehough to reach one foot, or even two feet, above, the advertised, height of the variety. When the principal stakes have been placed in' position finish off by sticking between them short ,pieces of the spray cut off when trimming the supports, these giving the young plants a start until they reach the brushwood higher up. —Wick,- in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>IMPROVING WESTERN CANADIAN</head>
        <p>IMPROVING WESTERN CANADIAN</p>
        <p>GRAIN.</p>
        <p>0 , The special seed-grain competition for tha •local members of the Grain Growers' Association throughout the Province of Saskatchewan will be held at the Annual Summer Fair in Regina. The event is arousing considerable interest ill the country districts, and already there are in- dications that a large- number of the 1,100 local farmer members will be represented in the com- petition. In order to ensure that only the best grain grown in each district be placed in the exhibit, elimination contests are being hold in some places. 'This should result in an exhibit of more than usual excellence. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</head>
        <p>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S WORK IN BRIEF.</p>
        <p>Shade is necessary for the growing stock dur- ing the hot weather. By placing the houses near a hedge or ditch the birds will take advantage of the natural shade.</p>
        <p>A constant supply of clean water is necessarl. It should, always be kept in the shade. • Leave the doors of the houses wide open during the day; and the brooder top open from 10 aan• to 4 p.m.</p>
        <p>Broody hens will be more plentiful now They should be placed in the coop, immediately they show signs of broodiness. Broodies are in great demand by bonmekecpers, and old birds should be disposed of in this way. The price obtained is satisfactory. Useful Meals far Summer Feeding.</p>
        <p>The mash must be bulky. Malt culms and clover meals are light and bulky. Bran and biscuit meal are very useful for suminer feeding. Through the summer months, rich foods such as peameal and maizemeal mar be left out •of the mash, and the amount of green food in- creased,</p>
        <p>Remember that during the hot weather mash will turn sour in a few hours. Should sour food Joe gi+en to chickens the results will be disastrous. It is best to prepare fresh mash for eachsmeal. Milk must be used very carefully for growing chickens. Flaked foods are very useful, since they are bulky and easily prepared.—" PoultrY World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>BiTS FOR SEGINNERS.</head>
        <p>BiTS FOR SEGINNERS.</p>
        <p>The Use of Nest Eggs. The use of nest eggs ;will not increase the productiveness of the hens. They simply teach the hens to lay in a place chosen by the poultrr keeper, i\Test eggs, when filled with insect, poi-der, may be useful to keep .the vermin down, but ,the beginner must not use this method of keeping the setting' hen free from insects. • The Cause of Abnormal Eggs.. , The majority of abnormal, eggs are caused bi an over-fat condition, or are the result of en pver-stjmu.lating diet. The two chief causes a shell-less eggs are a shortage .of grit, and fright Pale-yolked 'eggs are usually the result of 3 shortage of green food or underfeeding.</p>
        <p>The Indian Runner Ducks. The padian Runner duck is undoubtedly the most prolific layer of any duck we have, and if; will ,conipare..tvery. favourably With the average fowl. Cottagers or others with a plot of .land at their dipposal Might do worse than invest in 0, pen of Runners. , The Two Classes of Ducks. ' Ducks may. he divided into two classes—the egg-producer and- the 'table bird. In the first class maY be placed the Indian Runner, the Buff Orpington, the Khaki Campbell, the Coaley Fawn. In the second class may be placed the Aylesbury and the Rouen. A cross between. these ' two ,is .often advantageous. Although 'ducks may be successfully reared without swimming water, the results will be more satisfactory if this can be provided. At any rate, to rear ducklings on a large scale without swimming water would prove but un- satisfoctory and unprofitable .to the beginner.</p>
        <p>Disposing of the Cockerels.</p>
        <p>The cockerels from the February-hatched. broods- will be almost ready for the market. \ They. should be separated from the rest. of the I stock and fed on fattening foods such as Sussex ground oats, boiled maize, and barley or pes" The 'birds should be fed three time 13; day, .and their food should consist entirely 751 soft food. Do not all,ow the birds too much exercise, and see that the birds are not over- fed, otherwise loss of appetite will result. Give them as much -as they will clear up in ten k] minutes, and do not leave food in the troughs.-- " Poultry World."</p>
        <p>DIFFERENT TYPES OF CHICKENS. The demand for eggs is probably greater than ever before, and every indication is-that there will .pro.sperity for. the iionitry", keeper owing to the great falling oil in foreign supplies. .Thus the natural inference is; that the British producer will at last assert himself, and never again allow the foreigner to dominate</p>
        <p>our markets: Man Y--individual-eases of develoy7</p>
        <p>ment..have come Tinder _,our _notice (hiring the</p>
        <p>last twelve months and with the growing 11" crease in the number of small holdings, "R which poultry play such an iMportant part, ,v" shall see a still greater output of eggs. . Why Not Table .Fowls? The same imprqement and the same increased development in table poultry is not nearly 5° apparent; as a' matter ,of fact it is very doubt- ful whether this side of the industry has' even maintained its former position either in quan- tity or quality. We. are, of course, speakinj generally and 'not' of .any, one district. We have' made wide inquiries thioughout the entire poultu-k-eeping districts of England, and Ive are sorry to have to admit that the table quali- ties of the larger proportion of ,the poultry sold for consumption leave a great deal to be desired. This, at first sight, seems somewhat difficult t 0 'account for, but on closer inquiry it is not realbr so. The conclusion that one is bound to arrive at is that 'the great and growing demand fol. eggs, • and at such extraordinary high prices. has 'tempted existing poultry-keepers to confine their ehergies to egg, production, and it has als° tempted non-poultry keepers- to take up the business. The Two Qualities. The great incentive in both cases is to cater for the demand for eggs. Naturally, to, do 5° the most prolific laying breeds have been selected ;for the ,purpose generally to the eS- elusion of allpther Varieties, and, consequentlYi since no one breed excels both as a layer ane as:a table fowl, the non-sitting races Shave beeu given the preference, while. the edible proper" ties have, been sacrificed for the. domestic egg' We do not Mean to suggest that table poultrYt should. take precedence over egg production; hu there ro -reason why the two should not he' A specialised in. It is perfectly true that one breed will not adequately provide for both CO modifies, and that the purely egg-laying type ° hen :is practically of no Value for producing table chickens.—" Farm and. Home."</p>
        <p>THE NEXT GERMAN HARVEST' , A correspondent of the "Daily TelegrWine writes from Amsterdam that eventual in Germany is inevitable. "According to, able information which has reached ItteLb. says, "the next harvest is bound to re- failure. It is not so much the immediate 120, sent, but the hopeless prospeet for the f11tw"1. fe° next winter at the latest,--which has putmat into the hearts of the •Government. It is —ace which impelled them to their desperate 1)os venture through the medium of the'negatiati;:o7, with the 'United States. Not from any ullrge ourable climatic conditions, or from sliorilled of labour, is it thaethe next harvest is d°°l3lbe to comparative failure. • The reason is Wripr. found solely and simply in the blockade. 'itio man crops will be poor because the Ger lei" ground is impoverished, and the ground 15t off poverished because the British Navy has clit, of from Germany all its oversea suppliesW cattle feeding-stuffs; secondly, of aram manures.</p>
      </div>
