      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ARCHDEACON'S FIRST</head>
        <p>ARCHDEACON'S FIRST</p>
        <p>CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Churches And Officials Must Stand Shoulder To Shoulder.</p>
        <p>a The Rev: Archdeacon Howson &amp;livered his 11:8t charge as Archdeacon of Warrington at Parish Church on Wednesday morning. l'he Service was of a very impressive character, 111;d considering the pressure .of business now- there wits a fairly large congregation of churchwardens and sidesmen from different urches in the borough and district. The ser- ce Was taken by the Rector, the Rev. Canon nhs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHURCH'S RESPONSIBILITIES.</head>
        <p>CHURCH'S RESPONSIBILITIES.</p>
        <p>PeHow-Workers For The Nation And</p>
        <p>For Christ.</p>
        <p>- 4, The Archdeacon, in his "charge," an archaic ‘erta which indicated the distinctive character °f his work and their work, said his work was to admit to office and theirs to accept; both had realise that they had a burden. to carry. ere never was in their experience a time wlten that sense of trusted responsibility was Mare fully illustrated than to-day. The ter- e struggle going on in the world only served Itcil throw up into greater and clearer outline ° need of doing their duty and accepting re- eponsibility. Ile greeted them as -fellow workers for the, nation, as well as for the Church. The motto of the Borough of Warrington was It symbolic message: "God giveth the increase." It was significant that a great commercial tentre should take its inspiration from the Word of God. It was significant of the spirit of their forefathers, who traced their success to 40d in no artificial or unnatural manner. It ,\!aa good when the laity felt that it was to the 'land of God they looked for that measure of a2ccess in enterprise, which was the reward of miligent service. tjlaving paid a tribute to his predecessors in tiaa office—Archdeacon Madden and Archdeacon 4130 oner—and referred to the losses the Arch- ,Leaconry had sustained during the year by gtath, including the death of the Rev. James 'arson rector of Newton-in-Makerfield; Colonel Wit • hington, a notable and generous friend; and Ralmforth, lay representative of St. Peter's, arrington, he said they had several church- Ordens away on active service. They remem- ired with pride and gratitude the .names of Ose men in the Archdeaconry who 'had given. '-ucir lives for their King and country. Little Things of Real Importance. t It Was sometimes well, said the Archdeacon, " 'IV emphasis on certain principles which lay 4t the base of the two offices of churchwarden 431,N sidesman. The duties of churchwardens reference firstly to the care of the church „7"1 churchyard, and the ornaments, goads and -;?neY of the church; secondly, to the mainten- -:4ce of order in the church and churchyard; Vld thirdly to discipline, the urging a habit of eblic worship, and to the life which such a bit pre-supposes. Might he come with them a stranger to their churchyard? Was it not "4IY tidy, but eared for? Even if it be but a tall, not a burial ground, it was a garden. witter or refuse, jampots, broken or whole, eald not be tolerated in a private garden. ,;::11:1Pulous and reverent care was not expen- re"-e, and it conduced to help people to get OdY for worship. On the church door, per- -03,8, there was a notice board, but there might laYers of old notices left from the ages of a :reetful past. Would that be allowed on their ere publicly It might be urged those notices b:31.43 Publicly affixed and should be publicly re- Ilvecl• Why? How many wardens went into gi" belfry? It was true the bellringers were ,P,Pointed by the incumbent, but they were :41(1 by the wardens. Were the bells in good ;I:der and the ringers men of good character? .o,ere should 'be a careful overhauling of the us at least once in five years. By so doing ti!TY would,save time and money. Coming in- talge the church, the returns assured the Bishop, torough his deputy, that everything was done ti Make and to keep the building scrupulously On; The vestry was part of the fabric, an 4 ;31iggested a little care about its furnishing, eh as the provision of a diocesan calendar, of the Church of England, and the parish 17agazine from the first year of its publication, ticplnd and kept for reference. • They were little 08,8, but of real interest in the history of the of the diocese, and the Church of Eng- hd.</p>
        <p>Need for Unity Front. IthilleY were passing through a testing time, ri4l ele -and laity alike must feellit. Never van th '3 there a time when it was more needfill for tdae°l as church officers to stand should&amp; to 4.oulder and show a united front. While they :11 not minimise their differences, let them ahagnify their agreements. This awful war had tob"r11 how nations differing widely could unite 41;,ene sacred purpose, though with a bitterly n 1 result. Let them aim at a unanimous th• u,teh life. Uniformity they did not ask for; tiould spell monotony; but to aim at being • ciie mind must do good. The Women and Children's Part. ijheY must be ready to welcome the assistance ki large measure and degree of our woman- He did not say how, for that was a pro- bur Which would be solved as they went on, olv„.,theY must be gratefully sympathetic. They more than they had any idea of to the ,artmlietian women of our land. Let them have all °Pen mind and a readiness of welcome to tkil Project which might bring within measur- e distance a co-operation at once practical atki cm, reasonable. They must be awake to the '„ef the children. There were some parishes ith‘n? Archdeaconry where there were no day ver°°l.e. and probably in those parishes there of ' means taken to supplement the teaching real? Council schools. But where, there was a ke` Interest, not of interference with manage- • Or captious criticism of method, Church thEtrs Could help. They were right to claim kid _ligion should be reatore.d to, its riahtful 4ikt; 'erninating place- in the whole scheme of 'nal education. They must see to it that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>REV. T. VICKERS.</head>
        <p>REV. T. VICKERS.</p>
        <p>Valedictory Presentations At</p>
        <p>St. Clement's Mission.</p>
        <p>A large and regretful audience assembled in St. Clement's Mission Room on Monday even- ing to pay an an revoir tribute to their worthy curate, the Rev. T. Vickers, who is leaving the parish to take up a post at Liverpool. Opportunity was .taken of making a presenta- tion of a purse of gold and a travelling bag, and as numerous compliments were paid to Mr. Vickers, and as he replied, tears fell from the eyes of several in the audience.</p>
        <p>H. Houghton, in asking the Rector to make the presentation, said the example which Mr. Vickers had shown them was superb. His loving 'kindness to all was indescribable. Although his work there had finished, yet he was only passing from one sphere to another, and he hoped that it would be one of still greater usefulness. They all knew what he had done for them, and he was sure it was a very kind Providence that brought him in their track. On behalf of the congregation. of St. Clement's he offered him their most hearty thanks, and wished him God-speed. (ApPlauge.) In making the presentation, the Rector said it was with much feeling they met there that evening. They all regretted losing Mr. Vickers, and he was sure they were all grateful for what he had done for them during the past six and a half years. They had had some very hard- working curates, but he was sure none had done more than Mr. Vickers. He had led them on gently and wisely, and during all that time he (the Rector) had not heard a murmur against him. He, along with Mr. Buckmaster, might have left them two or three years ago to have gone to Padgate, but they preferred to work in town. Now the time had come that he should go, and he (the Rector) thought he was acting quite rightly in doing so. It was a great loss to him (the Rector) and also to the people. (Applause.) _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Loth to Go.</head>
        <p>Loth to Go.</p>
        <p>On rising to reply, the Rev. T. Vickers was greeted with loud and prolonged applause, the audience giving three hearty cheers and also singing " For he's a jolly good fellow." When thedeMonstration had subdued, Mr. Vickers said he had been glad to labour as a working parson among working people. He was grateful for all the support given to him, and would like to offer his sincere thanks to Miss Boardman, who, from the time he came to St. Clement's, .had done for him everything possible in her power. Mr. Houghton also had done everything pos- bible for five long years, and in him he always had a loyal friend. Mr. J. Whalley (caretaker) and his wife had rendered very good services, as also had the choir, sidesmen, Sfinday school teachers, Bible-class and sewing meeting. He was also very grateful to Capt. Sankey, Mr. Gibson and Mr. Essenhigh, of the C.L.8., and to many others too numerous to mention. For the kindness and training he had had under the Rector, he was extremely grateful, and it was chiefly due to Canon Willis that they stood *here they did to-day. There were many absent ones whom he heartily wished were present—those who were out in Flanders and other places. One of them, alas !—Charles Shepley—one of the best boys with whom he had to deal, had laid down his life. He was a thorough Christian, and be hoped all others would try and be like him. There had been several changes at St. Clement's, and a great one had taken place in their church. They should love that church, for they would not get another place like it. He'(Mr. Vickers) would not, for it was a place where they could go in joy, sorrow, or pain. The centre of the church was not the man in the pulpit, but the altar, and the centre of their religion should be He who came to them in their trouble and joy alike. Urgency of Foreign Missions.</p>
        <p>"Two years ago," continued Mr. Vickers, "I offered myself to the Bishop of Zanzibar for mission work, but it was God's will that I should not then go. I still mean to go, how- ever, if God allows it. You should not forget the Church in India and Africa. We have sent .250 a Year to the Foreign Missions, and I hope you will keep it up. You have collected .247 already this year." Referring to the work of the future, Mr. Vickers said he knew they were glad that Mr. Armour was going to take on his (Mr. Vickers's) work. He knew they would help him in every way, and he would ask them to remember that different men had different ways of working. They should all live life to its highest, fullest, and noblest point. There were much brighter days in-front than there had been behind. They should look after the boys and girls, for upon them depended the manhood of the future.</p>
        <p>Concluding, Mr. Vickers said he was leaving them solely for experience, and it was causing him much heart-searching. He was much more sorry than he could express. "But I must do my duty, and although we shall be divided in space, we shall not be in spirit," he said, amid applause.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. M. Buckmaster,. the Rev. S. G. Stanton, Mr. F. Darby, Mr. Armour and Col. Mocatta all spoke in glowing terms of Mr. Vickers's good work, and expressed regret at his departure. A suitable concert was given, the artistes being the Misses May Cross, Jessie Berry, Edith Glover and Alice Burgess, Mr. Berry, Mr. Bes- wick, and Master Neville Davies. Mr. F. Darby and Mr. E. Dell accompanied.</p>
        <p>A Country Joke. An old bachelor, after all his abortive matri- monial attempts, exclaims :—" When I remem- ber all the girls I've met together, I feel like a rooster in the fall, exposed to every weather; I feel like one who treadi alone, some barn-yard 'all deserted, whose oats are fled, whoso hens are dead. and 6ft to rnarket started."</p>
        <p>\ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S ATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>REV. W. AND MRS. REID.</p>
        <p>Presentations On Their Depar-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ture For London.</head>
        <p>ture For London.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTE FROM TOWN AND CHURCH.</p>
        <p>No fewer than four presentations were nuide to the Rev. W. Reid and ,Mrs. Reid at a social gatheting on Monday evening ;n the St. John's Presbyterian Church, Wilderspool-causeway. The occasion was the leave-taking of Mr. and Mrs. Reid for a new sphere of religio is activity at Trinity Church, Canonbury, London, N.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Reid has been minister at St. John's for seven years, during which time the Church has made splendid progress. In his labours for its welfare, Mrs. Reid has been a constant and 'able helpmate, and by their activities and lovable characters they have gained the esteem of not only the members of their own Church, but the townspeople. Mr. Reid's activities have gone beyond the sphere of his congregation. He has taken a deep interest in the religious and social welfare of the town as a whole, and it seemed only a fitting con- clusion to his services in Warrington that his laudable work should be recognised in civic quarters. Alderman P. Peacock, the Mayor, honoured the occasion with his presence, and paid a tribute to Mr. Reid's work in the borough. • There was a large gathering of members cf the congregation and friends. Mr. W.' Russell presided, and others present were the Revs. W. T. Derry, j. J. Glover, H. Ogden, j. Paton Rogerson, Nantwich, and R. j. Wright, Bowdon.</p>
        <p>The presentations consisted of a beautiful sitting-room clock and purse of money to the Rev. W. Reid,\ and a gold pendant, set with pearls, to Mrs. Reid. a notepaper embossing stamp from the Sunday school to Mr. Reid, and ,wbook to Mrs. Reid from the members of the British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MINISTER OF PARTS.</head>
        <p>A MINISTER OF PARTS.</p>
        <p>On behalf of the Free Church Council, the Rev. W. T. Derry said thpy had no stauncher minister in the town during the past seven Years than the Rev. W. Reid. He commented upon the friendly spirit prevailing among the ministers of the town, and declared that if Ministers were not friendly they could net ex- pect the Churches to be. The spirit of frater- nity had something to do with the unity, of Churches more than some people thought. The congregation of St. John's had fond their minister to be a man of knowledge, and a man of experience, watchful and sincere. They had been' favoured in his ministry. (Applause.) The Chairman voiced on behalf of the congre- gation the sense of regret they felt at the de- parture of the reverend gentleman and his wife, and expressed the hope that they would find happiness and help in their new sphere of labour. During the ,seven years Air. Reid had been among thef. the Church :lad been very progressive, and they had worked together</p>
        <p>harmoniously. The congregation 'desii(;el in some tangible way to_express their appreciation df the services Mr. and Mrs. Reid had ren- dered to the Church, and he called upon Mrs. Hawthorn and Mrs. Brown, two members of the congregation connected longest with the Church, to make the presentations. This the ladies did in it graceful way, which was followed by a third nreseiftation. made by Mr. R. Mungall, on behalf of the Sunday school staff and scholars.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mungall said the Rev. W. Reid had taken a great interest in fostering the young life of his Church. It was, perhaps, a sail fact that there was a tendency among ministers to divorce the Sunday school from the Church. Wherever this was done it was fatal to Church life. The life of the Church and the Sunday school should be interwoven; unless this was done, no minister could hope for success, and did not deserve success. The Sunday school was the feeding stream of a Church. They admired their minister because he had fostered and nurtured the young life of his Church. (Ap- plause.) A pleasing tribute to the work Mrs. Reid had done on behalf of the British Women's Temperance Association was paid by Mrs. Owen, who handed to Mrs. Reid a sheaf a lovely lilies and a book, "The Life of Frances Willard," as a token of esteem from the ladies of the Association. Mrs. Owen said that though she was not a Presbyterian, she loved all who loved the Lord Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>REV. W. REID AND THE BOYS' HOMECOMING.</head>
        <p>REV. W. REID AND THE BOYS' HOME- COMING.</p>
        <p>Responding on behalf of his wife and him- self, Mr. Reid said their work on behalf of the British Women's Temperance Associatipn had been gladly done. They had been in the Midst of a companionship which had been in accord- ance with their own thoughts and sentiments. They might wonder why he was going away, and where the necessity came in. He might give reasons for going and reasons for staying. Every one of the reasons whin he could advance, to his .mind, seemed swept away by the realisation of his own soul, that the hand of which he had spoken of last night 1-ad come out of the unseen, and was pointing his way for him from here to yonder. His one great pang of regret was that he would not be in Warrington when the boys came home from the Front. He would not be the minister at St. John's to welcome them. ,1-Te would give a great deal to be with them When that happy day came. They had prayed earnestly and regularly for their boys, and the answer would cetme. They would have great joy when that day came. Another reason for the sadness ef his parting was that the boys and girls of the Sumlay school, and those who attended the morning services, might have received more of his interest and companionship. The relationship between a minister and his congregation was a very peculiar one. The relations which existed between him and the souls of his people was altogether unparalleled in any other walk of life. If the relations were happy there was no happier state in the whole world. If e they were unhappy, and supposing that the man felt that he- must of necessity take up a fighting attitude, the whole fabric of the Church was injured. The man's soul became embittered, and the congregation's as well. During the seven years his wife and himself had been amongst them their relation- ship with the congregation had grown more cordial year by year. There was not an office bearer or a Sunday school teacher but whom he parted with on the most cordial terms and relationship. From the day when he and his wife came among them they had kindness on all hands.</p>
        <p>He felt confident that the congregation would weld itself for one purpose—to secure for the Church a man of God's own choice. Their new minister would probably go al9ng different lines that he (Mr.- Reid) folloW6d. and very likely it would be good for the Church and their town. Their new minister would COMP with a newness of life, newness of thought, and newness of method, and it would be a benefit to the solid foundation which had been built at St. John's. If ever they desired him to come to St. John's for a Week-end he would do so most cordially. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MAYOR EXPRESSES TOWNI, REGRET.</head>
        <p>MAYOR EXPRESSES TOWNI, REGRET.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, speaking on behalf of the towns- people, said the departure of Mr. Reid from Warrington was not only a severe loss to his Church, but to the town. He (the Mayor) had a great personal regard for Mr. Reid, and on all hands he had heard regret expressed at his leaving. They needed a minister more than ever now, and they would need them more when their loved ones came back from the far. They would need them in the rejoicing to wel- come back the heroes, and so let their soldiers know that the town had been looked after in the way it should be. He hoped that in his new sphere of work his usefulness would be felt just the same as• it had been in Warrington. (Applause.) The remainder of the evening was spent in social. intercourse, and refreshments were par- taken of. A pleasing vocal programme was con- tributed to by Miss Emily Taylor, Mr. Pearce, Mr. J. E. Hannan, with Mrs. Hutt-m as accom- panist. ,Letters -regretting inability to attend the preEtentation were • read from the Rev. T. T. James and the Rev. J. Yonge, who, unhappily, is unwell.</p>
        <p>A woman seldom loses her head over a man who has lost his hair.</p>
        <p>• ••••.:..**.:..:. • Children' 8 Corner.; • • • v • • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. •</p>
        <p>• + • 8 4* •:* + 0 • •:* • + 46 4 , * • • * 444 0 0 6</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best!</p>
        <p>Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, "Examiner," Warrington.</p>
        <p>FLOWER DRAWING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>MT DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>As I expected, the flower-drawing compe- tition has, produced a good response and a num- ber of really artistic drawings. You will have noticed that in many cases of late our prize- winners have been new members. This week I am pleased to know that one of Uncle Ned's oldest nieces has won, and so maintained the reputation of those who formed the Corner in the,old days. There are several very clever boys and girls among those older members. The prize goes to</p>
        <p>Niece Jenny Dunlop (No. 380), 52, Chester- road, who attends Arpley-street School. Niece Jenny also tried to send me the 800th new member, but only succeeded in getting two of the necessary three to make the number. Nephew Leo. P. Fox sent in three names, and so had the honour of supplying the 800th.- Leo is an energetic member of the Corner. Niece Lucy Hankinson sent in two new names; while the following sent in one each: Nephew Harold Whittle, Niece Lilian Himsworth, Niece Mabel Langley.</p>
        <p>WALKING DAY!</p>
        <p>You will all have been very glad to hear that Walking Day is to be observed this year. ' It will be slightly different from past years. Bands are very scarce because of the war, and there are few men left to carry the great banners that usu,ally make the procession so magnificent. Carters will not be available to cart the very little children through the town to the fields or railway stations; and economy makes it neces- sary that there shall be no trips by train. Last year the treat was partly dropped because it was thought-we should not enjoy ourselves while so many of our friends and relations were suffering in the trenches; but our brave-hearted soldiers .wrote home saying it was against...their wishes that the children at home should be .deprived of their enjoyment. They said, in fact, that they would be happier if they knew that the children at home were happy, and so we are to have Walking Day with all its enjoyments and as little of its expenses as possible. It, would be a good guide to the Mayor, Town Council, and the school superintendents if they knew what the children thentselves would like, so I will give next week's prize to the boy or girl who sends me the best essay on How to Observe Walking Day This Year. I shall expect to have suggestions from most of my 800 nieces and nephews, considering the im- portance of the subject. Your loving UNCLE JACK,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>•:•.:•.:-:•.:••:.•:••:•.:••:.•:••:••:••:••:*.:••:••:•+•:•.:••:••:•.:••:••:••:•.:.•:••:••:".•:••:••:••:**:•.:••.1.•:.-,:•.:..:•4:..:••:••:••:. •:••:••:••:••:..:..X.•:••:••:••:••:":••:••:-..):•••:••:").:••:•.:•4:••:••:••:••:••:.•:••:••:••:,-:••:••:••:••:••:••:••:••:. •:•.:•'-- „_ ~.:. .:..:.- . ....•.::... WOMAN'S WORLD. .:..:. .:..:. .:„. ~.:. . • , .:. .:.</head>
        <p>•:•.:•.:-:•.:••:.•:••:•.:••:.•:••:••:••:••:*.:••:••:•+•:•.:••:••:•.:••:••:••:•.:.•:••:••:".•:••:••:••:**:•.:••.1.•:.-,:•.:..:•4:..:••:••:••:. •:••:••:••:••:..:..X.•:••:••:••:••:":••:••:-..):•••:••:").:••:•.:•4:••:••:••:••:••:.•:••:••:••:,-:••:••:••:••:••:••:••:••:. •:•.:•'-- „_ ~.:. .:..:.- . ....•.::... WOMAN'S WORLD. .:..:. .:..:. .:„. ~.:. . • , .:. .:.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Lady Hardinge In India. The work of Empire building has not been accomplished by men only; women have done their part in the development of the Oversea Dominions. Thus the name of the Marchioness of Dufferin and Ava will always be gratefully remembered in India, where she was untiring in her labours for the good of the native women and children. Lady Hardinge also did a great work in India, in ameliorating suffering, ,and the Maharani of Bhannagar has paid a remark- able tribute to her memory. It is the more in- teresting from the revelation it affords of the qualities that appeal to the native feminine mind, which evidently fully appreciates the aid and help given by the English wife to her hus- band in a time of stress and difficulty. One of the greatest of the good works due to the initiative of Lady Hardinge was the establish- ment of a medical college for women, staffed by women. The magnificent college buildings, now nearing completion, will be one of the glories of the new capital of India, and another monu- ment to her womanly concern for the welfare cf the children of India, is the infants' home at Bhopal, to which she gave her name. For the British Housewife.</p>
        <p>Thrift exhibitions are the order of the day, both in town and country, for the subject of economy has become -all-important, and is no longer a personal, but a national duty. No better way could be devised of bringing before housewives the economical use of meat, fuel, clothes; the possibilities of poultry-keeping in suburban gardens, &amp;c., than these exhibitions. Haybox and lamp cooking, home' lapndering and carpentering, the care of money, gardening for towndfolks, and how to raise vegetables and hardy fruits, the care of infant life, are dealt with by the National Training School of Cookery, at their exhibition in London. The newest labour saving appliances are on show, and form an interesting feature of all the exhi- bitions. A number of these are most ingenious, and deserve to be widely known, and used in</p>
        <p>servantless and other households. As the profits pf the greater number of these shows are devoted to one or other of the many war charities, they deserve to be supported by the general public, as well as by frugally minded housewives. 1 Home Economy.</p>
        <p>If people are not saving and economising it is not for want of being shown how, and yet another attempt in this direction will be niade at Princes' Skating Club, where the National Economy Exhibition and Conference will be held from June 26th to July Bth. The club is in normal times the resort of rank and fashion, but women whose dress allowance runs into three if not four figures a year, as well as others who do not spend as many shillings, will be shown how a dainty summer frock can be fashioned out of eight yards of material at sd. per yard, and how a charming hat can be made of materials ,that need cost no more than 2s. At the dress consultation- bureau, visitors will be shown how they may dress artistically on £5, £lO or £2O a year. Also how children's clothing may be made from discarded skirts and other garments, and how a use may be found for old stockings, gloves, boots, &amp;c. There should</p>
        <p>be no need for a dustbin except for ashes, and another of the axioms of this up-to-date economy is that every woman should be her own handy- man, and be Able to carpentery, plumbing, and glazing, as well as intensive gardening. These exhibitions astonish even the most careful by illustrating the many unexpected ways in which they may save and he self-reliant.</p>
        <p>Black, and Contrasting Colours. Black is to be very much worn this summer, perhaps because it is, or can be made, economi- cal, as well as smart. The woman with. an instinct for colour can, by means of ribbons or flowers, ring a dozen changes on one black gown. For instance, the lower half of a bodice, if formed of a broad floral patterned ribbon, gives relief to a gown of sable 'hue or the same result may be sought in a filmy veiling, on which the embossed flowers in vivid colourings, of ribbon, have been appliqued. For evening wear black may be saidato be thee leading shade, and when contrasted r with white, if carefully arranged, nothing iS of more distinguished appearance. The fashion for mixing several colours in a gown leads sometimes to disaster, more especially when the colours chosen are vivid. It is a mistake to mix pastel shades with others, yet this is frequently done by those devoid of the colour sense, but a clear light colour such as lemon yellow, lavender blue, or r+nrnly pink, makes an effective touch with black.</p>
        <p>Evening Blouses and the Apron. Afternoon and evening blouses made in filmy materials such as georgette, are elaborately trimmed with openwork stitching, and picot- edged frills, and for these a delicate lilac is a very proper shade. Stripes and squares in strong contrasting colours are also favoured. Dressy blouses of this description are given hem- stitched seams, and, now and again becoming high collars that button up closely sto the throat, the cuffs almost covering the hands. Basques, of course, are well to the fore, gener- ally short, but always full. Sleeves are so various in shape that to enumerate the half would require considerable space. They may be of the bishop variety, when they are drawn in with ribbons, and end in a frill, or they may reach-only to the elbow, where they are frilled with lace. Others are of accordion-pleated chiffon. Among revivals, the apron, that is to be worn over the fashionable tea frock,' deserves nbtice. In its newest guise, this old-fashioned accessory is made of white lace, but that it will meet with much favour is very doubtful. Fashions in Bodices and Sleeves.</p>
        <p>r The skimpiness of the bodice, as compared with the generous width of the skirt, is the pre- liminary merely to the tighter waist, ithat fashion has decreed shall be the coming mode. This necessitates a change in the setting of the sleeve, which is now put into the shoulder as in Early Victorian days, although the Raglan has by no means disappeared. A dart seam goes from the arm-hole to the waist, and this firm line causes the bodice to hug the waist, and is the latest and most approved style. This season a tendency is shown to mingle the fashions of distinct periods, in one toilette, the effect being often both quaint and picturesque. Watteau and Early Victorian fashions are not now con- sidered inconsistent with the charming Marie Antoinette fichu, and the ruffled sleeve of the time of that unfortunate Queen. By the aid of such a toilette, another scheme may have for its distinguishing feature severely simple draperies, arranged on classical lines, whilst yet another will have panniers, and a short train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PRIZE FOR FIRST LETTER.</head>
        <p>PRIZE FOR FIRST LETTER.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,— " Thank you very much for the lovely book which I received last Saturday. I never expected to win a prize with my first letter, and I will try my best to win another one.--Your loving niece, Lucy Hankinson, 393, Wellfield- street, No. 784." To win with the first 'letter is good; to decide to go on and do our best 'to succeed again, is better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
