      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ST. BARHAM' CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARHAM' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Sale Of Work in Aid Of The</p>
        <p>Parochial Funds.</p>
        <p>In order to swell various parish funds of the St. Barnabas' Church, a two days' sale of work was held on Wednesday and Thursday in the Patten Hall. There was a fairly large number of parish- ioners and friends present on the first day to witness Mrs. John White (late of Moore Hall) perform the opening ceremony. The Vicar (the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) said that, after paying all expenses, they had left from last year's sale a sum of M. He (the vicar) had decided to divide that sum equally between the Extension Fund and Augmentation and Beneficiaries' Fund. He had had an offer from the Liverpool Aug- mentation and Beneficiaries' Fund, that if he could raise £5O they would meet it with another £lOO. This would be handed over to the Ecclesiastical Fund, with the hope that it would be met with another £l5O. By means of the interest on this sum, the living of St. Barnabas' would be inci eased from £230 to £239. They hoped by this year's sale of work they would be able to help the Extension Fund. Their hearty thanks were due to the workers, who had laboured hard to get the work to- gether, and it was wonderful to think they had done so well, considering the high prices of materials, &amp;c. He (the vicar) was especially pleased with the refreshment stall and the Bible Class stall, which he 'did not think would have done so well, particularly the last named stall, for many members of the Bible Class had joined' the Colours.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ashley, Mrs. Sutton, Mrs. Kent, Mrs. Birchall, Mrs. Gleason, Misses Whitehouse, S. Whitehouse, E. Whitehouse and S. Booth. Flower Stall.—Mrs. Hands, Misses A. White- house, H. Hasall and N. Hands. Ice Cream Stail.—Misses B. and E. Broad- hurst.</p>
        <p>Dips.—Misses L. Boyle, L. Hankinson and J. Howard.</p>
        <p>SECOND DAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony on the second day was performed by Mrs. Fox.</p>
        <p>The Vicar announced that the proceeds of the previous day amounted to 59 ss. 6d., which in- cluded a donation of £lO from Lady Greenall. (Applause.) They were extremely thankful to receive anything from her this year, because they all knew what a good work she was doing for the soldiers, who needed the support better than they did. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hollingworth then announced that they were trying to help the soldiers and sailors by means of two draws, half the proceeds of which would go to them. Half the proceeds of the first draw, organised by the Bible Class Stall, would go to the blind soldiers and sailors, and half the proceeds of the other draw, organised by the men of the congregation, would go to the Whitecross Hospital. They hoped to raise about £lO altogether. In declaring the sale open, Mrs. Fox said it gave her great pleasure to attend and help Mr. Hollingworth and his willing people, for they were doing a splendid work. In proposing a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Fox, the Rev. H. V. Pigot said she had an ancestral connection with St. Barnabas' Church, a relation of hers, the late Mr. Owen, having planned the building of the church. The best thing they could do was to press the 'completion of the church—which would be a most handsome one when completed—as a means of thanking Mrs. Fox, and to the honourable memory of the late Mr. Owen.</p>
        <p>The proceeds of the second day amounted to £7l 15s. 3d., bringing the total to £l3l Os. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Church Must Be Carried On.</head>
        <p>Church Must Be Carried On.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Seeker said that at the present time they were all being called upon to economise in eyed way, and there were people who thought that sales of work should not be held. He did not hold with such views, however, for the work of the Church must be carried on, and if the national obligations were to be met at the expense of cutting down parochial charities, we were not making the sacrifices we ought to do. We must be ready for our boys when they came home. Not only must we find work to keep the parish going, but we must keep it going longer and brighter. It was our bounden duty to do so, for the boys were com- ing back with far stronger religious instincts than before. (Applause.) On the proposition of the Rev. B. Percival, seconded by Mr. Frank Webb, a vote of thanks was accorded Mrs. White. The Stan°!dors. The stallholders were : _ _</p>
        <p>Work Stall No. I.—Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Parkin- son, Mrs. Lewis and Mrs. Head. Work Stall No. 2.—Mrs. Bandalls, Mrs. Stringer, Mrs. Oxley and Mrs. Rotherham. Work Stall No. 3.—Mrs. Howard, Mrs. Iddon, Mrs. Sutton, Mrs. R. Smith. I. Bible Class Stall.—Mrs. Hollingworth, Mrs. T. Oxley, Mrs. Pepper, Misses E. Kimberley, E. Bowers, M. Singleton, and Dendy, with Mr. B. Dimelow as secretary.</p>
        <p>Men's Stall.—Mrs. Boyle, Mrs. Hind, Misses E. Daintith, F. Bennett, E. Bromley, and the church officers.</p>
        <p>Toy Stall.—Misses Fry, A. Fry, Glendenning and N. Allen.</p>
        <p>Refreshment Stall.—Mrs. Connell, Mrs. Jones,</p>
