      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</head>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Notices' of births, marriages, and deaths, in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at the following rates:—Not exceeding 20 words. 1/6 each; every additional seven words, 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Deaths.</head>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>COOK.—On the 22nd inst., suddenly, at Whitstable, Herbert Cook, aged 52. husband of Amy M. Cook, and eldest son of Richard Cook, of Warrington.</p>
        <p>LEIGH.—May 20th, at 168, Winwick-road, Alice Leigh, aged 56 years. MANNING.—May 19th. at 7. 'Kendrick-street, Thomas Manning, aged 45 years. PARR.—May 21st, at 6, Navigation-street, Jane Parr. aged 73 years. PARRY.—May 21st, at 10. Orchard-street, Charlotte Parry, aged 68 years. PLITMPTON.—On the 16th inst.. at The Cottage, Moore. Stephen. the dearly beloved husband of Jeanne Plumpton. in his 39th year. The only intimation.</p>
        <p>TOFT.—May 20th, at 39, Willis-street, Mary Ann Toft, aged 53 years. KILLED IN ACTION.</p>
        <p>GMSON.—On June 16th. 1915. Private William Gibson. Warrington Territorials, in his 21st year. eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. James Gibson. of 5, Mill-street. Warrington. One of Britain's heroes, His duty nobly done.</p>
      </div>
