      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL</p>
        <p>AND TOWN COUNCIL DEADLOCK.</p>
        <p>There is a deadlock between the Town Coun- cil and the Trades and Labour Council, the leading lights on the latter body having suc- ceeded, as was expected, in getting Messrs. DUKES and &amp;ADMIX restored among the nominations for seats on the Military Pen- sions Committee. There is no possibility of the Town Council re-considering, or, in any case, altering their decision, and the select few who spoke in the name of the Trade Unions of the town would have been well advised had they bowed to the inevitable. Instead of acting wisely in this way they began to hurl asnersions about, because they form a kind of fragrant red herring to draw across the trail. For instance, Mr. CHAS. DUKES said •the men who had opposed his election on the Pensions Committee were more desirous that he should be discredited in the Labour world than that the soldiers who were to 'return should receive the generous treatment which they boasted of. One oan easily see the object of such a state- ment, and it can as easily be disproved. Most of those who have objected to the actions of those who control the Trades Council have the interests of Trade Unionism at heart, quite as much as Mr. DUKES. The Town Council consists largely of Trade Unionists and friends of the movement. The Wire- drawers and others who have seceded from the Trades Council, and others who have re- fused to join the Council "as .at present constituted," are Trade Unionists.</p>
        <p>It seems to have -puzzled the champions of the Trades Council to find a reason for the objections of the Town Council to the nominees who were rejected. One of the principal reasons that caused the Council to refuse to accept the nominations was the reading of the following :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MILITARY IDEAL.</head>
        <p>THE MILITARY IDEAL.</p>
        <p>Young man,—The lowest aim in your life is to be a,good soldier. The good soldier never tries to distinguish right from wrong. He never thinks, he never reasons; he only obeys. If he is ordered to fire on his fellow-citizens, on his friends, on his neighbours, he obeys without hesitation. If he is ordered to fire down a crowded street when the poor are clamouring for bread, he obeys, and, seeing the grey hairs of age stained with red and the life tide gushing from the breast of woman, feels neither remorse nor sympathy. _ _ _</p>
        <p>If he is ordered off as one Of the firing squad to execute a hero or benefactor, he fires with- out hesitation, though he knows the bullet will pierce the noblest heart that ever beat in human breast.</p>
        <p>A good soldier is a blind, heartless, soulless, murderous machine. He is not a man, he is not even a brute, for brutes only kill in self- defence. All that is human in him, all that is divine in him, all that con- stitutes a man, was sworn away when he took the enlistment oath. His mind, his con- science, and his very soul are in the keeping of his officer. No man can fall lower than a soldier—it is the depth beneath which we cannot go. Young man, don't be a soldier or a Terri- torial. BE A MAN!</p>
        <p>Reproduced from the Daily Herald."</p>
        <p>That appeared in "The Labour Gazette," "the official organ of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, guaranteed circulation 3,000 monthly., Editor: Chas. Dukes, July, 1914," or just on the eve of the outbreak of the war. It was, we believe, also pointed but that since the war began the Trades Council Executive stood " neutral " when asked to assist in drawing up a scheme for recruiting; that(they refused to assist the thrift cam- paign, and that there was a great revolt against their action when they suddenly abandoned their neutrality and sought to oppose the Military Service Bill in the name of the Trade Unionists of the town. In the meantime, they had forced a contested elec- tion—and were somewhat soundly beaten— at a time when internal peace was asked for, nationally and locally. Finally, having failed to get into the Council, Mr. DIMES accepted the situation philosophically, if not calmly, and declared that he was content not to have anything to do with the 'representatives of our "rotten borough."- The Town Council, we presume, are but taking him at his word.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FEAR OF INVASION.</head>
        <p>THE FEAR OF INVASION.</p>
        <p>We should like to draw the attention of our readers to the letter which Mr. W. Peter Rylands has addressed to us on the question</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>BACK TO THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Dukes And Seabury Re-nominated for Pensions</p>
        <p>Committee.</p>
        <p>"WHAT HAVE I DONE? "-QUESTION FOR ALL.</p>
        <p>By a large majority the Trade Unionist representatives, at a meeting at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, protested against the re- fusal of the Town Council to accept their nominees, Messrs. Charles Dukes and A. E. Seabury as members of the War Pensions Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>These two gentlemen headed the list of the six nominated by a similar meeting of trade unionists a few weeks ago, but for a reason which the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) explained to them on Tuesday, they were referred back " with instructions to nominate others. Instead of doing that, however, they renominated ;the rejects, and sent the names back for the Council's acceptance.</p>
        <p>MAYOR LEAVES NOMINATIONS ALONE. On taking his seat</p>
        <p>The May-Or recalled that at the previous meet- ing he pointed out that it would be for the Council to accept or reject the nomination of trade union representatives. They had had a meeting lasting an hour and a quarter, and it had been decided not to appoint Messrs. Dukes and Seabury. In case of a deadlock between that meeting and the Council the matter would be submitted to the Statutory Committee in London, who would decide exactly what was to be done. He had received a letter from Mr. Edwin Plinston, president of the Trades and Labour Council, who said, "'I am extremely sorry and disappointed that the names selected and approved unanimously- by the last meeting (which was composed of repre4ntatives of ,non- affiliated as well as affiliated unions, to the Trades Council) should not be accepted by the Town Council. It appears futile to meet and nominate when those receiving the highest votes, are to. be ejected. I hope, even yet, tihe Council will agree to the last Belection." In.con- elusion, the Mayor suggested that the representit- tires should appoint their own chairman and go into the matter, afterwards' sending in 'their nominations to the Town Clerk. " Before you leave," said Mr. Harry Sinclair, as the Mayor was gathering up his papers, " I think we should know some of the reasons why they have' not been appointed. Also I regret to say that one of the approved nominees, Mr. Robert Boole, has enlisted and, therefore, some- one else will have to be appointed." The Mayor replied that the Council by a large ' majority believed that two of the gentlemen nominated were not the right people to be on the committee, being of opinion that those who sent their sons to fight would not like Mr. Dukes and Mr. Seabury to deal with their cases should they. require assistance on their, return.</p>
        <p>them. Within his own family there were men who had made sacrifices in the war, but he de- murred to preaching patriotism by proxy. What he meant by that was: if he had a brother who had fought during the war and a father who had worked on munitions at double the age of some who were boasting, that should not be placed to his (the speaker's) credit. The ques- tion for them all should be, "What have I done?" Mr. Baker: That's it !</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: Well, I will tell you. Years prior to the war I was interested in those political organisations which have specialised upon international difficulties, and I was part of the general Labour movement which agreed to a war programme._ __ _</p>
        <p>Mr: Baker: Mr. Chairman, I shall ask you to limit the speeches. Mr. Dukes: You may be afraid that I am going to upset your theories. , Mr. Baker: I am not afraid of anything you say.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: You are a representative of an industry which has sacrificed nothing. There is no dilution in the wire-drawing trade. Mr. A. Siddall (Wiredrawers' Society): I beg yours. There are fifteen per cent. of our mem- bers fighting with MM. Forces. Mr. Dukes: Thi• statement I am making is that those men have no more gone because they were wiredrawers than ours have gone because they were gas workers and general labourers. As wiredrawers you have not made any sacrifice at all. Your rates and conditions have 'been maintained. You have worked Upon war re- quirements equally with other industries in which trade union privileges have been sacri- ficed, and there are men on the Town Council who are representative of interests who express their patriotism in the lione of a return of five per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MR. ELWELL TAKES THE CHAIR.</head>
        <p>MR. ELWELL TAKES THE CHAIR.</p>
        <p>No Dividing Line 01 Labour.</p>
        <p>Bowing politely, the' Mayor then left the Chamber, and Mr. J. Elwell was immediately voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>Having expressed his regret thatit had been found necessary to hold that meeting, Mr. Elwell thought they ought to go into the whole problem and be properly satisfied before they separated. He thought, however, it would be better if they did not lay down anything about the Trades Council or non-Trades Council mem- bers. That barrier should not enter into the question at all. There shouT.: be no dividing line in the Labour ranks. Mr. H. Sinclair said they ought to raise a strong protest against the Town Council being the deciding factor. It as not to be expected that the men chosen to be the delegates of Labour should represent either capitalism or the Trades Council, 'and they ought not to he thrust lightly on one side. Messrs. Dukes and Seabury were well fitted to ,judge in such cases as would come before the committee, as 2,800 of the local members of their union were in the Forces, and the representatives had to deal with such cases day by day. It was a farce to call trade union representatives together to select men if they were to be thrown out be- cause two or three individuals did not care for them. That meeting ought to refuse to allow any other names to go forward. (Applause.) _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Bakei, of the Wirdrawers' Society, said that at their last meeting they did not an- ticipate any' trouble, but they could quite understand why the trouble had arisen. The Trades Council took a ;course of action which did not meet with the approval of a great many people in Warrington;tit did not meet with the approval of the Wiredrawers' Society. The Council refused to give any help in recruiting or to do anything to, further the objects of the Government in trying to win the war. The Town Council no doubt thought that, on that account, they should take no part in anything appertaining to pensions or to anything con- nected with the men who had gone to the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MR. SEABURY-AN OLD SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>MR. SEABURY-AN OLD SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Wozencroft : I don't think we should have this. Some representatives of the Trades Council have already been appointed on the committee.</p>
        <p>.Mr. Baker: I am trying to show why they have been refused., These men on our Town Council are not a lot of duffers and, therefore, we ought to try and find out their reasons. You have to consider the action of the Suggested representatives. Have they done anything to help the country'in this terrible crisis ? • _ . _</p>
        <p>Dir. C. Harrison: I don't think that should come in at all. One of these men has been a soldier.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baker: But they refuse to do anything at the present time. Mr. C. Dukes, who was sitting next to Mr. Seabury, interposed, remarking that he had no objection to his attitude on the war being dis- cussed, hut he should claim the right to state exactly what his attitude was. He could say many things about people who were doing their patriotism by proxy, but he did not want to enter into them unless his own attitude was to be discussed, Resuming, Mr. Baker said he was desirous that no men should be put on the committee who would cause friction and trouble, and he believed that men who had opposed anything in connection with the war would he likely to cause trouble. Members of the committee ought to be men who had stood up and helped their country.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: I think You ought to name them. Mr. Baker: I am not working my patriotism by proxy. I have a son fighting in France. Mr. Dukes: Don't proceed by innuendo. If your remarks apply to me, say so. Mr. Baker: You are one. I say the Council are the deciding people, and if you quibble about this for a month they will still decide. Why, then, waste all this trouble? We have other men who can take their place. I do not think the Council will refuse anybody who has done something to help the country. .Mr. T. Howard observed that what had been said as to the soldiers not liking certain men to deal with their cases was pure assumption. He thought that, having once chosen renre- sentatives, that meeting should stick to their choice and show the Town Council that they considered they were people of some respecta- bility. If the Council persisted in rejecting the names let them go to the Statutory Committee, where no personal spleen could be introduced and where qualifications would be the test.</p>
        <p>MR. DUKES AGAIN "GETS TO GRIPS." A moment or two of silence and then Mr. Dukes rose, remarking as is his wont in times of stress, "I think we ought to get to grips on this matter." Stating that the function of the War Pensions Committee was to help soldiers, he asked anyone whether any action he had I ever taken in the whole of his career would indicate that his vote NNimild be recorded in a way which would lessen the amount or degree of equitable treatment? He ventured to assert that the fear was rather on the other side. He wanted to remind friends that he was as much as ttohuecyhwweirteh; 2 th,Booe,memnzhboelhoandgignog to his•union gone out to fight had enlisted in Warrington, and 60,000 had gone from the union in various parts of the country; their attitude to him was, "Just as we claim the right to exercise our judgment with regard to the war, so we concede to Dukes or anyone else that right." . Some men were patriotic by proxY. It was pt no Credit to them that others Were serving for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WHAT HAS MR. DUKES DONE?</head>
        <p>WHAT HAS MR. DUKES DONE?</p>
        <p>Mr. Baker: I rise to a point of order. We have not tome here to hear long addresses. We never said the wiredrawers had sacrificed any- thing. We were talking about the principles of men who pretend to be leaders of Labour. Mr. Dukes: Yes, and I am giving you my answer. Just as you say I have made no sacri- fice, so I tell you that you have not. Mr. Baker: I have done something to help the country. You have done nothing. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes :You don't know wfrat I have done. The fact is you can't take your gruelling. Mr. Baker lighted a cigarette. The Chairman: Don't let us have cross-table talk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes, Continuing, said that as to his position in the recruiting campdign, he con- sidered the qupstion of enlistment was a matter of life and death. He had never assumed the position of telling a man what he ought to do in matters of that kind, and he disagreed with any man who claimed the right to dictate what another ought to do in matters of life and death. For that reason he refused to assist in recruiting. If he had been instrumental in per- suading any man to sacrifice his life he could not have looked his (the soldier's) parents in the face again. And yet that was what he was asked to do when he was asked to recruit. if he had persuaded a man to enlist and he had lost a leg he (Mr. Dukes) would have regarded that as a life's responsibility. What was really at stake in the present dispute—and it was no use burking it—was this: The men who had opposed his election on the committee ,were more desirous that he should be discredited in the Labour world than that the soldiers who were to return should receive the generous treatment which they boasted of.</p>
        <p>A BACK ANSWER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baker: That is nonsense.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: I say "Yes." And, clever though they may be, we know from A to Z what was said in that committee. I could tell you much more were it not for the fact that it would in- criminate certain individuals. In this case, he added, they were dealing with a Tensions Com- mittee, and it was not a question of Dukes or Baker, but whether, as a Labour body, they were going to sit down and allow the Town Council to dictate to them who the Labour representa- tives should be. (Applause.) Whether he got on the committee 9r not it would not prevent him doing what he considered to 'be right. The rostrum at Bridge Foot was a more powerful lever than rubbing shoulder to shoulder, with people who had never been Labour 'leaders. The reason for the opposi- tion was simply because Labour had made pro- gress and opponents were seeking to damage it by trying to discredit the leaders. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I think the whole of the time has been wasted so far. We have come here for a special purpose. We have heard several speeches, but have done nothing. We ought to begin to lay out some kind of action, either to repudiate the action of the Town Council or otherwise to elect two representatives. Mr. F. Berry then moved that they, as trade unionists, apart from the Trades Council or as individuals, resent the action of the Town Coun- cil, and Mr. Howard having seconded, Mr. Siddall proposed that the attitude of the Town Council be accepted, remarking that Mr. Dukes had not the confidence of the 1,150 trade unionists from Warrington who were fighting with MM.'s Forces. Mr. Dukes: . You have no idea what the opinion of ,the men in the trenches is. Mr. Siddall : Tithe members of the trade union societies in Warrington had a ballot -Mr. Dukes would not sit on the Pensions Committee. I Could stake. my life upon that. (" Question.") WAS THE MEETING REPRESENTATIVE? There were more altercations , as to whether those present represented the views of the trade unionists in the town, during which the Chair- man remarked that the 26 (those who voted ,for Mr. Dukes at the last meeting) were "not the trade unionists Of .Warrington. I know what branch meetings are." Mr. Berry: You ought to assure us that this meeting is representative of the trade unions of the town. If so it would clear the whole question. Our prestige as trade unionists ought to be interwoven that we may join hands in a cause.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I cannot do what I am asked to do, because I know for a fact that in the time given it was impossible in most cases to have branch meetings Or to consult the members. When you get notice from town officials asking you to send representatives to a meeting it is impossible to call a special meeting unless time is given you, and the result is that we are meet- ing here to-night not altogether as the repre- sentatives of the trade unionists of Warrington. Mr. Berry: Then I beg to move that the meeting be disbanded. Mr. J. Hamblett seconded, hut merabers dis- suaded them from pursuing it, and eventually the resolution ' protesting against the Town Council's attitude, was carried by 27 votes to four.</p>
        <p>As to whether only Messrs. Dukes' and Sea- bury should be nominated or whether more should be addetl to them there was also some sparkling discussion, one remarkinz that what was the use of renominating Mr. Dukes if he had to go into the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NOT "IN THE CART."</head>
        <p>NOT "IN THE CART."</p>
        <p>Whereupon Mr. Dukes intervened, remarking, " I don't want anyone to run away with the idea that either Dukes or Seabury is going. A special cqmmittee has been appointed to submit the names of those who are indispensable to ,their unions. Don't run away with the idea that we are in the cart. We are a long way off it—although there are some people_ itio would like us to be in it. That Messrs. Dukes and Seabury should be renominated was then decided by 24 votes to eight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ANOTHER COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>- - Another meeting of the Town council will be held to complete the Pensions Committee. The Unions have been invited to send further nominees, and this is being done. • Last night the National Union Life Agents (Warrington Branch) decided to nominate Mr. Edward Plinston, president of the Royal Liver Society: and Mr. Ralph Bird, of the Victoria Legal Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. M. Hughes, Prime Minister of Australia, came to Warrington yesterday morn- ing unhonoured and unsung. Along with his party he was travelling on the 9.55 through the Central Station from Liverpool to Manchester, where he has been acclaimed of the people.</p>
        <p>Few knew of his coming to Warrington—a friend,. .of mine, myself, ancl, of course, Alderman Bennett. We had. no difficulty in espying the beautiful saloon in which the party were housed. At one end Mr. Hughes' secretaries were relishing pipes and popular magazines, Mr.i. Hughes sat mid- way reading a newspaper, the nurse had her hands full with the baby, who was trying to rub its dainty nose against the window. Apparently, the youngster was the only one in- terested in Warrington.</p>
        <p>But where was Mr. Hughes? The Alderman was getting anxious. So we moved along, and by putting our faces full against, a window saw him lying on his back in the compartment of the saloon, thinking out his MaAchester speech.</p>
        <p>He had his jacket off, used his coat for a quilt, And one could see his blue and white shirt sleeves as he raised his hand to keep his cigarette alight. He looked rather tired. His tall hat and umbrella were taking a rest on the rack.</p>
        <p>With fon/. blinds .down all was peaceful twilight within, and the P.M. was net easily discerned-. from without: . We waved our hands to him, and ha waved his cigarette and smiled pleasantly in return. Then he looked up at the ceiling to resume his meditation; and we retired.</p>
        <p>We were all Mr. Hughes saw of Warrington. His baby at the window was the only sightseer among the party, and perhaps when it grows to talking age it will tell its illustrious father what its impressions were.</p>
        <p>In getting Alf. Leonard and Elven tic:ll;es, of the "Step This Way " revue, to appear at the Blinded Heroes' matinee at the Palace and Hippodrome on Thursday, Mr. Jackson has garnered some particularly good talent into his net—or, rather, on to his stage. These notable comedians will be performing in Manchester during the week, and, if at all possible, will make the journey, returning as soon as they have finished their "turns" and the applause has died down.</p>
        <p>When in Warrington a few weeks ago they told me they make it a point of duty to con- tribute to matinees in • aid of -war and allied funds whenever they are asked.</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago the town was honoured by a visit from the 'North-Western Federation of the Ancient Order of Foresters. .</p>
        <p>To-day we shall be further honoured by the holding here, for the first time, of the Indepen- dent Order of Oddfellows (M.U.) Lancashire and adjacent districts' conference. The Mayor will welcome the delegates at the Town Hall, at 2.45, and at 3 they -will proceed with the con- sideration of the programme of the A.M.C. at Bristol in Whit-week.</p>
        <p>On niembers' of Mr. Arthur Down's Band presenting themselves for the weekly practice on Wednesday evening at his lthme in Stockton Heath they-. saw displayed under the clock a notice: "This clock is at the old Greenwich time," and Mr. Down intimated that he is not going to alter it. Neither does he intend ad- vancing the fingers of his watch.</p>
        <p>It is as well to follow "common time" when the music of the land is set to it.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Parish Church Magazine announces that the purse of gold presented to the Rev. T. Vickers contained £so—" a noble tift by worthy givers to a worthy recipient," it adds.</p>
        <p>At tho Warrington Tribunal this week most of those who were conditionally exempted signi- fied their willingness to join the Volunteer Training Corps, and Mr. Bradbury, a member, remarked: "Soon we shall have the Park full." That. battalion we have been waiting for should soon he formed, if they all keep their promise. I think they will. Englishmen have a keen sense of honour.</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NO SMALLPDX.</head>
        <p>NO SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>The Mayor Says No Truth In Startling Rumours.</p>
        <p>UNNECESSARY ALARM.</p>
        <p>We are requested by His Worship the Mayor to state that after very careful inquiries into the alleged outbreak of smallpox in this town he is assured by the Medical Office, of Health that no such case exists and that the town is entirely free from that fell disease. His Worship is most anxious to appease the public mind, which he regrets has been un- necessarily alarmed. _</p>
        <p>We have ascertained that the condition of quarantine among the Military Forces is not limited to Warrington, but applies to the whole of South-West Lancashire, and will remain in force for military reasons throughout that dis- trict until the 31st of this month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OSWALD GARSIDE, R.I.</head>
        <p>OSWALD GARSIDE, R.I.</p>
        <p>Warrington Student Who Has Had Remarkably Successful Career.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians will learn with pride that Mr. Oswald Garside has been elected an "R.l." that. is a Member of the Royal Institute of Painters in Water Colours, an election that is subject to His Majesty's approval. It is the highest distinction that c,an be bestowed upon an artist in this particular branch of. work. The holders of the title are limited in number to 100.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garside is the son of the late Mr. Thomas Garside, chemist, of Southport, and Mrs. Gar- side, of Stockton Heath, and a brother of Mr. F. Garside, of the firm of Garside and Jolley, printers, Warrington. He came to Warrington when about twelve years of age, and was subse- quently apprenticed to cabinet-making with Messrs. Garnett, of Penketh. When out of his time he went to the Warrington School of Art, studied with a keenness that foretold success, and gained a Lancashire County Connell Scholarship, which took him for three years to Paris, where he studied at the well-known Julien's Academy, under Jean Paul Laurens and other famous French masters. His progress was so marked that he was given a twelve months' extension of the scholarship, and the additional time was spent in Venice. Coming home, he spent a couple of years experimenting before he entered upon any serious work. Then he rapidly established his worth, became a regular exhibi- tor at the Royal Academy, the Royal Institute of Painters in Water Colours, the Paris Salon, and the various exhibitions, including the one held a few years ago at Liverpool. Now, at the age of 46, he finds himself classed with the select few at the very top of his profession. He re- sides at Barnes, London.</p>
        <p>There are two or three pictures by Mr. Garside in the Warrington Museum, and naturally there are a good many privately cwned by. local ad- mirers of his work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Needed In Case Of Possible German Invasion.</p>
        <p>A REAL DANGER.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—On Saturday last Sir James de Hoghton, the County Commandant, visited Warrington in order to inspect the Warrington and Widnes Companies of the South-West Lancasaire Volunteers.</p>
        <p>He expressed himself well pleased, with the bearing and smartness of. the men but was disappointed with their small number...</p>
        <p>The War Office have issued orders tl at the Volunteer Corps must be organised for the defence of the main lines of railway:. in the event of invasion, when enemy agents would undoubtedly make every endeavour to interfere with the movement of troops. All in authority are of opinion that the Germans will make a serious endeavour to invade Great Britain, and the less encouraging their progress on the Con- tinent the more likely they will be to make the attempt:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Would Be Lamentable.</head>
        <p>Would Be Lamentable.</p>
        <p>The present number of the Warrington Corps available is insufficient for the purpose, and it would he lamentable if we have to advise the War Office that Warrington men are unable to face the small sacrifice involved in offering this amount of service to their country, and I con- fidently appeal to the men of Warrington to come forward and join. Sir James was saying on Saturday that Great Britain did not know what war is. They have vever experienced the horrors of war them- selves, and I am sure he is right, and that the men of this country do not fully realise the terrors against which they are being protected by our Army and Navy, and what would be their fate and the fate of their womenfolk should that defence fail.</p>
        <p>Numbers must stop, at home owing to age, or for the purpose of necessary. duties, but it is difficult to justify an arbitrary, arrangement allotting hardship, discomfort and peril to some, and ease, comfort and safety to others. Surely the men who are privileged to stay at home should at least show their readiness to help, should serious emergency to the State require it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Disastrous Cocksuredness.</head>
        <p>Disastrous Cocksuredness.</p>
        <p>It would be a great encouragement to the men at the Front if they felt that the men at the back had a better appreciation of the realities, and were preparing themselves to play their part should necessity arise. Our cocksuredness has frequently landed us in trouble in the past and one, day will cer- tainly bring us to final disaster, just as the same cocksuredness has caused the downfall of all the Empires in the past. A strong Warrington Battalion of Volunteers would show that Warrington, at all events, was determined not to slack, and was willing to provide the force required to fulfil the responsible duties now demanded by the War Office.</p>
        <p>The Government have found a difficulty in equipping the Volunteer Force owing to the pressure for equipment for our huge army, and this may have been some source of discourage- ment to the movement. I am satisfied a suffi- cieney of rifles could be obtained by purchase, and if the men are forthcoming I am confident I would be able to appeal with success for funds to defray the cost of any equipment in reason required to make the Corps a credit to the town.</p>
        <p>Recruits can be enrolled most conveniently at the Territorial Quarters on Mondays and Thursdays, at 8 o'clock, or by sending their names to Mr. P. H. Swallow, 9, Egypt-street, Warrington.—Yours faithfully, W. PETER RYLANDS. Area Commandant. Massey Hall, Thelwall, Cheshire. 25th May, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SOLDIER-VOCALISTS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIER-VOCALISTS.</p>
        <p>The[wall Heys Hospital Benefits By Concert.</p>
        <p>A spirit of happiness permeated the atmosphere of the Latchford Parish Room on Thursday night, when a concert arranged by the wounded soldiers of Thelwall Heys Hospital attracted a crowded audience.</p>
        <p>Appreciative of the care and kindness accorded to them, the men who are at present receiving treatment at the hospital, arranged the ooncert, and it was gratifying to them to have their efforts so handsomely rewarded. It is expected that between £24 and £25 will be available for the support of the institution. There are many vocal and musical soldiers stationed at the hospital, and they gave the public an idea of their abilities in this direc- tion, the programme being almost wholly contributed by them. Patriotic, sentimental and comic songs were given, to the evident de- light of the assembly, who were particularly enthusiastic over the rendering of "When the Boys Come Home," by Miss Norton, a lady gifted with a charming voice, a number of soldiers joining in with gusto, and at the same time the Union Jack was unfurled. Miss 8100 l also gave pleasure with vocal items, and a sketch, " Mutanical Jane," played by Private Molineux, Private Batty and Miss Blood kept everyone in a merry mood. Miss Mabel Fair.- dough acted ably as accompanist, and the duties of stage manager were capably fulfilled by Lance-Corporal Ingham. Mr. and Mrs. Prost, Grappenhall Hall, took a kindly interest in the concert, and tende:ed thanks to Dressrs P. Walker ,nd Son ltd for decorating the room, and to Mr. Crompton, the staff, and soldiers for helping to make the function a success. Mr. Frost announced that arrangmeents were being made for the holding of a Whist drive in the grounds of the hospital on Whit Monday. A bouquet was presented to Miss Norton, and chocolates and cigarettes were handed round to the soldiers. Refreshments were also served by the hospital staff.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Mrs. Jefferson, of Gatesgarth, Latchford, held a sale of work on behalf of the hospital, and she has kindly handed over the profits, £6 13s. 6d., to Mrs. Frost.</p>
        <p>The usual Pound Day on Thursday was very successful, gifts in money and kind amounting to over £ll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>'A MONSTRE PROGRAMME.'</head>
        <p>'A MONSTRE PROGRAMME.'</p>
        <p>Big Demand for Tickets FOr</p>
        <p>Blinded Warriors' , Matinee.</p>
        <p>Big things are going to happen in Warrington on, Thursday afternoon, when the matinee in aid of the Blinded Soldiers' and Sailors' Hostel will take place at the Palace and Hippodrome. The tickets have gone exceedingly well, and 'the porter has been looking up his notice board, "Standing room only." To those who want to get in had better apply for their tickets at once. Mr. Jackson, the manager, has been active in getting together a galaxy of professional music- hall talent, palatably mixed with the band of the South Lancashire Regiment, Miss Marie Houghton, and Mr. J. Babbitt. In all, there will be ltwenty items, and it is almost certain that two of the best numbers will• be provided by Alf. Leonard and Elven Hedges, of "Step This Way " fame. A delightful programme in a good cause! We wish success for the matinee. Along with Mr. Jackson, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mr. J. H. J. Hampson have been particularly hard workers in its organisation. Its patrons are the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Sir A. H. Crosfield, Bart., Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and Colonel Moggridge—all united in charity of the first order. A more pitiable plight than being blinded by shell-fire is 'unconceivable, and we are proud the people of Warrington intend sending a handsome sum to their aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Borough Roll of Honour.</head>
        <p>Borough Roll of Honour.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman 'P. Peacock) invites relatiyes and friends of Warrington soldiers and sailors, who have 'lost their lives in the war, to send him as soon as Possible the full names and addresses, and names of the regi- ment or ship in which the; soldiers and sailors served, so that a complete and accurate roll of honour to their memory may- be prePared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>faralYntellig.enre</head>
        <p>faralYntellig.enre</p>
        <p>Time Table for June. The Warrington A.B.C. Railway Guide for June will be on sale next Thursday morning.</p>
        <p>Colonel Crosfield at Sir Arthur's:Home. Telegraphing from his home at Highgate last night, Sir Arthur H. Crosfield, Bart., stated,. "Colonel Crosfield safely installed here. In good health and spirits. Is making capital pro- gress."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>There were 19 more births than deaths in, Warrington in the week ended May 20th, the births numbering 40 and the deaths 21. Tho new babies were equally divided between the sexes-20 of each.</p>
        <p>One of the deaths was due to diphtheria.</p>
        <p>Infectious Disease:</p>
        <p>Last week the following new cases of in- fectio-us disease were notified —Scarlet fever, 6; diphtheria, 1; tuberculous phthisis, 8; measles, 7. In the hospital are the following cases :-- Scarlet fever. 36; enteric fever, 2; diphtheria, 6; phthisis, 21.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>League of the Cross Band.</head>
        <p>League of the Cross Band.</p>
        <p>The League of the Cross Band (conductor, Mr. T. Hynes) will play the following programme in the Park ,on Sunday :—Hymn,. "0 Sacred Heart"; march, " Holyrood " (Kenneth Alford); overture, "La Vivandiere" (E. Le Duc); selec- tion, " Zar Una Zimmermann" (G. A. Lortzing); selection, "Le Due' Illustri Rivali" (Merca- dante); selection, "Sweet English Songs" (W. Seddon); "God save the Ring."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Railway Clerks in Conference.</head>
        <p>Railway Clerks in Conference.</p>
        <p>The Railway Clerks' 'Association was in con- ference at Cardiff last week-end and the War- rington branch, with a membership of 215, was represented by a delegate. The strength of the Warrington branch has greatly increased during the last year or two. Six years ago there were only fifteen members. At the present time they have lady members exceeding that figure, and the committee reports "And we have still more to come."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Rev. J. Martin to Say Farewell to St. Peter's.</head>
        <p>Rev. J. Martin to Say Farewell to St. Peter's.</p>
        <p>After two years' faithful work as curate if SL Peter's Church, the Rev. John Martin has accepted the curacy of Emmanuel Church, Fazakerley, and will enter upon his new work about the middle of August. During his sojourn at St. Peter's, the reverend gentleman has become exceedingly popular, and has done much good work among the parishioners, by whom he will be .sadly missed.</p>
        <p>Elmwood Avenue Congregational Sunday School.</p>
        <p>The- Sunday school anniversary services in connection with Elmwood-avenue Congregational Church were held on Sunday, and were well- attended. The Rev. T. T. James was tl.f: preacher at the morning and evening services. In the afternoon a service of song entitled "The Building of the Temple" was rendered with splendid understanding and vocal power by the choir and scholars. Mr. J. W. Slack presided. The collections for the day amounted to over .215.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Presentation to Mrs. Carson.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Mrs. Carson.</p>
        <p>A deputation representing the —s-taff--;;:nd old employes of the Whitecross Company on Satur- day waited upon Mrs. W. Carson, at Oawestry, and presented her with a solid silver tray as a token of esteem entertained for her late hus- band, for many years the managing director of the company. The tray, which is of the Adams' design, contains a' lacsimile of the signatures of the 49 subscribers, and was also suitably inscribed. Mr. R. Garnett made the pre- sentation, and Mrs. Carsen responded on behalf _ _</p>
        <p>of herself and family. The tray was supplied by Messrs. Eustanee and Co., jewellers, of Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>Leather Trade Activity. Unprecedented actWity prevails here, says "The Leather World," and in spite of the Con- tinued enlargement of tanneries which has been so noticeable for the past few years, it is ex- tremely difficult to cope with the amount of business passing. Tanners are workEing whole- heartedly with the authorities in producing the kind of leather mOst required, and willingly vary their production at every call from head- quarters. Thus the old conservatism has passed and the "quick-change artist taken its place. There is the huge order for high-legged Cossack boots following on the present Russian order, which must not be overlooked in esti- mating future values and supplies.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. J. Partington. The remains of the late Mr. Joseph Parting- ton, of Bold-street, Warrington, were interred on Tuesday in the Great Budworth Churchyard. The deceased, who died at his residence on. Satufday, was a member of an old Cheshire family, which can be traced as far back as 1200. Mr. Partington, who was 65 years of age, had been in ill-health for some months. Ile had been in business in Warrington and sur- rounding district for many years. He was a Conservative and a prominent Freemason, being a Past Master of the "De Tabley" Lodge, Knutsford; also the "Lodge of Lights," War- rington. He leaves a widow, two sons and three daughters.</p>
        <p>- Next Week's Great Garden Party. Over 2,000 wives and widows of soldiers and sailors have accepted the invitation of the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock) to the garden party in Bank Park to-day (Saturday) week. Given fine weather, the event should, be an immense success. The host and hostess are in a. generous mood and their guests may rely upon having a splendid time. Refreshments are to be served, and the Band of the South Lancashire Regiment is 'to play selections, in addition to which there will also be the attraction of Mr. W. W. Whittle's Troupe, known as "The Scarlet Poms." From 2.30 until 6.30 the wives and widows, with their host and hostess, will have everything to them- selves and then the general public will be ad- mitted to share in their social pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Baptist Sunday School Anniversary</head>
        <p>Baptist Sunday School Anniversary</p>
        <p>Services.</p>
        <p>In connection with Golborne-street Baptist Sunday School the anniversary services were held on Sunday, when the Rev. R. D. Darby, of Leigh., preached both morning and evening to good congregations, and said that the only effec- tual teacher is the one who has learned in his own life the truth he is trying to apply, and, like his Master, practises it in the• sphere in which he moves, This is a lesson hard to learn, but it is a trith that all history confirms. It had its full demonstration in Jesus Himself, Who, in order to save, gave His life, and those who would be His followers must in their own way give if they would gain. In the afternoon a musical service was held, presided over bY Mr. Thos. Dale. The Rev. P. meson spoke on the decline in the attendance et Sunday schools, and the increase (pf juvenile crime,nd suggested that the former might /VI some deasure be responsible for the latter. The choir and scholars rendered special music at each service, under the leadership of Mr. II- Barnes, with Mr. S. Rickers as organist. The collections for the day amounted to over .tl5, which was an increase on the previous year.</p>
        <p>Wood's College Successes. Pitman's Shorthand (National Shorthand Association): Ninety words per minute, Hughes, E. E. Johnson; eighty words per minute, G. Challoner (distinction), E. Smith, R. SUB- kin, E. Owen, B. K. Harrison; seventy words per minute, I. F. Owen; sixty words P....5r minute, A. E. Hardcastle, K. Eddowes, C. XL• Kisby (distinction), L. I. Hamilton Smith, de M. Gorton, E. Charnock (distinction), Broady, J. Dodd; . fifty words per minute, M. Boumphrey, A. M. Boumphrey. Inter" mediate theory, M. Smethurst, W. Arrowsruitli• Elementary theory, N. Griffiths, M. Forster, .11-• Merrington (distinction), H. Booth, I. M. Lyons' (distinction), I. Cooper, E. Withenshavr (dig' tinction), A. Roughsedge, M. M. Mayhall, Dykes.</p>
        <p>Gregg Shorthand: Ninety words per minute, K. Murtagh, Ivy Shaw, Ethel Oakley; eight/ words per minute, Mabel Bagshaw, Lily Bagel' Dorothy Burthera, Alice Higham, May Tanner; sixty words per minute, M. S. Burke; fifty wolf; per minute, Elsa Young; forty words Per. minute, Ada Lloyd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EMPIRE DAY.</head>
        <p>EMPIRE DAY.</p>
        <p>The Sane Imperialism Of The</p>
        <p>Schools.</p>
        <p>YOUNG WARRINGTON TRUE</p>
        <p>Per the first time in its short history Empire bey was on Wednesday officially recognised by the Government. The fact gave an added enthusiasm to the celebrations held all over the country. In London flags waved in the gentle breeze, not only in almost every street, but from the Houses of Parliament. There was no ?stentatious display of Union Jack rosettes, but le a hundred different ways the solidarity of the Empire was emphasised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>GRAIIIIIA.R. SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>GRAIIIIIA.R. SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Visit Of Officer From The Headquarters</p>
        <p>Staff.</p>
        <p>Major G. C. II: Wheler, I.Q.M.S. of the Head- quarters Staff of the Western Command and M.P. for the Faversham Division of Kent, _visited the Warrington Grammar • School on 'Wednesday, inspected the remarkably fine Cadet Corps possessed by the school, and then ad- d-re, ssed the boys on the Empire and its privi- legeS and responsibilities. The Corps, about 70 strong, was under the command of -Captain • W. Pearson, with Lieutenant J. Pape and • Company-Sergeant-Major L. J. Witts. On the Parade ground, and in the school afterwards, were Alderman Smethurst, one of the governors, and Mr. Vere Davies, clerk to the governors. _The Rev. Horace Gray, in calling upon Major Wheler to address the boys, referred to men horn this district who, like Colonel Greene, had Jnade the great sacrifice for the Empire. Major Wheler congratulated the boys upon their steadiness and soldierlike bearing, and expres3ed the confident belief that they would acquit themselves creditably to themselves, their school and their country when the time came for titeDA to join His Majesty's Forces. He then Passed in review the part played by the Dominions in the war, from the way in which the Canadians saved the Empire ',)y with- standing the first German surprise gas-attack, to the way in which South Africa is clearing the great sub-continent of German influence. The -Empire, he said, was on its trial and we de- pended upon the steadiness, determination and the stubbornness of the British race to see us through. Whenever the boys he was speaking to were called upon to take a serious part in the work of the Empire they must' be true to the clear duty which the Empire imposed upon' them. (Applause.) Alderman Smethurst, in proposing a vote of . thanks to Major Wheler, mentioned that the school had provided four men who had taken a prominent part in the war. The Vote was carried with ringing cheers. SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Senior Girls Raise £5 For Disabled</head>
        <p>Senior Girls Raise £5 For Disabled</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Home.</p>
        <p>The Secondary School held their annual Enlpire Day celebrations. For the morning a Programme of patriotic songs and recitations had been prepared, and the school was again favoured with a visit from Alderman Bennett, who gave the pupils an inspiring address on the liritish people as Empire builders, in contrast tO the Germans. For the afternoon the senior girls of the echool had organised a fete at the athletic 'field at Fearnhead. The entertainment com- prised sports, folk songs, old English dances and !lag songs. The proceeds, amounting to £,5, will oe devoted to the fund of the Star and Garter :Rome for totally disabled soldiers and sailors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Kr. JAMES' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>Kr. JAMES' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Reason Why We Are Filled With</p>
        <p>Pride.</p>
        <p>The niorning session at St. . James' Mixed School was chiefly devoted to lessons which illustrated the history, extent, population, in- dustries and resources of the Empire. In the afternoon the Headmaster (Mr. Stans- field) addressed the children, who, at intervals, sang • suitable. Patriotic songs. He showed that Empire Day meant more to Us this year than ever, because the war had revealed the unity and loyalty of our vast Empire. It was not the thought that our Empire occupied one-fifth of the surface of the globe and contained one-fourth of the population Which filled us with pride, but the fact that thousands of our fellow-subjects from across th 3 Seas had willingly come forward to help us and save us from disaster. This loyalty and Patriotism was the result of justice, which is the chief feature of British government. Mr. Stansfield referred to the fact that this Year was the tercentenary of the death of Shakespeare, perhaps the greatest Englishman .who ever lived, and he quoted many extracts from his works, showing the great poet's idea Of true patriotism.</p>
        <p>" He which hath no stomach to this fight, Let him depart," , indicates what he would expect from the -conscience of a true Englishman, and other Passages from "Henry V." seemed particularly appropriate to the present struggle. The King's prayer before battle, and his wish to give thEroredit of victory to God are exampl:s Ive should follow:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BOLTON COUNCIL SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>BOLTON COUNCIL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Ilqw The War Has Strengthened Bonds PAGEANTS AT FAIRFIELD GIRLS' Of Love And Friendship. SCHOOL. At nine o'clock the children of the three de- Partments assembled in the playground, in the Presence of many parents and friends, and after saluting the flag. sang the National Anthem. Throughodt the day special references were tnede in the various lessons to the growth and 'development of the Empire, and to the strength- ening of the bends of love and friendship by the great war in which we are engaged. In the Ifigher classes special lessons were given on the filter and deeper aspects of Empire. In the afternoon a number of woancled soldiers from the Raddon Court Red Cross Yespital were pleasantly entertain-ad by the neys, with patriotic songs and recitations. At the close of the proceedings Miss Beoadbent, nn behalf of the soldiers, thanked the Head- Insister (Mr. E. H. Turton) and the boys ler their kindness. Amongst others present Was Alderman Pierpoint, chairman of the managers. A collection oF behalf of the Overseas Club for comforts, &amp;c., for British prisoners of war in Germany realised over £2.</p>
        <p>Empire. Day was celebrated at Fairfield Girls' School by a succession of pageants. Britannia was surrounded by representatives from Eng- land, Scotland and Ireland, who each extolled the merits of their respective lands in solos; whereupon "gallant little Wales" rushed hi highly "indignant at being left out in the cold," and voicing a claim' to equal rights with the other countries of the United Kingdom. At- tention was next directed to the tercentenary of Shakespeare. This was celebrated by Shakes- pearian songs and recitations, interspersed with Elizabethan dances. The Pageant of Empire followed. Girls in characteristic costume repre- sented each of our Colonies, and the close bond betwen Mather and daughter countries was emphasised. The last section of the pageants echoed the "Story of the War " given by the girls in Parr Hall last February. The *hole concluded with a soul-stirring poem, Flag," during which all saluted the flag.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HAMILTON STREET SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>HAMILTON STREET SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Children's Sacrifices To Benefit War</p>
        <p>Funds.</p>
        <p>During the morning at Hamilton-street School, special lessons on "Our Empire" were Fiven by the teachers to the children of the ncYs' girls' and infants' schools. In the after- -noon, when several of the Training College .I;tudents were present, all the scholars of the `hree departments assembled in the school yard, where a varied programme was carried Out. A pleasing item was the collection of "ppers amounting to twenty-three shillings, which the children willingly gave as their !ocrifice of a week's luxuries or amusement for the benefit of the war funds. In a short address by the Headmaster (Mr. 4.Jongshaw), reference was made to the numerous Subscriptions of the children for the wounded snidiers, the sending of parcels to prisoners of the services of the lady teachers as nurses, ~.."343 absent teacher-soldiers. and the twelve Itamilton-street heroes who have been killed in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EVELYN STREET SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>EVELYN STREET SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>pritain Stands For Liberty Throughout</p>
        <p>The World. , The whole of the 400 boys attending Evelyn- street Council School marched from the play- ground at nine a.m. into the open road, and Past the flag in quadruple file, saluting within h4l usual paces of the flag-staff. °n assembling in the Central Hall (im- -I;lediately after prayers) the Headmaster (Mr. Appleton) addressed them for twenty minutes °ll the significance of their action. He dwelt the extent Of the Empire, its riches, and the 'reedern of its people=-the last being the great cause of the wonderful reoponno made to—the call for men by our Colonise. This liberty in</p>
        <p>*ritain has been secured by the struggles and ; sacrifices of past generations—historical ex- amples were found in the case of worship, by ; Latimer and Ridley; the Press and publication 'by Prynne and Cobbett ; the freedom of thought, by Sir Thos. More. We prized +his so highly as to be prepared to stand by the smaller nations in danger of losing their liberty by the attack or oppression of the greater—as in the case of Belgium and Serbia. Yet we endeavoured to avoid misunderstandings withotther nations, and indeed seek to make international amity—a notable feature in King Edward VII. and King George. "To all the world I give my hand: My heart I give my native land."</p>
        <p>By quotations from Tennyson and Shakes- peare the Headmaster showed how the call comes to us—including the boys—to serve our country with zeal and courage. ENTHUSIASTIC SCENE AT THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CAMP TRAGEDY.—Continued.</head>
        <p>CAMP TRAGEDY.—Continued.</p>
        <p>since I have been in the company. I have made a clean job of it. That is only one of my victims."</p>
        <p>COULD NOT HAVE SHOT HIMSELF.</p>
        <p>Dr. R. T. McGeagh, who was called to the sergeants' sleeping quarters, stated: I found the dead body on the floor. I found a circular wound in the chest, with blood flowing from the, mouth and nostrils. I ,could not find the exit of the bullet until after the removal ef the body to the mortuary, when, along with Dr. Symes, I found the exit at the back, about two inches from the spine. There was no mark of burning. which would have been there if the man had shot himself. Moreover, he could not have shot himself from the position of the wound., Death would, take place in less than a minute.</p>
        <p>The Coroner asked Mr. Kelly if he wished to call his client at this stage. Mr. Kelly; No, sir. THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>The jury retired, and returned after six minutes' absence, when Mr. J. H. Kelly, fore- man of the jury, announced the verdict as follows: .</p>
        <p>"The jury is unanimous in finding a verdict of wilful murder against John Williams." The accused who sat unmoved throughout the whole of the proceedings, heard the verdict an- nounced in the same stoical manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</head>
        <p>COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>At Peel Police Court on Monday, Sergt. John. Williams was charged on remand with murder- ing Colour-Sergeant Wm. Henry Malings. At the request of Inspector Shimmin, a further remand was granted till yesterday, pending the result of the Coroner's inquest. • Yesterday the evidence given at the inoucst 'was repeated, and prisoner was committed for triaL He reeetied his defence.</p>
        <p>7.41111417: ';""1"'-'4''''''7Ce41.,NOWIrl"f"° •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WYCLIFFE SCHOOLS. •</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE SCHOOLS. •</p>
        <p>Empire Day to the scholars at Wycliffe Schools ' meant a red letter day. Each class received in- struction about the making of our mighty Empire, and the Union Jack figured largely in the programme of the day. Wycliffe is for- tunate in possessing the largest central hall of any school in the town. The various classes vied with each other in decorating their part of the hall, and the result was very pleasing and effective. The Union Jack and the flags of our Allies decorated the walls, and bunting streamed across the room. The rose bowl won by the girls for their prowess at rounders and filled with flowers displaying the national colours, occupied a place of honour in the centre of the hall, and near by stood one of the youngest and smallest girls—Bessie Fellows—holding a large Union Jack. To the singing of "When the Boys Come Home" the scholars marched into the hall, saluted the flag, and then took their allotted places. When all were assembled eager expectancy gleamed on every face, for each class was to provide an item to the afternoon's entertainment.</p>
        <p>In addition to the national and part songs, the following children took part in the pro- gramme :—Edna Beaumont, recitation, "A Song for England "; Edith Buck, recitation, "Red, White and Blue "; Selina Hill, recitation, "A Private of thP. Buffq "; Lillian Buck, recitation,</p>
        <p>"The Empire of our Dreams" (Alderman Ben- nett's poem); Annie Sullivan, song, "The Rosy Morn "; Harry Tatlock, song, "The Mother- land "; James Pearson and Albert Rowlinson. duet, "The Maple Leaf." Miss M. Swindells very effectively rendered the appropriate song, "Empire Day," the chorus of which was heartily entered into by the children. During the interval the Headmaster (Mr. Sydney W. Jeffery) delivered an address on Empire Day and the significance of the national flag with regard to duty, sacrifice, responsibility and obedience. After the singing of the National Anthem there were lusty cheers for his Majesty the King, the old boys of the school roll of honour, and a ringing cheer for the teachers, Messrs. Burtt, Cornall and Sinclair, who are serving with the Colours. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>OUR LADY'S SCHOOL, LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>OUR LADY'S SCHOOL, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Mimic Royal Review—And Great Loyal Demonstration.</p>
        <p>The celebration of Empire Day at Our Lady's School this year took the form of a mimic "royal review" in the 'school playground. At p.m. the head teacher (Miss A. Rourke) addressed the children on the meaning of Em- pire Day celebration, impressing upon them the necessity of being determined to grow up worthy of the great sacrifices which many of their fathers and brothers were now making for them, Sc) that in a few years' time, when they were men and women, they would be able to inherit and inhabit this great British Empire. At 3 p.m., the various classes, representing troops from a British colony or country, and wearing suitable uniforms, were marched into position by an officer (Master Lance Connor), each child saluting the Union Jack. The girls contributed a very pretty tableau, "Britannia," with "Wales," "Ireland," "Scotland," and " Italy " in attendance.</p>
        <p>After the review by the "'royal party" (Stan- dards VI. and VII.), an open-air concert was given, consisting of patriotic songs and recita- tions, national dances, and concluding with the National Anthem Cheers were given for the King and Queen, our soldiers kind sailors, and the flag of the Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PATRIOTIC CONCERT AT ST. ANN'S.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC CONCERT AT ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>The morning at St. Ann's School was spent in lessons given by the teachers on the Empire and its value to the Motherland. During the after- noon a patriotic concert was held. William Muskett sang "Keep the home fires burning," and the children took up the chorus in a spirited manner. Annie Lawless sang "The sunshine of your smile." A number of girls Sang "Loch Lomond," all joining in the chorus. Recitations were given. by May Garner, "Toll for the brave," and John Smith. Part-songs by the upper classes included "Three merry travellers" and "Ireland" .The lower classes, sang "Ken- tucky home and "The postman." At the con- clusion all the children sang "Are we down- hearted?"" Land of hope and glory," "Rule, Britannia," and "God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ST. PETER'S INFANTS.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S INFANTS.</p>
        <p>The children prepared for Empire Day by making the school gay with red, white and blue decorations and flags. Special lessons were given, and in the afternoon the children sang patriotic songs, marched round the playground, and saluted the flag.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J.OSEPH lIEESOIII,</p>
        <p>told him, he patted me on the back and said, "Well, you have made a good start."</p>
        <p>Writing later; Private -Ileesom Said he had heard that scale of his battalion had been recommended for the honours', but did not mention whether he was amongst them, but he sent the cheerful news of his distinction in a letter dated May 21st, which Mrs. Ileesom re- ceived yesterday morning. It was as folloWs: " I am writing to tell you that 1 have won the D.C.M., which news I suppose you will be sur- prised and glad to hear. I .got it in connection with the bombing raid. I don't know when I shall receive it, but I suppose I will get leave with it. lam now, resting for a few days." Private lleesom was educated at Bea mont School, where the news was received yesterday with great jubilation. The flag was hoisted, and the children gave three cheers for Private Heesom. Mr. W. ,Mason (headmaster) also applied for a holiday for the children, but this was not'granted in .view of the fact that Whit- suntide is so close at hand. We understand, however, that Mr., Mason will again ,apply if Private Ileesorn obtains leave:</p>
        <p>Private Heesom was formerly employed by the Longford Wire Company, and later by Messrs. Rylands Bros. He enlisted in Novem- ber, 1914, being only-17 years of age at the time. He attended the Brick-street Mission Sunday School, and played football for a local Associa- tion team. Another brother, Henry, has served the necessary time with the local Territorials, being v el-aided hJnne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CAMP TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>CAMP TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>The Story Of Colour-Sergeant Malings' Death.</p>
        <p>WILLIAMS COMMITTED.</p>
        <p>The Knockaloe Alien Detention Camp tragedy, reported at length in "The Examiner" last week, has had a sequel this week. It will be, remembered that Colour-Sergeant William Henry Malings, 46 years of age, a married hairdresser, of 269, Liverpool-road, Warrington, while on service as guard in the Alien Detention Camp, was shot dead. Sergeant John Williams, of the same com- pany, of the Second 4th South Lancashires, was arrested in connection with the affair. It was stated that Williams was a Liverpool man, but in a letter found upon him, addressed to Mrs. Williams, the address, 14, Ship-yard, Warring- ton, was given. We proceeded to make in- quiries at this address, but found the house empty, in which state a neighbour said it had been for some time.</p>
        <p>Against Sergeant John Williams, 47 years of age, a Coroner's Jury found a verdict of "Wilful murder," and yesterday he was com- mitted for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ISLE OF MAN PROCEDURE.</head>
        <p>ISLE OF MAN PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>Isle of Man law is somewhat different from the English law. The prisoner will next ap- pear before a Deemster and jury of six, to determine if there is sufficient evidence'to send him for trial. In that case the last act would be trial .at _General Gaol Delivery some weeks hence. At this Court three judges preside—the Clerk of the Rolls and the two Deemsters. The Lieutenant-Governor (Lord Raglan) is ex-officio a member of the Court. He is at present (and has been for some time) in London, unwell. The duties of Governor in his absence are per- formed by the Clerk of the Rolls, who has been appointed Deputy-Governor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>INQUEST RESUMED.</head>
        <p>INQUEST RESUMED.</p>
        <p>Curious Statements By Williams—</p>
        <p>And A Letter.</p>
        <p>The inQuest on the bddy of the late Colour- Sergeant Malings was resumed at the Peel Court House on Monday afternoon, Mr. 11. P. Kelly now representing Sergeant Williams. Mr. R. B. Moore appeared for the Crown. Joseph Madigan, special assistant of the com- mandant at the camp, said that when he' entered the hut where theshot had taken place he found the dead body of Sergt. Millings. A walking- stick was lying beside the deceased, near Ins hand. Malings suffered from some affliction, which affected his walking; that was Nay he used the stick. Witness searched Williams's ammunition pouch, which was hanging at the head of his cot. It should have contained ten rounds of ammunition—two clips each contain- ing five cartridges. It contained nothing. A clip containing five live cafiridges was handed to Sergt. John Sanger by Williams, who took it from his tunic pocket. There were also two live cartridges on his bed, and a spent cartridge ly:ng on the floor at the foot of the adjoining cot to Williams's. In Williams's trousers' pocket witness found two more live cartridges. That accounted for the whole ten. In Williams's trousers' pocket he found a letter, which stated: Dear Rose,—Please excuse me; I died in a good cause. Men to men. God bless you. Mrs. Williams, 14, Ship Yardi Warrington.</p>
        <p>On the other side of the notepaper was written:</p>
        <p>Twenty years' service with men, not a (word here indecipherable, apparently " tutor," but possibly "traitor.") Sending expert of armes. (Some more words indeci- pherable, probably a signature). I_ enclose</p>
        <p>ei4 to Mrs. Williams, NO. 14, Ship Yard, off Bridge-street, Warrington. When he entered the room Williams was seated at the foot of his cot on a box. He had spec- tacles on reading some printed matter and smok- ing a pipe.</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. R. B. Moore, witness said he* had taken the height of MaHugs, and, judging from the fact that the bullet passed through the top of his chest and the height of the broken window, be was of opinion that the bullet was fired at a lower plane than, the height at which the wound was made. The nature of the hole in the glass shoved that the bullet was fired from the extreme far end of the building froiq the door near where Williams's bed was. •</p>
        <p>By Mr. Kelly: He did not think the hole in the window had been made by a stone.</p>
        <p>Did you notice anything about the appearawe of the prisoner?—lle had a peculiar expression of face, which one would not associate as a rule with one of ordinary intelligence.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Stoll was called: but it was stated that he had gone across with the body and had not returned.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that he particularly wanted Sergt. Stoll's evidence.</p>
        <p>"THIS IS THE CARBINE THAT DID IT."</p>
        <p>Lance-Sergeant James Slingsby Duncan Clair said</p>
        <p>'On getting in the room I, saw Malihgs' body on the ground, lying on his back. I saw at once he was dead. Williams was against his own cot. Vie was then filling his pipe. My cot is next but one to Williams's. Williams did not say anything at this time, My carbine was lying on my bed and Williams's was lying on his bed. Two colour-sergeants came in and one said 'Which is the carbine that did it?' Williams said 'Find out,' and a minute or two after said 'This is the carbine that did it,' and he pointed to his own, which was lying on his bed. There was no quarrelling or cross words in the Sergeants' Mess." •</p>
        <p>By the Coroner: He did not know of any ill- feeling between the two men and he had been with them in the same room for seven months.</p>
        <p>By Mr.'Kelly: Williams began to walk about —a most unusual demeanour. It was not his usual habit to smoke when an officer was about.</p>
        <p>Corporal Unsworth said he had known both men for eleven months, and there was no unpleasantness between them that he knew of. Private Brown, who also rushed to the room on hearing the shot, said Sergeant Williams was standing by his cot. He was the only man there. He did not say anything, but looked dazed. "I have known both men," concluded the witness, "and I know of no reason why one should damage the other."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>"I AM SATISFIED."</head>
        <p>"I AM SATISFIED."</p>
        <p>Provost Captain Wm. Phillips.deposed that on Monday afternoon, while he was watching over Williams, the latter said: "I have rid of the bastard. I am satisfied." He made the state- ment voluntarily. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>By Mr. Kelly: Only the accused and I were present. This was about twenty minutes past five—about two hours after the occurrence. Wil- liams was sitting down about three yards from me. That was the only remark he made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SMART SERGEANT.</head>
        <p>A SMART SERGEANT.</p>
        <p>Lieut. Allen stated that on receiving a report that a man had shot himself he went to the ser- geants' sleeping quarters. The man who was shot had a stick in his hand. The ferrule was in his hand. He was struck at. once that the Inan did not shoot himself. Witness exautined a carbine lying on a cot on the right, but found it had not been used. One of the N.C.O.'s took a rifle off the other cot and said, "This is tie one." One could tell it had been discharged within an hour. There was an empty cartridge case on the floor on the right on Williams's cot, under the next cot. Witness asked who took the empty cartridge out of the breach, and Sergt. Williams, who was smoking his pipe, said, " did it." There were only two carbines lylng about.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moore: Did you hear anyone ask whose rifle it was?</p>
        <p>Witness: Someone said, "There is the one that did it," but I could not swear who said it. By Mr. Kelly: I told Williams to take the pipe out of his mouth when speaking to an officer. 'Williams seemed to be in a dazed con- dition. Knowing what a smart sergeant he was, it struck me as peculiar that he spoke to me with a pipe in his mouth. He always had the appearance of a smart sergeant. I know Malings, but do not know what he suffered from.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>"I HAVE MADE A CLEAN JOB OF IT."</head>
        <p>"I HAVE MADE A CLEAN JOB OF IT."</p>
        <p>Colour-Sergeant Harrison deposed': I have known these men eleven months and so fair as I know there was no ill-feeling between them. Both were well-conducted men. I was at Mr. Madig,an's office after in charge of Williams. Some time about 5.15 Williams said volun- tarily—l did not speak to him, but ho ad- dressed me—" The cur has been all my trouble</p>
        <p>Continued. lei preceding column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Women and Secrets.</head>
        <p>Women and Secrets.</p>
        <p>Wen are selfish in the matter of secreotms.Ther n r who will not share eele.e Very ery few their last one with' you if you 'give-threm a chance. ,</p>
        <p>was formerly employed by Messrs. Kenyon and Co., oil merchants. He was a member of the Young Men's Class at the Working Men's Mission, and h„,..,d been in the Territorials four years. His father is employed by Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., on the extracting plant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>-e -4=13E74-7-7:-.7*</head>
