      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ALIVE AND WELL.</head>
        <p>ALIVE AND WELL.</p>
        <p>Private Ireland Not Killed, But</p>
        <p>A Prisoner.</p>
        <p>Gloom and sadness caused by the official an- nouncement from the War Office that the bread- winner of a house in Warrington had been killed in action has been changed into happi- ness by the receipt of a. letter from the husband stating that he is alive and well. There is no happier • family in the borough than • Mrs. Ireland and her 'seven young chil- dren, residing at 77, HaydOck-street. • Some three weeks ago Mrs. Ireland received informa- tien from the War Office that her husband, Private john P. Ireland, of tlic West Ridinv, Regiment, had been killed in action on the 26t11 April. A letter was also received from Captain Malcolm, Robertson • confirming the sad intelli- gence._•_ • • _</p>
        <p>On Saturday, however, the post brought tho unhappy woman a letter from her "dead " husband and, naturally, her sadness quickly gave way to joy. The epistle .was very brig. but Privaite Ireland stated that he was captured while attending to wounded, 'and is now a prisoner of war in Germany, where the letter was posted. There is no doubt that, Private Ireland is quite safe and alive.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL B. PARSONAGE.</p>
        <p>Royal Engineer Dangerously Wounded</p>
        <p>In The Neck.</p>
        <p>News has reached Mrs. Parsonage, 100, Gorsey-lane, that her husband, Lance-Corporal Benjamin Parsonage, ol.! the Royal Engineers, has been dangerously wounded. A telegram received on 'Monday, ran :—" Regret to inform you that officer commanding 10th Stationary Hospital, reports 65832 Lance-Corporal B. Par- sonage, Royal ,Engineets, is dangerously ill with a gunshot wound in the neck. Regret also that permission to visit cannot be granted." Mrs. Parsonage has received A postcard from</p>
        <p>her husband, who says that the wound is very painful.</p>
        <p>- Lance-Corporal Parsonage, who is 38 years of age, enlisted on February 2nd of last year. He was previously employed as a joiner by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., at their Dallam- lane 'Brewery. He was educated at Orford School, and attended Orford Church. He was also an Orford "old boy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PRIVATE H. DITCHFIELD.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. DITCHFIELD.</p>
        <p>Killed By Shell Whilst Sitting Near Dug-Out.</p>
        <p>Through the medium of a letter written by an officer, Mrs. Ditchfield, 13, Dutton-street, has been informed of the death in action of her</p>
        <p>husband, Private Harry Ditchfield, of the Loyal North Lancashire Regireent. The letter, written Under date .May 20th, stated that "he was killed yesterday afternoon. by a heavy shell, whilst sitting in front of a dug-out in the re- serve line. Ditchfield is a great loss to us, as he was one of our strongest fellows and hardest workers, and we—the officers and men of his company—fully share your sorrow." This letter was Only received on Wednesday, and the Monday before Mrs. Ditchfield received</p>
        <p>reported to have died of wounds, and later wounded and missing, is now officially 'reported to have been killed in action on June 16th, 1915. This news has reached his parents at 5, Mill-. street, who for months have been anxiously awaiting news. Private Gibson, who worked as a plumber and painter With his father, received his education at the People's College, Arpley-street; and he formerly belonged to the choir of St. Paul's Church. He was also greatly interested in the company of the Church Lads' Brigade associated with that parish. He was a member of the lloro.!g't .Pand, and also of the. Cheshiro. Lines Baiid;*.-41111 Attended the Toiing Men'a Bible Class' at Bt. Paul's • • .</p>
        <p>have only got one finger to write with. I got it pretty hot and I am sorry to say I have lost my left eye, for I got a piece of shrapnel right into it\ Still, it is better to lose that than get killed. I also got hit in the left shoulder, both hands, and left. side of the head, so I reckon I am lucky not to have been killed outright. My left shoulder is all right now, and my right hand is nearly better, so I am doing fine. I got up to-day for the first time. Cheer up! the war is bound to come to an end some time. We are having lovely weather here." Rifleman Dickenson, who is 19 years of age, is one of two soldier-brothers, George being now in training. .Rifleman Dickenson worked at Orford Tannery before enlisting, and was edn- cated at the school .at Orford, in which district the family had livel almost all their lives, only leaving it very recently.</p>
        <p>• `;'•: ••••: . • ••:4 • ••k-•:•.tx•sa,,-.., ' 4 :4C,3 • • 114;.:Nt:-:, , • Kt' • •-; •</p>
        <p>PRIVATE Ir. DITCTIFIELD.</p>
        <p>• . . • ..• ..„..„ , „.„ , . -‘ • , • • „ , ' ; • , • • ...:,•;:iwV:Av's</p>
