      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PRIVATE J. RA YNOlt</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. RA YNOlt</p>
        <p>Bespattered With Shrapnel, But Li% ely For All That. Private John Raynor, whose wife lives at 83, Chorley-street, Warrington, has been wounded, but writes home a cheery letter, as will be seen</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN RAYNOR.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>by the extracts we reproduce. Private Raynor enlisted in the Manchesters in the Second month of the war and went out to France last November. He attended. King- street,School as a boy, and was in the choir at St. Mary's Church, Butterniarketstreet. Some time ago he was in the employ of the SL Helens Cable Co. and played football for that company's works' team. At the time he en- listed he was in the Counting House of the Calico Printers' Association, Manchester. He, is 27 years of age. Private Raynor, writing under date May 19th, says :—" I am pleased to itfohn you that I am going on 'O.K.' -To-morrow I am getting up; having only been in bed for a ,week. On the evening of the 11th, just at dusk, seven of us were going to take up our positions on what is called an advanced post. We had just got about half-ay there when 'Fritz' started sending over shrapnel. Anyway, we Continued to go 'along. until he put two shells in quick succession quite close to us. Just as we were making another . start, One bursted right amongst the seven of us. At first I thought the moon had dropped and hit me. I was abso- lutely covered with dirt, and everyone .of us, with the exception of one chap, had been hit, afid he was suffering terribly from shell shock. I can congratulate myself on my lucky escapes. In the first place, I must say that my shrapnel helmet saved My life. One piece went straight through it, but apart from a small scalp wound no further ,damage was done in that quarter. Another piece caught me in the stomach—this is the piece which put me in bed. Another fraction of an inch with this wound and I would have 'gone West' all right. Giving it you as the doctor put i, 'I am the luckiest/ man in the world.' Of the four other pieces that "found their way through my great-coat, one went through the cuff of my left sleeve, another piece through the right sleeve near the elbow, while the remaining two pieces also went through my coat. There was a hole in my shrapnel helmet as big as a five-shilling piece, so you can form an idea of how much larger the bole in my 'nut' would have been lint for the. helmet. I should not have been writing you now; I should have become a land- owner a week ago."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SERGEANT H. HEATH.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT H. HEATH.</p>
        <p>Writes That He Is Progressing Favourably.</p>
        <p>Sergeant H. Heath, of the North Staffs Regi- ment, whose home is at Burton-on-Trent, but whose wife now resides with relations at 38, Algernon-street, has been wounded in action, but, according to the' latest news received, ;s progressing favourably. He is an old soldier, and has been at the Front some time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Bishop Vaughan's Visit.</head>
        <p>Bishop Vaughan's Visit.</p>
        <p>The Right Rev. 'Bishop Vaughan, of Man- chester, preached at the morning and evening services at St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket- street, on Sunday. The Mayor (Alderman P. Peadock)-Was prest in hia-offielal 'capacity at High Mass in the morning. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>THEATRES.</head>
        <p>THE THEATRES.</p>
        <p>Royal Court.</p>
        <p>" Bella Donna" is a notable play notably acted by Mr. Dudley Stuart and Mr. Gerald Alexan- der's London Company.</p>
        <p>Particularly in the principals have we been fortunate. Miss Georgina Wynter has been a wonderful success.</p>
        <p>Mass Wynter is a lady or classical contour. As the, daughter of a c/ergyman, she met Canon Willis. on several occasions at her lather's house at. Wellingborough, and, she added with a smile, "What he admired most about me was my hair."--soft brown, abundant and billowy. She is the most beautiful lady who has acted at the; Royali Court this year. Miss' Parry, who was with Alr. Norris, also stands out in tho memory,. but she, *of another type.. _</p>
        <p>When "Bella Donna" was here about three years ago Miss Wynter was playing :Mrs: Chep- stow,.but she has not continued thus ever since. She 'remained Mrs. Chepstow for a year, and. then helped' topopularise "A Pair of Silk Stock- ings" in the provinces, afterwards going into. "Within the Law," as the elusive shop girl.</p>
        <p>Here was a part she esteemed even wore than that of Mrs. Chepstow, for it gave her ample opportunity to display her ability in strong' emotional characterisation. One gets rich Beams- of it in her meetings with Baroudi.</p>
        <p>Miss Wynter, 1" may say, is the wife of Mr. Dudley Stuart, the Hon. Nigel Armine.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Henry, who has created a very good' impression as. Dr. Meyer rsaacson and seems to have made a lif study of the character, has, as a matter of fact, only been with the company for three months. Until three or four months ago he was a leading baritone in Robert Courtneidge's productions. HIS wife is Miss Julie Venard (Mrs. Marchmont) who, until she attached her- si4f to this company, played" Lady Isabelle fn "The Whip."</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Marriott-Watson's company will occupy the boards at the Royal Court Theatre next week, when- "What Happened at Mid- night" will be presented twice nightly. Mr. and Mrs. Marriott-Watson will play the lead- ing Parts in the di-ama.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WONDERFUL WOMAN COMING TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL WOMAN COMING TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>La Somna At The Hippodrome. At the Hippodrome, Warrington, next week, there is appearing the most wonderful woman in the world.' In private life she is a delightful little person. In. the modern prophetess one would expect to find, some fabled ogress, or some • spiritualised mystic. I found neither (writes a representative Who interviewed her), but a dainty little lady, who sat playing with her Porn, and spoke with just the faintest suspicion of a lisp. And this was La Somna, the little lady who set all London talking, and who is equipped with the most wonderful gift. She has appeared several times before royalty and very large Society audiences. Even her little Porn, has its little tricks, and washed its tiny face for me, and with an exclamation of disgust told me its opinion of the Kaiser. It is an amazing and haunting performance which La Somna gives, and not the least striking is the easy man- ner in which she answers the thousands of ques- tions put to her. A question paper is handed' about the welfare of their future husbands at the Front. "The most trying part of my work," she said, "is answering hundreds of the many thousands of letters I receive from the patrons of the halls which I vAit: Just as it is impossible for me to reply to all the questions put to me, so is it impossible to answer all MI correspondents. Sometimes it takes half a day to open my letters, and I can only reply to the most urgent communications. I should like to reply to all, but were I to attempt to do so, the nature of my work and the tremendous strain it puts upon me, would soon Make me a physical wreck. That is an imnortant point overlooked to you as you enter the theatre, on which is. printed, "Please have your question written.</p>
        <p>LA SOMNA.</p>
        <p>and ready for La Sbmna, to answer." No invita- tion could be more direct and plain, yet many people appear to be hypnotised by the demand, or overcome by trepidation as to the result; but there is nothing to fear, as the wonderful woman answers the most delicate inquiry with the greatest tact. Her answers are given with, the greatest rapidity and accuracy, and the following are some of the questions replied to at a recent exhibition of her powers:— " Where is my wife?"' "When shall I get married?" "Will my husband return safe?" vr• "What is my regimental number?" "Who stole my diamond ring?" "When shall I retire from business?" "Where is my brother?" "Will I receive a money. settlement?" "' What is my next dividend'?" "Is the ticket I hold a winning one?"' "Am I engaged. If so, who to?" "Where was I wounded?" • "Will I pass my examination ?"- " Who wrote an anonymous letter to me?" As La Somna remarked, a very large number of inquiries come from young women anxious by many, .and that is why I cannot make my performances too long. Some don't care what happens to me if they get their questions answered. On the other hand, lam deeply indebted for many kind letters received from people to whom I have been instrumental, by the powers given to me, in bringing a little brightness into their lives." • La Somna was born at Newton Abbot, Devon. "It was there," she remarked, "I was dis- covered. By accident it was found out that I could read the mind's of people around' me, and had located the whereabouts of missing friends, and how to recover lost and stolen property. My parents were approached to allow me to go on tour through the country." "How keg ago is that?" "I must not tell you my age," said La Scmna in a charming manner, and added with a laugh, "Since that time I have toured London and the provinces. At the London l'allodium I demonstrated my powers to nearly fifty thousand people in one week." La Somna has delighted, charmed, and amazed many brilliant Society audienc',s, including a distinguished company at the Vio4,regal lodge. Dublin, amongst those present bring Lord and Lady Aberdeen, Duke of Westminster, Earl and Countess of Carrick, Field-Marshal Sir John French, General Forestier Walker, and many other notabilities. Their Excellencies Lord and Lady' Aberdeen thanked La Somna personally for her visit, and remarked that such powers as she possessed were really marvellous and the most wonderful they had ever seen. "You know whet they say of the seventh daughter of a seventh daughter," said La Sonana, "that she is gifted with some special gift. Well, I am one of those daughters, and my powers are simply inherited. Why, I cannot tell, but it 'does not retrain with me for very long periods. 'Often I think it may be taken from nie at any Moment, this. Dower of concentrating my will .so as to meet other people's brain waves, for if anyone puts a question to me, he or she must , be thinking about it so that I read what is passing through the min'!" • -Trulr, there are ore.)4.iiinge .in heaven and !earth than rn are draggat giti o philtitiophy.l</p>
