      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>laters to the (Ettitrit THE LATE MR, T. NIXON.</head>
        <p>laters to the (Ettitrit THE LATE MR, T. NIXON.</p>
        <p>IRISHMEN</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was indeed surPrised when I read in your issue of Saturday, last. the remarks of one or two .Members of the Rural' District Tribunal in reference to. the troubles of-,a 'publican, who had to look after the Irishmen who came into his public-house. It_ is very painful to Irish- men to have continually to hear and to read such remarks. Because a few 'fanatics rise up in rebellion are Irishmen in this country to be libelled in this manner and to be told that they have no right to be here? I wonder if Mr. Ball would like to send back to Ireland those brave Irish lads who are fighting in Flanders, and in every other place where there is fighting to be done for the Empire? Is the time so long past that the gallant deeds of the Dublins, Mun- sters, Connaught Rangers; and also the Ulster Volunteers, who are now fighting side by side with their Nationalist countrymen-, are for- gotten? Can we not point with pride to the dash and bravery of the Irish troops in far away Gallipoli, where so many of them laid down their lives in the sacred cause of liberty? But still in spite of all that Ireland and Irishmen heve done, and are still doing, there are individuals who never miss a chance to cast a. slur on the fair name of Ireland and on the Irish race. This is not the way to get the best out of the Irish people, whose loyalty, by the way, is the admiration of the whole world. In conclusion, I would like to offer to the Irishmen concerned in this case a little advice. Keep clear of public-houses, be sober and tem. perate, and you will then be a credit to the land that is proud to own you as her sons, and, at the same time, you will allow the publicans who so desire to go and do their bit with an easy mind.</p>
        <p>A HIBERNI..kN,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GREAT BRITAIN AND GERMANY.</head>
        <p>GREAT BRITAIN AND GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read very carefully and pleasurably the instructive and interesting article on "The Possible Economic Arrangements between Great Britain and Germany after the War," contributed to your columns a few weeks ago by Sir A. H. Crosfield. I think it is a subject deserving further extension, but one requiring a far abler pen than mine to deal with*. There: fore I„vielcomed the two vigorous and 'capable responses it has drawn from your correspond- ent, "Phil Squod," with whom I seem to feel in full agreement. But there is just one phase of the subject which presents itself to me, upon which I would like an expression of opinion, if possible, from both our friends, or others, who may be interested. It is this: Are we to be satisfied after the war with dealing with a Ger- many, constituted as she was before the war? I am one of those hoping and believing in the termination of the war, with Great Britain hav- ing a prominent voice, not in the mere arrange- ment, but in the dictation, of the terms of</p>
        <p>peace. My impressions are that nearly all our public men, and the Press generally, agree, that Ger- many' entered into this war (for which she' will have to pay the price) through the dominating influence of Prussia. When Austria and Prus- sia declared war upon Denmark and defeated her, they denuded Denmark of Schleswig-Hol- itein—Austria being then head of the German confederation. Prussia soon after, having ap- propriated Schleswig-Holstein, declared war upon Austria, defeated her, deposed the King of Hanover, pushed Austria out of the German confederation, appropriating Hanover as part of the Prussian dominion, and assuming the headship of the confederation herself. Then was engineered the war with France, the result being the seizure of Alsace-Lorraine and the crowning of the King of Prussia as German ,Emperor. I have roughly outlined these events, as they create precedents, and we are very much influ- enced by precedents. Naturally I do not pre- sume to say what statesmen ought to do, but I have opinions and ideas, which if I could give effect to them would be something.as follows: The re-creation of the State of Poland, also of Hanover and Bohemia, the peoples of each to choose their own forms of government.- If a monarchy, their own ruler; Alsace-Lorraine re- turned to France; Prussia I would put back to her original boundaries, and outside the Ger- man States, refusing to recognise her as having any part or voice in their destinies. As I have shown, she has created these, precedents for herself. May I remind your readers that United Europe, and not France herself deposed Napoleon I. and exiled him as a menace to the peace of Europe? I regard the present great conflict, in which we have lost thousands of the finest specimens of British manhood, as the outcome of the assumption and arrogance of the -present Ger- man Emperor, his family and intimates (he has brooked no adverse advisers) and unless he and they are personally punished and humiliated, I, for one at least, will feel we have not fully and efficiently achieved the end for which these brave boys have died. 125, Wilderspool-road,</p>
        <p>PATRICK O'BRIEN.</p>
        <p>May 22nd, 1916,</p>
        <p>A Terrible War. First Tramp: "This 'eres a terrible war, Bill."</p>
        <p>Second ditto: " Yus. What's the price o' beer now?"—" Punch."</p>
