      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LATE MR. T. NIXON,</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. NIXON,</p>
        <p>Messrs. T. Boscow, T. Gandy, R. Holland, W. Kerfoot, W. Dickens and A. Dickens (brothers- in-law)., Messrs. A. Boscow, T. Nixon, W. Nixon, B. Corless, H. Boscow and W. Allen (nephews), .and Mr. Phillips (Liverpool, cousin). Among others present were: Messrs. J. Pat- terson, T. Turner, A. Paul, W. Houghton, sen., W. Houghton, jun., Bibby, W. Wright, Fair- dough, • Chorlton, E. Stubbs, ,E.- Bennett, J, Ashley, Cook, F. Houghton, H. Murray, A. Cook, T. Booth, A. Warburton, F. J. Emson, J. Rowlinson, J. Harrison, _Hunt, Locker, Dykes, A. Browne (solicitor). Representatives of the Wine and Beer Sellers: Messrs. T. Roberts, J. Shaw, W. Longmore, W. Brookes, P. Lawless, B. Little, F. Bonnell, Dutton, T. Malkin, Hankinson and R. Thomp- son.</p>
        <p>Latchford Gun Club: Messrs. J. J. Fairhurst (Ashton Heys), T. Turner (Warrington) and J. Hall (Crowton).</p>
        <p>Latchford Garden Society: Messrs. G. Hooper and C. Foster.</p>
        <p>Rational Friendly Society: Messrs. J. Burgess and T. Bate. Tontine Club: Mr. J. Stockton. Latchford Homing Society: Messrs. W. Wright and H. Craven.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd.: Mr. D. E. Watson.</p>
        <p>The bearers were Messrs. R. Cook, J. Wrench, J. Cooper, E. Shoebridge, J. Locker, E. Brannen, A. Cook and A. Clare.</p>
        <p>Beautiful floral tributes were received from: "His Widow."" Dug' and Nellie," "Sam and Minnie," "Ralph and family," Mr. and Mrs. W. Dickens, Mr. and Mrs. A. Dickens, Mr. and Mrs. A. Browne,. Mr. and Mrs. J. Cook, Mr. Dykes and faMily, Mr. and Mrs. Bibby, Mr. and Mrs. Row- linson, Mr. and Mrs. Corless, Mr. and Mrs. Birch, "May and family," Mr. and Mrs. Boscow, Mr. and Mrs. T. Boscow, Mr. and Mrs. Nixon (Orrell, Liverpool), Mrs. Wright, Mr. Turner, Mr. and Mrs. Booth, Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Marsh, "Friends of the Top Room," Mr. S. Smith, Mr. Phillips and family, Mr. and Mrs. Patterson, Mr. arid Mrs. Kerfoot, Mr. and Mrs. Slater, Mr. and Mrs. Harrison, Mr. and Mrs. D. Johnson, "Tom and Siss," Mr. and Mrs. B. Daintith, Mr. and Mrs. W. Daintith, "Joe and Maggie," Mr. and Mrs. F. Lake, Mr. and Mrs. 'Looker, Mr. and Mrs. Boardman, Mr. and Mrs. Stubbs, Mrs. Starkey, Mr. and Mrs. Charlton, Mr. and Mrs. D. E. Watson, "Marie," Coun- cillor J. Locker and family, Mr. Stubbs and family, Sergeant B. Parsonage (2/4th South Lancashire Regiment), Latchford Homing ,1 Society, ,Beer and Wine Sellers' Association, ' Mersey District Gun Club, Warrington Angling Society, Black Bear Angling Society, and Branch 654, Rational Friendly Society. The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. Hampton Bros., of Thelwall, assisted by Mr. Bradford, of Messrs. Boydell Bros.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LATE Mil. J. KENWORTIIV.</head>
        <p>THE LATE Mil. J. KENWORTIIV.</p>
        <p>Impressive Funeral At Hill Chile.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. James Ken worthy, who died suddenly at his residence at Grappenhall, last week; were laid to rest in the Hill Cliffe Burial ground on Saturday after- noon, in the presence of a large number of friends.</p>
        <p>A short service was donducted in the chapel by the Pastor, the Rev. G. Curtis, who said that during the whole five years he had known Mr. Kenworthy, he had always found him a true Christian and a thorough gentleman. He had worked hard and well for the Church, to which his loss would be great. The chief mourners were :—Mr. Joseph Ken- worthy (father-in-law), Mr. 0. B. Kenworthy (cousin), Mr. P. Kenworthy (brother-in-law), Mr. J. Brierley (brother-in-law), Mr. D. Walker (cousin), Yorkshire; Mr. L. Kenworthy (nep- hew), Mr. A. B. Kenworthy (nephew), Messrs. John Bradford, W. H. Bradford, Ben. Bradfor I and Joe Bradford (cousins), Messrs. C. E. Row- 'son, J. Brocklehurst, P. Travis, A. Rathbow, P. Rathbone, the Rev. H. Ogden, and Messrs. Herbert Hatton, W. Oughtred (Manchester), J. Moss, son., J. Taylor, T. Grounds, J. Whitfield, J. R. Lilley, Fox and Booth. The following, representatives of variou, institutions and organisations, were also pre- sent:— Hill Cliffe Baptist diapel: Mr. / William Hough. / 41 L --</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Chapel: Mr. J. R. Conway (secretary). Freemasons: Brothers Leah and Rathbone.</p>
        <p>Knutsford Division Liberal Association: Councillor Henry Roberts, Mr. H.,Clifle r(secre- tary), Mr. J. Longland, Mr. A. Albinson, Mr. R. Billington and Mr. J. Rose. Beautiful floral tributes were placed on the grave from: 'Mrs. Kenworthy, "To 'my darling husband, a small token of affection ft/0m his sorrowing wife," "Father and Mother," "Sisters Anne .and Evelyn," Mr. and Mrs. j. Kenworthy, "Lawrence and Nellie," "Eric," "John Bradford and family," "With deepest sympathy, from Edith and all at Gorsey-lane," Mr. Stanley Gibbs, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Brad- ford, Mr. William Booth and -farnily, Officers and members of the Hill Cliffe Chapel Choir. Mr. and Mrs. Taylor (Martha), "James and Nessie,"._".With.4eepest_sympathy from rnall 't Reddish Hall," Mrs— Bradford, sen., Mr. and Mrs. A. Rathbone and Mr. and Mrs. Frank Fox.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>RATES.</head>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>Substantial Increase Caused By ' Epidemic Disease.</p>
        <p>The rate to be asked for at the ,meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council, on Monday night, will be 48. 4d. in the £ against 3s. 3d. last year. This increase of is. id. in tho £ has been caused by the recent epidemic of scarlet fever and diphtheria, which cost some £5OO or £6OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Chairman: Mr. J. 'l'. Ridgway. Military representative: Mr. A. de C. Parmiter. A chemist was granted conditional exemption on the grounds of national interests, the con- dition being that he remained at his profession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Father Starts Business For His Sons.</head>
        <p>Father Starts Business For His Sons.</p>
        <p>A coal merchant applied for exemption for his son (35), stating that he was his parent's chief support, and that he was indispensable to the working of the business. He bought the business five years ago, and his two sons assiste.l to manage it. ,He bought it chiefly for his sons. Both sons were married men, and the son appealed for had three children. The other had been rejected as medically unfit by the military authorities.</p>
        <p>Chairman: Could not you manage the business with yourself and the one son? Applicant: The son referred to recently had to be off work for 26 weeks, and I myself am 63 years of age.</p>
        <p>Chairman: Are you healthy? Applicant: Pretty fair; and I have to do my fair share of work.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal decided to grant eiemption un- til June 19th.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymm Platoon.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing Saturday, May 27th, 1916 :</p>
        <p>Tuesday.-7 p.m., Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Thursday.-7 p.m., Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill.</p>
        <p>By order, G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander</p>
        <p>Driver Dandy Arrives in France.—Mr. and Mrs. Fred Dandy, of Moston-street, Newfield% hav4 this week received word to the effect that their son, Driver James Dandy, of the A.S.C., has arrived in France. Writing home, he states: "I was very pleased to hear from you, and to learn that you are all right at home. Do not get down-hearted, but keep on smiling, and with a bit of luck I shall get through." Prior fó 'the war Driver 'Dandy was employed carter by the Irlam District Council.</p>
      </div>
