      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>10" DISTRICT PIEW, -,:j</head>
        <p>10" DISTRICT PIEW, -,:j</p>
        <p>LYMM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FATS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC. People seem to have got used quite auto- matically to the new "summer time."</p>
        <p>Why didn't the Church bells ring last Sun- day morning? They failed to herald the new "summer time."</p>
        <p>Possibly the bellringers forgot to put their clocks an hour forward before retiring on Satur- day night.</p>
        <p>Anyhow, the one bell that did ring, fairly " tolled ". the tale.</p>
        <p>Oh! it's s'nice to get up in the morning, but it's s'nicer to stop in bed.—llarry Lauder.</p>
        <p>What a time they had at Statham last Satur- day! And what a handsome contribution was raised fo; the shirt and sock fund!</p>
        <p>What a profitable game whist really is-2-13 is. 6d. was made at the whist drive at Wormald Green last week!</p>
        <p>Have you bought your flag yet? If not, why not?</p>
        <p>Remember what you are paying your money for. Not for the flag itself, but to help to build workshops for our maimed and disabled soldie7..s and sailors.</p>
        <p>Also a smile with every flag. Try it, boys!</p>
        <p>What is wrong with our Council Offices? k gentleman told me the other dak that they would always be incomplete as long as they did not possess a public clock.</p>
        <p>I politely told him that 'we were economising in "</p>
        <p>I saw Private R. Smith, who is home from the, Front, the other day, and he certainly looked the picture of health.</p>
        <p>I was very sorry to hear that nothing had yet been heard of his brother, Private Victor Smith, who has been missing several months.</p>
        <p>The sale of flags at the Primrose League con- cert on Thursday night realised A:2 Os. 2d.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Schofield is fast making a name. During his monologue "The Sheep and the Goats" he fairly held the audience spell- bound. '</p>
        <p>His talents do not stop at singing. He sings several of his own songs.</p>
        <p>The duets ;rendered by Messrs. Poole and Rabbitt were another speciality. The sweet blending of their voices was a remarkable feature.</p>
        <p>The members very rightly recognised the valuable services of their officers in the past and re-elected them for the coming year.</p>
        <p>What do you think of this "The life of a soldier"? I received it from a friend of mine the other day:—</p>
        <p>THE DAILY LIFE OF A SOLDIER. Written By Himself. 6.0 a.m.—Reveille--" Christians, Awake." 6.45 a.m.—Rouse Parade—" Art Thou Weary, Art Thou Languid?" 7.0 Parade—" When He Cometh." 8.15 a.m.—Manceuvres—" Fight the Good Fight." 9.30 a.m.—Company Orders—" Oft in Danger, Oft in Woe." 10.30 a.m.—Kit Inspection—" All Things Bright and Beautiful." 11.45 a.m.—Swedish Drill—" Here We Suffer Grief and Pain." 1.0 p.m.—Dinner—" Come All Ye Thankful." 2.15 p.m.—Ride Drill—" Go Labour On." 3.15 p.m.—Lecture by Officer—" Tell Me the Old, Old Story." 4.30 p.m.—Dismiss—" Praise God From Whom All Blessings Flow." 5.0 p.m.—Tea—" What Means This Anxious Eager Throng ?" , 6.0 p.m.—Free for the night—" 0 Lord Rosy Happy We Shall Be." 6.30 p.m.—Out of Bounds—" We Do Not Know, We Cannot Tell." 7.0 p.m.—Route March—" Onward, Christian • Soldiers." 9.45 p.m.—Zeppelin Raid Drill—" We Plough the Fields and Scatter." 10.0 p.m.—Last Post—" All Are Safely Gathered In." 10.30 p.m.—Lights Out—" Peace, Perfect Peace." 11.0 p.m.—Night Manceuvres—" T.he Day Thou Gayest Lord Is Ended." 11.30 p.m.—Guard Inspection—" Sleep On Be- loved."</p>
        <p>J. W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LATE PRIVATE JOHN STATHAM.</head>
        <p>LATE PRIVATE JOHN STATHAM.</p>
        <p>Last Letter To Ills Mother.</p>
        <p>Last week we recorded the fact that Private John Statham, a Newfields soldier, had been killed in action. He was 37 years of age, and leaves a widow and three children. He enlisted in NoVember, 1914, and was drafted out to the Dardanelles in ,June, 1915, and was wounded in the head in August, and returned to duty in October of the same year. He was employed by Councillor J. H. Holt, builder, of Barrs Bank, and worshipped at the Congregational Church. At one time he was connected with the local football club. " That Will Be a Day—Won't It?"</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother a few days before he was killed, Private Statham wrote:— " I am just snatching time to send a few, lines, hoping to find you both in good health, as it leaves me at present. We have just been moved again, but I am not allowed to tell you 'where, or anything at all about the place that we are at. I hope our Arthur comes through all right, and I hope that with God's help we shall very soon ..be together again. That will be a day, won't it? Please remember me to all at home. I cannot tell you. all I should like</p>
        <p>11-</p>
        <p>to in a letter, but you know the feeling that exists, so I hope that you will excuse this short letter. Good-night and God bless you both." This was possibly the last letter that the gallant soldier ever wrote, for a few days after- wards he was killed. It was received at Moston- street, Newfields, tour days after the official War Office communication, which told of his death.</p>
        <p>The brother Arthur referred to is at present serving "somewhere in France."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WORD ABOUT THE CAMERON HIGHLANDERS.</head>
        <p>A WORD ABOUT THE CAMERON HIGHLANDERS.</p>
        <p>By PRIVATE ERNEST SHAW. [Continued.]</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY, 1915.</p>
        <p>What weather! Rain, hail and frost! Every- thing to make life as unpleasant as possible, and as we muffled ourselves up and wrapped ourselves tighter in our 'great-coats our thoughts would wander to the bright, cosy kitchen fires at home, and a smile would light up our faces as we glanced down the trench and saw the men ,clamouring round the small coke fire, all eagerly listening to the funny yarns of a typical old Scotsman. How did we get on with the Belgians? Well, very well. They rather liked the Scotsmen in their tartan kilts and coloured gaiters,' and very often inconvenienced them- selves to get in our company. We often won- dered if they were really in earnest when they sang to us their praises, and very often doubted them, until one day we happened to come across a Belgian's diary, which contained the following extract :</p>
        <p>"They won our approval, the tall Englishmen with their beautiful uniforms, but I prefer the Scottish soldiers; really fascinating with their little caps with the floating ribbons, their tartan kilts, which swing rhythmically to the measure of their elegant march, their light- coloured gaiters fastened round their shapely legs, which contribute to give them that 'lordly' air of theirs., The Scots are the most splendid soldiers in the world. I would have liked to tell them so, but, unfortunately, I did not understand a word of English." Anyhow, let us get on with the diary. February Ist.—Two of our officers joined ranks of London Scottish. February 2nd.—Second-Lieutenant sent down to base, sick.</p>
        <p>February 4th.—Captain Grieve and Lieuts. Wilson and Mclntyre joined us from England. Special training given to snipers and machine gun sections. February 7th.—A draft of 37 arrived from —' under Lieuts. Pattison and Sloan. February 15th.—Battalion moved to other billets in the village of , about one mile off. Arrival of fresh draft of 102.</p>
        <p>February lith.—Men issued with flat High- land bonnet instead of the Glengarry cap. These bonnets looked very well, but appear to have been made for Light Infantry or Rifles, as they are dark. green, with dark green toories, no dicing. February 21st.—Lieut. Peto, M. 8., R.A.31.C., replaced Lieut. Milne, M.D., R.A.M.C., as medi- cal officer to the battalion.</p>
        <p>February 25th.—General inspected the batta- lion,</p>
        <p>• February 27th.—Marched from p.m. and went via , over canal. Here we took over breastworks, not trenches. Occu- pied firing line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TO THE Llllll BOYS AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>TO THE Llllll BOYS AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>desires to Mrs. Jack Reid, of Cherry-lane, thank her husband's comrades, through the columns of "The Examiner," for the splendid present she has recently received through Sergeant-Major It. Clare. She trusts that God will protect them from all harm and bring them safely home again, and give her the opportunity of thanking them Personally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SCHOOL CHUMS MEET IN EGYPT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL CHUMS MEET IN EGYPT.</p>
        <p>Private James Jennings Longs To See</p>
        <p>It Raiti.</p>
        <p>Writing to his home at Moston-street, New- fields, Private James Jennings says:— "I have got the Paper with poor Sam's re- pqrt in, and I think it is very good, so I .have cut it out and mean to keep it. We are still in the same place, with nothing to see but sand, more sand, and still more sand. You ask me if I have had a change yet. I am glad to say I have. We are having almost every eomfo-rt that's needed at present—good food; and, what is , still a greater luxury, plenty of water to wash our clothes with. You say that you are going to send me some socks. Don't I wish I had them now, for I have only one pair, and those are the ones that are at present decorating my feet. We get a lot of marching to do, -so. YOU can imagine that one pair is not of much conse. quence. It would amuse you if you saw us on washing day, for we have to take our socks off to wash them, and go barefooted until they are dry. Of course, • it- only takes-a matter of •about 10 minutes Or a quarter of an hour to dry them.</p>
        <p>Otherwise we are all right, for, as I said before, we have plenty of everything. "I believe you are having some very damp weather over in England, and I suppose that accounts for most of the sickness that is spread- ing about. You ought to see the diseases that we have to fight against. No wonder they inoculate us, 'for what with the dirty streets and bad sanitary arrangements, one could not be sur- prised at anything. It is quite a common sight to go out in the desert and come across dead camels lying in the full glare of the sun. The natives don't seem to care a rap. Oh! how I wish it would. rain! I don't think I have seen it rain out here yet. You can always depend on a nice day, only when its windy; then it's get under cover. If you don't—well, you get blinded with sand!</p>
        <p>Would Have Been a Fine Easter.</p>
        <p>"You say that Corporal Agthur Read has just been over on furlough. I wish that I could have been over too. Willie Taylor and I, would not we have had a time the three of 'us? You can depend on it that it would have been the finest Easter of my life. Well, I hope he had a good time, for he has done his bit, and well-earned his furlough. I was out on Good Friday with Willie Taylor, and a very good time we had together. We did intend to have our photo- graphs taken,'frat when we saw what they were going to cost, we reserved our judgment. Fancy 4s. for half a dozen, they must think we are made of money. Perhaps- it would surprise them if they knew that we were the proud possessors of five bob every fortnight." Private James Jennings is a well-known local lad, and the William Taylor referred to is an nlO ar.hr,nl chum whom he accidentally met with in Egypt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>• FLAG DAY.</head>
        <p>• FLAG DAY.</p>
        <p>To-day's Great Effort For Soldiers' Workshop Fund. Yesterday was "Flag Day" in this district, and to-day will again be "Flag Day," when a great effort is to be made to raise money for the LoTil Roberts Memorial Workshop Fund. The workers responsible for supporting this worthy oL)ject locally are Chairman and Hon. Tr3a.3ur,3r.--The Rev. 0. B. Thurston. Hon. Secretary.—Mr. F. E. Evans. Committee.—Rev. C. Egerton-Warburton, Rev. Pi. B. Lovell. D. Burrows, Mr. J. G. Lister Farrar and Mr. W. T. Limb. The district is divided up into several wards, and each ward w 4 be in charge of a President, as follows: • Lymm Ward.—Mrs. T. J. Ridgway. Heatley Ward.—Mrs. Tudor Evans. Booth's ,Hill Ward.—Mrs. A. W. Callender.. Statham Ward.—Mrs. Slaney. Warburton Ward.—Mrs. Egerton-Warburton. Partington Ward.—Rev. J. Batey. Thelwall Ward.—Rev. C. M. Johnson. That Broken Soldiers May Be Independent. After former wars little has been done to provide for the soldiers who have been wounded. The Lord RolArts Memorial Work- shop Fund seeks to make this reproach impos- sible after the present war. It is proposed to build workshops in which our unfortunate heroes are to be trained in the best way that, 'they can make a living. What are these workshops? They. are light, airy factories, several of which were inaugurated at the close of the South African War, and have proved most valuable in turning helpless cripples into valuable citizens. They will serve a double purpose—they will provide useful, healthy occupation for the men employed, and also help . England to produce goods that in many cases have hitherto been provided by enemy countries. How can you help in this good work? By giving every assistance that lies in your power to this scheme. Let Lymm to-day see to its proud record of helping to the uttermost every good cause that stands in need of friendly and material assist- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL E. HADDEN.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL E. HADDEN.</p>
        <p>"Nearer Old Fritz," But Does Not See Much Change.</p>
        <p>Writing to an employe in the Post Office, Lance-Corporal Ernest Hadden states "At last I am finding time to write you a few lines and I trust that they will find you well and hearty, as you usually are. Allow me to thank you very much for the socks you so kindly sent me; they are very comfortable. They have recently undergone a severe test with a long march, at the conclusion of which my feet were in splendid condition. Much depends on the socks you wear during these long marches. I suppose you often wonder how things are going on out here. Well, I don't think there is the slightest change from what it was this time last year. I think that by the time you receive this letter we shall be up the line again and a little bit nearer 'Old Fritz.' It will seem like a new thing to us, for it is fully four months now since we saw the trenches. What dp you think of the new Bill? I .am 'sure it will be very hard for some folks, hut the sooner we get this horrid business over the better it will be. Remember me to all at the Post Office, and tell them that I shall be pleased to receive a line any time." Lance-Corporal E. Hadden was mobilised at the outbreak of war with the Cheshire Regi- ment and was drafted out to France some 16 months ago. He is a single man and has several brothers serving with the colours. Prior to the war he was employed at the Post Office as a postman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>STATHANI'S LITTLE EFWORT.</head>
        <p>STATHANI'S LITTLE EFWORT.</p>
        <p>Successful Effort For Sock And Shirt Funfl.</p>
        <p>Lymm has encouraged its lads to go and do their bit, and right loyally they have responded. While they are away everything is being done, for their comfort. People at home have realised what it really means to be on active service, and the hardships they have to endure. Dirt and filth are the greatest enemies that our gal- lant " Tommies " have to fight. We at home mast supply him with what is necessary to wipe out these treacherous foes. To enable this to be done a shirt and sock fund has been organ- ised, and up to the present it has met with tre- mendous success.' On Saturday the people of Statham rose to the occasion, with the result that .210 has been handed over to Mrs. Thurston, the hon. treasurer. This was raised through the means of an entertainment and sale of work, which was organised by Miss Dalton, of Ivy Cottage, who was assisted by Mesdames Watson, Hench and Proud, and the 'Misses Guthrie, Dixon, Little, Walton, Wright and Dalton, and took place in the Statham Schools. The principal attractions were a hoop-la stall, which was exceedingly popular, especially amongst the ladies and wounded soldiers from Brookfield, who were the guests of the after- noon. A stall which displayed household and fancy goods was very, well patronised, and a jumble sale, conducted at the other end of the room, judging by the way the various bargains disappeared, was perhaps the most attractive of the Whole lot. During the afternoon an enter- tainment was given by several local ladies, and was thoroughly enjoyed by the whole company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PRIMROSE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>PRIMROSE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Meeting, Social And Concert Of The Lymm Habitation.</p>
        <p>I The annual Meeting of the Lymm Habitation of thet Primrose League was held at the Con- servative Club on Thursday evening, followed by a social and concert. Mrs. Cornwall Legh , by lminr. g caonudncmillros. W. P. Rylands, Mrs. A. W. councillor) presided. and was supported Callender and Miss Moorhouse. The Hon. Secretary (Mrs. A. W. Callender) ned' reported that notwithstanding present trying chiardcumbeestnanfcaeisrly well maintai . The total, the strength of the ,habitation members at present on the books was 472. A letter received from the Grand Council on the 14th August, 1914, suggested that the various habitations shoilld make an allowance from their funds to be invested in war funds—On the motion of Mr. T. F. W. Dixon the report was adopted. Mrs. W. P. Rylands (hon. treasurer), sub- mitted her report, which showed a balance in hand of £47 2s. 11d. Then came the election of officers for the 'coming year. Mr. W. p. Rylands proposed that Mrs. Cornwall Legh be re-elected -ruling -councillor. This was seconded-- by Mr. W. E. Hillier and carried. unanimonsly.</p>
        <p>Other elections were made as follows :—Mrs. W. P. Rylands, hon. treasurer; Mrs. A. W. Callender, hon. secretary; Miss E. Callender and Miss Moorhouse, assistant secretaries; the Executive Council was re-elected; Mr. J. Thomason, auditor. .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. itylandtyroposed that .00 of the Habitation funds 'sEnuld be invested in five per cent. Exchequer bonds. This was seconded by Mr. T. F. W. Dixon and carried unanimowly. Mrs. Cornwall Legh explained that the pro- ceeds of the sale of flags.at the door prior to the meeting would gO to help our soldiers who were so gallantly facing the dangers of war. She.hoped that the members would not be dis- appointed at not having their annual tea and dance, but dancing was out of the question at the present-time. She also paid a compliment to the Lymm men who had answered the call, and, dealing with the slackers, said that some- thing was radically wrong with the parents of those whose sons and malefolk- shirked their duty. She hoped that at the next annual meeting they would be discussing the colebra- tions of peace.</p>
        <p>. Mr. W. P. Rylands proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Cornwall Legh for her inspiring address, stating that this dreadful war had now been on almost two years and looked like being on another two years; but when the silver lining did come he believed that it would come suddenly. The party then partook of refreshments. The concert opened with a violin solo by Miss Reynolds', followed by d duet by Messrs. Poole and Rabbitt, "Flow, gentle Deva "; humorous song by Mr. H. E. Gibbon; song, "Song of Britain," Mr. Rabbit-t; screamingly funny song, "Would you mind passing the salt?" Mr. A. Schofield; song, 'The Bedouin's Love Song," Mr. Poole; song. "Come Sweet Morning," Mrs. George Williamson; violin solo, Miss Reynolds; duet, '"l`he moon hath raised her lamp," Messrs:, Poole and Rabbitt; humorous song, "Has it ever occurred to you?" Mr. H. E. Gibbon; song, " tip from Zummerzet," Mr. Poole, encore; song, "I wonder if you miss me sometimes?" Sapper Crossby, from the Brookfield Hospital; monologue, "The Student," Mr. A. Schofield, encore monologue, "The Sheep and the Goats"; song, "Sons of the Motherland," Mr. Rabbitt, encore. • At the conclusion of the concert Mrs. Cornwall Legh thanked the artistes and guests for their presence. •</p>
        <p>The Matron of the Brookfield Military Hos- pital replied on behalf of the soldiers, and thanked the organisers for so kindly inviting them. She also thanked the people of the village for what they had done for the soldiers in the past.</p>
        <p>A Pretty Khaki Wedding.—Oughtrington Church was on Wednesday the scene of a very pretty khaki wedding, the contractinc, parties being Sapper James Wood, of the Engineers, eldest son of Mr. Wm. Wood, builder, Broom Edge, Lymm, and Margaret nightield, youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Geo. Highfield, Tower Cottages, Lymm. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. G. S. Tudor-Evans. The bride, who was charmingly attired in a dress of soft material of a lovely shade of blue, and carried a sheaf of lilies, was accompanied by four bridesmaids, Miss Ada Wood and Miss Nellie Clay, and two little girls, Peggie and Maggie Wood, who carried baskets of pink tulips and smilax. She was given away by her father. The best man was Mr. James Young, of War- rington. Sapper Wood, who enlisted in Septem- ber, 1914, is about to go to the East on active service. He has two brothers and the bride one brother already on active service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>EMPIRE DAY.</p>
        <p>The Empire Of School And Of Self.</p>
        <p>At the Church of England School, Stockton Heath, on Wednesday, the scholars were ad- dressed in the yard by Mr. Rose, the head- master, who spoke of the Empire, the empire of school and the empire of self. Dealing with the British Empire, he referred to its great size and countless productions and pointed out that an empire consists of people; that 225 pieces, i.e., citizens of the empire, were now assembled; and that good citizens make a good empire, bad citizens make a bad empire. At this point the National Anthem was sung. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose, dealing with the empire of school, said the school consists of scholars; if scholars are good, so is school; bad scholars make bad school.</p>
        <p>The school song (composed by Mr. Rose) was sung. The chorus runs:—</p>
        <p>"Then scholars all, great and small, do your best, Let honour be your rule. You must work and play, ever fight and pray, For the honour of the dear old school."</p>
        <p>The empire of self, said the Headmaster, is the most important empire—good selves make good school and a good empire—the higher self must rule.</p>
        <p>A verse of "0 God, our help in ages past," was sung, and the .flag was unfurled. The scholars did not cheer, but were told that when they saw a Union Jack it must remind them of the Britisli Empire, the empire of school, and the empire of self—the last empire being always the most important.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 29th May, 1916, issued by Mr. Frank Warburton, O.C. Monday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m. Tuesday.—Musketry, Appleton Rifle Range, 5.30_ p.m.</p>
        <p>Th-ursday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m. The Detachment 'is still open for recruits, either under or over military age, and for any others desirous of preliminary instruction to fit themselves to help their country. Saturday (to-day).—Uniform Parade. Fall-in Victoria Square, 3 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending June 3rd :</p>
        <p>Monday, Musketry, at the Range. 7 p.m. to 8 p.m., Class Firing and Practice; 8 p.m., "Daily Mail" Competition. _</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Lecture at the Drill Hall by Capt. Pearson, at 8 p.m. Wednesday.—Drill. The Company Sergeant- Major will attend and a good muster is there- fore specially required. Fall-in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m, sharp.</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry, at the Range. 7 p.m. to 8 p.m., Class Firing and Practice; 8 p.m. "Daily Mail" Competition; this competition closes on this date. •</p>
        <p>Saturday, June 3rd.—Parade (in uniform). Fall-in at Headquarters at 2.45 p.m. sharp. Orderlies.—Section Commander F. Short and Private J. Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>The following appointments appear in the current Battalion Orders :—Section Commander C. G. Hutchings to be Platoon Sergeant; Section Corporal W. Trantom to be Section Com- mander; Private F. Fox to be Section Corporal: Platoon Quartermaster C. F. Read resigns his position as Section Commander. By order, F. A. FROST, O.C. Headquarters, 26t1i31ay, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>A CHRISTIAN SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Memorial Service At Hill Chile To Corporal Fletcher. The Hill Cliffe Baptist Church was crowded on Sunday night, on the occasion of a memorial service to the late Corporal Percy Fletcher, of the South Wales Borderers, who died from wounds received in action on April 29th. Corporal Fletcher attended the Sunday school, and was keenly interested in the Free Church Boys' Brigade, in which he held the rank of Lieutenant. A large contingent of the Boys' Brigade attended the service to pay their</p>
        <p>tribute of respect. - • The Pastor (the Bev. G. Curtis), paying a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WAR-TIME CURE FOR ALL FOOT TORTURES.</head>
        <p>A WAR-TIME CURE FOR ALL FOOT TORTURES.</p>
        <p>MUNITION WORKERS FIND NOVEL WAY TO BANISH FOR EVER THE CORNS, CAL- LOUSES, SORENESS, SWELLING, ACHING, ETC., CAUSED BY UNACCUSTOMED FOOT</p>
        <p>STRAIN.</p>
        <p>Foot misery is 'often the first diffieulty encoun- tered by munition workers who have to remain on their feet all day. I found, however, that all such tortures can be instantly relieved and permanently cured by simply resting the feet a few minutes in a warm foot-bath containing a tablespoonful of ordinary Reudel,Bath Saltrates. This softens even the hardest corns so they come right out at the touch, root and all. The feet being the farthest points to which the blood must be pumped, foot troubles are usually due to defective circulation and irritation in. these extremities. The hot saltrated bath will stimu- late the circulation, instantly soothe and relieve any painful congestion due to shoe pressure, reduce swelling and open the clogged pores. This renders the skin active and healthy, pre- vents offensive perspiration odours, and banishes smarting, burning, itching or other foot afflic- tions for all time. It is marvellously effective for soldiers' "trench foot," chilblains, bunions, and similar troubles, even including rheuma- tism, gout, &amp;c. Most chemists keep the common refined Reudel Bath Saftrates ready put up in convenient packets, one of which should prove sufficient to permanently end all foot misery at slight cost. ,Try this refreshing foot-bath after coming in footsore from a long walk or after being on your feet all day. You will soon feel like dancing with joy, and your newest, tightest boots seem like the oldest pair you have.—D.L.C. IMPORTANT NOTE.—Upon enquiry we find that although supplies of the above compound are limited, local chemists can still supply reasonable quantities from stock, and there has as yet been no advance in price. Air in the case of all drugs, how- ever, we are told that a sharp rise may be expected shortly, and it is therefore advisable to obtain a supply while it is still to be had easily and at very low cost.</p>
        <p>tribute to the deceased, said he was beloved by all who knew him. He had been a splendid worker in the church, choir. and Sunday school, and they would all feel the loss very keenly. But they felt it an honour that such a young man had first of all given this heart 1-,) God and to the Church, and then had given his fife for his country. Their deep sympathy went out to the sorrowing young wife, who was overcome with grief, and to his sister (Hrs. G. Ault), who had all their life been all and in all to each other.</p>
        <p>Corporal Fletcher's favourite hymns were rendered, and Mrs. Roughsedge played the Dead March in "Saul." The hymns were "Beloved, let us love," "I'm but a stranger here," "God is lave, His mercy brightens," and "Nearer, my God to Thee."</p>
        <p>A solemn and impressive service was closed by a contingent of buglers sounding the "Last Post."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Egg Collection.—The scholars of the Padgate C.E. Schools again gave evidence of their patriotism when a collection of eggs for the wounded soldiers was made in the schools on Thursday and Friday last week. The total number collected was 325, besides Gs. in cash. The eggs were distributed on Saturday to local military hospitals in the following proportions: Winwick, 100; Whitecross, 40; Raddon Court, 70; Thelwall, 70; Warrington Barracks, 45.</p>
        <p>Church Parade.—On Sunday evening a church parade organised by the St. Elphin's, St. Paul's and Padgate Companies of the Church Lads' Brigade took place. Headed by the various bugle bands the lads attended the service held in Christ Church, Padgate. The Rev. J. Hirst preathed from the Brigade motto, "Fight the good fight," and the National Anthem was heartily sung at the conclusion of the service. After service a combined route march was ar- ranged, under the command of Major Brooke, the route being along Fearnhead-lane to Cinnamon Brow and back again. Captain Simpson was in charge of the Padgate Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>A Productive Jumble Sale.—Despite the heavy calls on both time and purse of all at the pres- ent time, the lady workers of the St. Mary's Mission at Penketh managed to clear over £25 at the jumble sale held in the Mission Room last week-end, which sum has been handed over to the church fund.</p>
        <p>Empire Day.—On Wednesday a good number of parents and friends gathered to watch the Empire Day celebration at the Council School, Penketh. Dr. Murray was the chairman, and speeches were given by him and by the Head Master, Mr. Griffiths. The programme, which included patriotic songs, Maypole dances by the infants, and country dances by some of the older scholars, concluded with the National Anthem, after which the whole school, class by class, marched past and saluted the flag. Special mention was made in the speeches of the help which the children were able to give to the wounded soldiers in the local hospitals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>WHITLEY.</head>
        <p>WHITLEY.</p>
        <p>CONCERT PROCEEDS FOR SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>• A successful concert was held on Tuesday in the Assembly Rooms, Grimsditch, to provide funds for the Whitley Working Party, who are busily engaged in making comforts for the soldier boys who have gone from the district. Mr. F. G. Fairhurst presided over a good attendance. An excellent musical programme, including vocal and instrumental items, was submitted, in which the following ladies and gentlemen took part :—Miss Rigby, Miss Black- house, Messrs. Davies, Dutton, and Clayton. Mr. Wilde contributed two concertina solos, and Messrs. W. W. Whittle and A. Dutton. provided the humorous side of the entertain- ment. Mr. Cyril Clayton acted as accompanist, and to Mr. J. Clayton, of Thelwall, credit is due for organising the concert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending June 4th, 1916: Parades.</p>
        <p>Saturday (to-day).—Route March to Appleton. Fall in at 2.30 prompt. Rations to be carried in haversacks. The band will attend. Tuesday.—Fall in at 7.30 prompt. Uniform. Drill, &amp;c. Band Practice.</p>
        <p>Friday.—Fall in at 7.30 prompt. Uniform. Instruction, according to weather conditions. Sunday.---Church Parade. Fall in at 10 a.m. prompt. Orderlies of the week.—Corporal W. Rigby and Lance-Corporal N. Garlick. By Order,</p>
        <p>J. B. STARKEY, C.O.</p>
        <p>OVERWORK AND WORRY DRIVE A HARb BARGAIN.</p>
        <p>It doesn't pay to defy nature's laws. We all need peace of mind, rest, outdoor exercise and eight hours sleep to keep well. We must not overeat, nor drink much, unless it be milk or water. These are both good and should be freely used.</p>
        <p>Anything- bad leads to trouble. Bad habits are no exception. Careless living and overeating are among the worst—they help load the blood with Uric Acid poisons. The kidneys act as safety valves, filtering the blood and passing off , the poisons, but they are bound to weaken under any long continued strain, and once they falter, you feel dull, tired, and nervous. Your back aches, you lose weight, you have head- aches, dizzy spells and urinary disorders. The first thing to do is to get back to simple, sensible habits. Eat less, sleep more and be careful what you drink. But you must also rheeeloptptinheenwdexlakoe4n4e.,sd. kidney:. Ta can rely on Doan's Backache Kidney. Pills for this help. They are a special medicine for the kidneys and bladder only. That is why so many peoPle. • Alrdealers, or 2/9 a box from Foster-7110MM* ' Co., 8, Wells St., Oxford Sc., London, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>"LONG PULL."</head>
        <p>"LONG PULL."</p>
        <p>"Off" Licsnce Hoicier Fined LG.</p>
        <p>• A fine of was imposed on Frederick T. Lashfard, 78, Battersby-lane, " off " beer seller; for giving the "long pull" in contra- vention to the regulations of the Liquor Control Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. 11. J. Jenkins represented defendant . -w- -and admitted the offence.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that on the night of the 18th inst. a 'person went to defendant's preinises and asked for a pint of beer. lie produced a bottle, .which he handed to the defendant, who drew the beer from a barrel by means of a pump, but did not use a measure. The liquor in the bottle was measured ,by an expert, who found the quantity to be one pint and five and a helf Amid ounces. 'The proceed- ings had been taken on account of complaints received.</p>
        <p>• .The Chief Constable was referring to an alleged offence on the previous day, when Mr. Jenkins objected and said his client . was charged with. the offence. on the 18th,, and the Chief Constable's remarks . should be coßfined to that offence, and not go beyond it. It was exceedingly unkind and unfair. The Chief Constable said he thought he was doing defendant and his • selicito'r a good ser- vice. " Invariably,when you do people a good service this is how you get treated," added the ,Chief Constable. The Chief Constable; continuing, said that on the night of the alleged offence, several other per- Sons were instructed to purchase beer from defendant's shop, and in every case mere than the legal measure was given. When the de- fondant's attention was called to the offence he replied. "I give a drop Over for freshness." For the defence, Mr. Jenkins said that on the day in question business was very ',brisk. The beer was 'very fresh- and it was exceedingly difficult to draw, the exact measure. Defendant had .no intention of. contravening the Order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>FROM OLD CALABAR.</head>
        <p>FROM OLD CALABAR.</p>
        <p>Black Men Attract Gids, Says</p>
        <p>Chief Coistara:,e.</p>
        <p>"There has been.a.lOtof trouble with some Of These black men. Thy seem to attract the girls more than they. should was a remark by the Chief Constable. yesterday at the Borough Court, when a native •of Old Calabar, named Alfred E.- kjones, of 21, Barbauld-street, applied for a war- rant against a fellow countryman, named Peter Johnson, of 14, Stanley-street. The Chief Constable said Jones was deserving of every consideration. He was only nineteen years old, and not being on friendly terms with some other coloured men, had left them to --go to other lodgings._ The other night; however, he went to a house in Stanley-street, and there three men, named Peter Johnson, Samuel Brown and" Philip George, locked him in a room and 'assatilted him, Johnson knocking one of complainant's teeth out. They said they had done it because of what Jones had remarked about them and some girls. Jones, said the Chief, was deserving of the protection 'of the Court. The magistrates granted a‘warrant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>WOOD PULP FROM CANADA.</head>
        <p>WOOD PULP FROM CANADA.</p>
        <p>To Make Us Independent Of</p>
        <p>Scandinavia.</p>
        <p>Reuter's Agency understands ,that ,consider- able Progress has been made with arrangements for the establishment in Canada of a mill for .the production of sulphite pulp on a large scale, and it is expected it will be neeessary in future to import very little wood pulp to this country from Sweden. This will have the additional, advantage of favourably affecting the present rate of exchange. It has been stated in the 'Press that .the present cessation of imports of wood' pulp from Sweden was partly due to the action of Great Britain. This is not the case. The prohibition was one of export, and originated with the Swedish Government. The Swedish prohibi- tion will, however, for reasons mentioned above, turn out to be a blessing for Canada, and manu- facturers in this country are confidently hoping that the freedom from dependence upon Swedish supplies will be permanent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>ARE YOU BILIOUS?</head>
        <p>ARE YOU BILIOUS?</p>
        <p>Most people are bilious—at times. It may be that they have violent•headaches; somethhes with vomiting, or they are dizzy. or see spots floating before their eyes, or tlhey have a bad taste in the mouth, and the tongu6 is thinly coated. AU these are common symptoms of liver disorder, and there are ,others. consti- pation. for example. is often simply the result of a faulty liver action.</p>
        <p>If you suffer in any of these ways, try the effect of the world-famous liver todic and stomachic remedy, Mother Seiges Syrup. The medicinal e:dracts of the.Svrap have a won- derful regulating and tonic effect upon, the action of the liver, the stoma:-11, and the bowels, and if you would enjoy really good health; you .must keep all these important organ:; in sound working condition.</p>
        <p>iNEW CAMERAS FOR OLD ONES f If you want a new Camera let us buy your old one for cash or take it in part exchange and make you a in' allowance. If you want a second- hold camera send for our list-25 per cent. chlr.:;z: : than possible to purchase elsewhere. ri WATSON'S —theoctioalists SBll-iETTIqEb</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>HAIR DESTROYER</head>
        <p>HAIR DESTROYER</p>
        <p>James' Depilatory</p>
        <p>Instantly removes Superfluous Hairs from the tees Ilech, or Arms, without injury to the akin. Of most Chemists; or, free from observation, post free on roceipt Of Postal Order for is. 3d., 9s. Pd., N. JANES .Caledonian Road, London, N.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>"WOULD DO HIM GOOD."</p>
        <p>Wife Of A Railway Labourer Granted Separation Order.</p>
        <p>At .the Borough Court on Monday, Albert Mann, a L. and N.-.W. Railway labourer, 13, Hume-street, was fined 20s. for an aggravated assault tipon his, wife, Dora Mann. • Complainant stated that on Friday' she visited the Police Station to make a complaint respecting prisoner, who had ill-treated- her the previous Wednesday. Her husband threw, his tea on the fire, hit her, and 'told her to clear out. He al,so smashed a number of ornaments and crockery. Ever since they, had been married her husband had ill-treated lir. He had It fiery temper. Accused said he hit his wife on the hand; "ii was only a tap." Detective Turner said he was called to the house and found a large ouatitity of ornameEs and crockery strewn about. He arrested accused on Saturday-, 'hen he had, prepared to go to Bolton with one of the children. This the accused denied. •</p>
        <p>Complainant also, applied N1...a separation order. She thought it would do him good. She was afraid he would do any injury to her and the children. The Chief Constable Said accused had threatened 14s wife "times •galore."., . ..</p>
        <p>The Court granted complainant a separation order, 'With the custody of the two 'children, accused to pay 12s. per week alimony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>FANTASIIC CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>FANTASIIC CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>Magistrates' Reply To Conscientious Objector.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Tues- day, before Mr. J. Rymer Young. (in the chair) and. Mr. J. Bailey, Ralph Herbert ClatwOrthy (23), a single loan, redding ,a ".c. Tower House, 'Sankey, and einployed- by MeSsts. Jos. CiesOld and Sons, Ltd., Airas charged with .failing to• report himself under the Military Service Act. - Mr. A. Hogson, military replesentative, said that defendant, who was a consientious objector, appealed before the Warring- ton Rural District .Tribunal,' 4nd was allotted to non-combatant service. Later he appeared before the West Derby Appeal Tribunal Liverpoot by whom he was ordered combatant service. • Accordingly, he was sum- moned fo report\ himself on the 16th May, at the Police Station, but failed to do so. .</p>
        <p>Asked if he had anything to say,- defendant replied, "I claim that this Curt has no right to try me," whereupon tho Chairman remarked, "We ham nothing to do with your fantastic claims." • lie was fined 405., to be deducted from his Pay, and handed dyer to the military authori- ties. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>MIS:SING BLOCK TIN.</head>
        <p>MIS:SING BLOCK TIN.</p>
        <p>, Employe Caitght In The Act Of . Stealing. Seen in the lact of stealing a quantity of block tin from the Florence Mills, Howley, where he was employed, Henly Holcroft, 1, Navigation-street, was fined 40s. at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday for stealing 711 b. of the metal, valte 14s. The Chief Constable stated that for a con- siderable time the company had been missing block tin from their wdrks. As a result, obser- Vation was kept, and shortly before one o'clock on Friday, prisoner was seen to pick up the tin and put it into his pocket. He was 'appre- hended when leaving the _works, and Was 'taken to the office and searched, the tin being found ppon him.,</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable added that the firm had suffered considerably from these thefts. In announcing the decision of the magistrates. the Chairman ,(Councillor H. Roberts)- said they were taking a lenient view of the case owing to prisoner being a.crippre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>AN ABSOLUTE WASTE.</head>
        <p>AN ABSOLUTE WASTE.</p>
        <p>For a contravention of the lighting regula- tions, 'Mr. Thos., 'Sangster, licensee of..the 'Royal Oak Hotel, Bridge-street, was fined 10s. at' the Borough Police Court on Tuesday. , • . The pollee evidence showed that a light shone from a room on defendant's premises, throWing a reflection on the building opposite. Mr. Sangster said he was not aware the light was on; indeed, it ..was an absolute waste, for the room had not been occupied, for three weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>MINUS LIGHTS.</head>
        <p>MINUS LIGHTS.</p>
        <p>For driving a cart without a front .white light, John Hall, Jockey-street, was fined 10s. at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, while Arthur Knowles, 71, Wash-lane, who rode a bicycle with- out a front white light and a red rear light, was fined ss. in one case,-and 2s. 6d. in the other. The Chief Cons)able stated that defendant would not have been summoned at all but for the fact that when about to be spoken to by a policeman about the offences he mounted his 'machine and rode off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>AT AVORK AGAIN.</head>
        <p>AT AVORK AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Lazy Man Has I,Varders As Foremen.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police 'Court on Wednesday, GeOrge Morrison, 1, Arnold's-yard; was charged with neglecting, his five children. Mr. Arthur Browne, prosecuting, said the de- fendant, who was a labourerf had been married seventeen years. ,He used to work at a tannery in .Merser,streot, but, in consequence of losing time, was' dismissed. • He thela:•got work at Grappenhall; but Was. again :stopped for' losing time. He then went to another tannery, and was again dismissed, his wife having to go into the Union.. He proceeded to a place at 'Win- wick, where he received 225. per week and house free, ,but again lost, time, and there was no food or fire' in the house. .Morrison- threatened his wife as to what he would do to her if she fold the- inspector of the conditions. -He then got work at a tannery, and again began to lose time; and men were sent from the. works to try tp get him to go back again. A fortnight last Monday a man came to the house and went, out with Morrison. The latter Name home drunk and said, "I have given notice and chucked it." This proved correct. During the past two or three weeks he had stopped in bed for two and three days at a time, and on Monday of this week he never went to work at all.</p>
        <p>Mr. Laws and Inspector Wainwright had given the family food, Stc., the children all being delicate. Defendant. had been, sentenced‘ to three months' imprisonment: at Runcorn some years ago for a similar offence. , Morrison asked for a chance, promising to sign the pledge and never touch drink'again. Sentence: Three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>FORBIDDEN FRUIT.</head>
        <p>FORBIDDEN FRUIT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Juvenile Police Court on Wednesda./, three - boys were charged with stealing six orarges from a stall in the Market, the. property of Mr. Arthur Holland. The Chief -Constable said that on Sunday a police officer saw the boys tampering with .a stall in the ,Market, and, on appreaching them, they ran away.. lie caught one, and on scotch- ing him found six oranges in his possession. Subsequently; he saw the Other- boys,: and they admitted the offence. 'One of the boy's was 'ordered to receive six Strokes of, the birch, rod, and the other two fined ss. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MOTHER-IN-LAW ASSAULTED.</head>
        <p>MOTHER-IN-LAW ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>Six Months' Hard Labour For An Attack With A Poker.</p>
        <p>James % Harris, labourer, of no fixed abode, was, at the Borough Court on Wednesday, sent to prison for six months on a charge of an aggravated assault upon his mother-in-law, Hannah Baker, of 11, Beswick-street, on the 24th of January last. He.was also charged with assaulting P.C. Harrison. The Chief Constable, pro4ecuting, said that in the early hours of the morning of the 24th of January last Mrs. Baker's daughter (prisoner's wife) rushed into her house and made a com- plaint respecting prisoner. Mrs. Baker -tent to</p>
        <p>see prisoner, who lived two or three doors away, and asked him what he had been doing. Prisoner armed himseV with a heavy poker and attempted to strike Mrs. Baker across the head with . it, but she shielded herself by putting up her arm, • which was fractured- as 'a result •of the blow. She FaS taken to the Infirmary and had been an out-patient till the 19th inst. The - fracture had • healed up. A warrant was taken out against prisoner, but he had managed to escape the 'police until Wed- nesday of last , week, when he was arrested by P.C. Harrison, who .found him sleeping in a pig-sty at Bewse3% had been, wandering about the immediate vicinity of Warrington, and sleeping in eathouses since the day of the alleged assault. Mrs. Baker. corroborated, and Mary Alice Lea, Fox-street, said if Mrs. Baker had not warded off the blow with her arm she would have been killed.,</p>
        <p>Dr. Cruickshank, senior house surgeon at the Infirmary, gave evidence as to the woman's injurivs. A/Suitable Sleeping-Place.</p>
        <p>: P.C. -Harrison .spoke to finding prisoner sleep- ing in a pig-sty on Wednesday last. On the way to the Police Station prisoner became vroknt and .butted him in the stomach, kicked .him about the legs, and bit his thumb. He had to put the 'handcuffs on , him. Accused said Mrs. Baker's family challenged him out to fight. His mother-in-law struck;him With a scraper, and he retaliated. Accused's wife said her brother and her mother were the aggressors. In reply to the Chief Constable, she admitted that she had been sleeping .out with her hus- band. .</p>
        <p>In sentencing prisoner the magistrates ad- vised accused to control hisAemper. •</p>
        <p>SPECIALS' ROUGH TIME.</p>
        <p>Labourer Sentenced To Six Months On</p>
        <p>Four Charges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>SUNDAY EIVE:.R. SCENE.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY EIVE:.R. SCENE.</p>
        <p>Peter Scully, labourer, of no fixed abode, at the Borough Court on Thursday was sent to. prison for six months oA charges of gaming, 'using obscene language, indecent -exposure, and assaulting Special Constable Wilding. - • Mr.' Wilding stated that on Sunday last he found accused swimming about in the river in a naked condition. when -he 'called- him out accused said he would kill every special con- stable .in the borough. ,On getting to the river •bank he threatened to' throw' witness. and Special Constable Rogers, who was 'with him, into the river. Accused struck. Mr. , Rogers a blow on thejace. He attempted to strike wit- ness; but he avoided him. Witness telephoned for the regular police, and when accused saw them coming he rushed at witness, struck him, and they closed. Witness defended himself with his cane.</p>
        <p>, Mr. 'Rogers said on the way to the Police Station accused behaved like a madman, and used most vile language. Defective-Sergeant Reddington gave• evidence to prove the charge of gaming. Accused said he had tried to keep straiight for two years, but the pollee were always tracking_ him down.,</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said accused had been before the Court 48 times previously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>GAMING RAID.</head>
        <p>GAMING RAID.</p>
        <p>Borough PoHc And Special Constables'</p>
        <p>Big</p>
        <p>Complaints having been made respecting the prevalence of gaming, on the river .banks and in fields in the vicinity of the town, members of the Borough Force and special constables made a concerted raid on Saturday and Sunday, -With the ressult. that upwards of, forty Youths and, men, some of , :Whom: were elderly and married, Made their appearance at the Borough. 'Court on Thursday, charged with gaming with cards and playing pitch-and-toss. Fines rang- ing from 20s. to' ss. were imposed' Upon the • defendants. , •</p>
        <p>Several men failed to appear and the., Chief Constable said he had • received •a' letter frOM the management of a controlled firm 'asking that the men 'should be excused attendance, as their absence from work would mean the stop- page of .important jobs. . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>SCOTTISII VISITOR.</head>
        <p>SCOTTISII VISITOR.</p>
        <p>Charged With Failing- To Produce Registration Card.</p>
        <p>A Scotsman named John Drummond 'appeared at the BorOugh Court on Thursday charged with failing to produce his registration card on 'the 24th inst. ' '</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable ,said the accused arrived in Warrington on Monday last, having traraped from Manchester. He stayed \at a house in the town, but did not fill up a form. Accused. visited .4 local public-house and got into conversation with a puddler employed, at Monks, Hall and Company. He asked this man a number of questions, which were con- sidered, rightly or wrongly, to be of a suspi- cious . character: He inquired if they were engaged making munitions, Szc. The matter was reported: tó the management of thp firm, and subsequently to himself (the Chief Con- stable) • On Wednesday night accused turned up out- side the works and he was kept in conversation till the arrival of Detective-Sergeant Redding- ton. The officer asked him if he had a regis- tration card and he. replied that he had not. •ACcused was Ei Mari of. sound intelligence, and he (the Chief Constable) could not underSta,nd" why he had failed to register. He asked that accused should be-t emanded for a week ..to enable inquiries to be iudde.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Denied, Asking Suspicious Cluestipps.</head>
        <p>Denied, Asking Suspicious Cluestipps.</p>
        <p>. . . , Acmied. said he. was a Scotsman:. h`red t4lnd born. .lie had .been working at Manchester and had b' in iveeipt.of compensation kr injuries he had received while at work there. He was not aware he had .to register, and. he had not wilfully violated the Defence•of the Realm Act. He had provided the police with informatiri. respiecting himself, and he; ol3jected to being kept in custody eeten days. He denied that he.. asked any quostions of the puddler rerf- ing. Munitions. 'Accused was remanded in custody for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>i;2O FINE.</head>
        <p>A i;2O FINE.</p>
        <p>With many convictions against him, and With one for a similar offence, William \Saunders, alias George White, of no settled abode, was iined£2o; or,. in default, two Months' imprison- by the Borough ,Magistrates yesterday, kr travelling froth Euston .to Warrington without having .paid his fate, and with intent to avoid payment. . _ _</p>
        <p>Ticket-conector Blinston proved the charge,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>WOMEN WAR WORKERS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WAR WORKERS.</p>
        <p>, During the. last twenty months or so of war things li'avp Changed so completely for everyone that E. seems as if. a, new world has come into being and that old modes of life were. no. longer possible: During these crowded months differ- ent things have appealed to different women as being of permanent importance, and the June issue .of "The Woman at Home • and Girls' Realm" gives some viqws of the leading women war-workers, including the Marchioness of Londonderry, Miss Swift, matron of the British Red Cross Society and the Order of Bt. John; Mrs. Henry Fawcett, LL.D.; Miss A. 13: F. Horniman, the witty and genial-lady who con- trols • the Gaiety Theatre, Manchester; and several others • who have .been alive to these Changes. Fiction in this issue is powerfully represented, and includes a long complete romance in the hest style of R. S. Warren Bell, and some furthes splendid stories by. Gertrude Aram .Annie S. Swan, J. E.' Buckrose, Cal Ewald, and others. Among the items of general interest to womenfolk the "Woman at Home and Girls' Realm" deals adequately with seasonable tmillinery, emphasising " Chic " beaded hats, home .dressmaking, and home cookery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>RUGBY NEXT SEASON P</head>
        <p>RUGBY NEXT SEASON P</p>
        <p>War rgtc Club Conzia'eling P!.o3pect3.</p>
        <p>LAST YEAR'S LOSS OF £324 EXPLAINED.</p>
        <p>Theugli in a m'Aified way, there is every prospect of fouC;aii being'played in the Wilders- pool enclosure next season. Last year the Warrington Rugby Football Club sustained a loss of 5:321 12s. 2d. These facts were broached at the annual meeting of the club, held in the Pavilion on Monday evening. The place of meeting easily accommodated those who put in an appearance. Those present heartily received the Chairman's (Mr. E. Ohorne) announcement of the possi- bility. of Rugby being played. They were the lindens Of 'enthusiasm.: , _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Pemberton Submitted the accounts, Which showed' that while the receipts had been £l2B 13s. 10d., the 'contra account showed £453 ss. These included £SO paid .to the' officials for the season 1914-15, £63 interest on— mortgage, £BO ground expenses, £7O rents, rates. and taxes, and £llB depreciation. £324 seems a considerable ?Mount to lose during a year of suspension of football activity, but we must take into con- sideration the fiet that the honoraria granted at the annual meeting last May were for ser- vices rcndered in the season 1914-15, and we Must not 'forget that the depreciation :is rather extensive. The enormity of the figures are in that way easily understood. The worth of the land, buildings, S:c.,`: is £3,118, and there is a balance of .assetS over liabilities of £1,470.</p>
        <p>I Considering all things, said the Chair Man, they 'could congratulate therriSelVes that the' loss was small, for the standing charges on a ground ,such, as their's were very Considerable. He thought they might say they had done fairly well: Of course, the one thing they were particularly keen about a this time was to save - and conserve as much as they possibly could, so that v•-lier. they came back, to the lime When they could have first-class. football, they would have sufficient cash to resume under sothewhat .abnormal conditions. That was one reason why members of the club ,should rally round and send in their subscriptions. In the 1914-15 season there were 116 honorary 'members, and out of these' only 59 had paid their sub- scriptions at the reduced.figure of 7s. 6d., which was half of the previous one. Whereas in 1915 they had 772 ordinary members, only 325 had paid the redueed subscriptions of 3s. Of course, a good many had joined the forces, and they were doing far better work elsewhere, but those who were-left should come forward, aS a matter of honour, and Pay the 'reduced suly.,.criptions,l at any rate., Regarding the, financial state- ment, he added_that when they compared it to the previous year, \ they had nothing to be ashamed of. They had cut down ex.nenses as low as possible. Next' year, if they had little football, they would be able to save on the ground, for they would not wind so much money. The treasurer had. presented a very_ good statement, and the committee had all done their work admirably. - The financial report Was adopted.. , -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>NEXT SEASON'S PROSPECTS.</head>
        <p>NEXT SEASON'S PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>• Regarding the resumption of football, the Chairman said that that was on the "knees of the gods." , They had to be guided • very largely by what took place in the next few months, kind what might take place at, the de- ferred annual meeting of the Northern Union, to be held on the Ist August. It was considered that by that time all the clubs would have an idea of the 'advisability or not of playing a modified form of football. As far as Warring- ton was concerned, he reiterated what he said at the last annual meeting, that during war time, at any rate, they should not be a party' to pro- fessional ;football. (Applause.) At the same time, they were, not going to place themselves in the position of the "(log in the manger." They wanted to do all they could to help forward the game, and keep, it alive, and .if some ukodified scheme were put forward, they, would be only too. pleased to 'fall into line ,and. do what they Could. By Au4g,ust Ist they .Might have somAdea of what -1110 Y. were prepared to 'do. The 'diffi- culty,: of course, was in 'regard to players:., It, haa been felt very largely in the Intermediate. League. and Several clubs had had to fall out, because they could not find players. .Yet those. who, had. remained had 'produced .a 'ferin Of fOOt ball which :had been the means of keeping to- gether a number of good players who would be a great help 'in maintaining football at a high standard later bn. He (Mit Osborne) had recently seen. Mr. J.• H. Smith, who told' him that ke had 'been particularly struck by the way the Warringthn 'and Widnes' Intermediate Clubs had played, and .dded that Warrington should try to emulate Widnes and do all they could to get local players.. He (Mr. Osborne) was in hearty agreement with that sentiment. (Hear, hear.) • Foothaii No Hinr!ra.lce to Recruiting.</p>
        <p>With regard to playing football next season, Mr. Osborne said that the conditions were not the‘sarne as last year, for by then compulsion would .be in force and all those who were not in the Army • would %. either be engaged on nrunition work or otherwise exempted from ser- vice, and, therefore, if they did resume foot- ball it could not be said by anyone or felt by themselves that they were hindering recruiting; in fact, be thought it would be. in the interest of the workers if some attractive football were played on Saturdair afternoons, for then men would be able to return to their Work with renewed energy and vigour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>OFFICERS RE-ELECTED.</head>
        <p>OFFICERS RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr; Edmund Osborne was re-elected president, Mr. T. P.. Bradshaw, hon. secretary, and Mr. T. Pemberton, hon. treasurer. Mr. Mason re- signed from the committee, and Mr. J. H. Witham WAS elected in his,pla6e, along with thc3 folloWing, who were, re-elected: Messrs.. F. Barber, T. Bunch, T. Barnes, J. T. Sampson, W. Hill, J. Winstanley, and J. F. Forster. MessrS. W. Webster and George Gillen were thanked for their services as auditars-,, and the President 'Praised all' the Officers for giv,il34 freely , their aid to the club in this time. , • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>WORKMEN FINED.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN FINED.</p>
        <p>a;\Vehd A Bflhiard Match Instead Of</p>
        <p>I,Vorking.</p>
        <p>In prosecuting a charge against three young workmen for absenting themselves from work, a works manager at a controlled establishment, told. the Warrington Munitions Court .on Mon- day that it had been reported to him that the men had spent most of the time in a public- house • watching a • billiards match. " It was alleged against the men ,that on the night of the 15th that they stayed', away from work, causing a loss of 45 10s. td the othq members of their squad, and about £l2 to the company The men went to supper and were an hour late in resuming work, causing a stoppage of one of ,the /mills. Having waited for the defendants sometime, the other men of the squad gradually left Work. • The defendants were aware that the work could not be proceeded with without them.</p>
        <p>Defendants stated that there were not sufficient men present when they returned to carry•on the mill. _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. J. Ridgway (the chairman) said the ,Court was satisfied that it was a serious case: If the defendants did not consider the compolly they, should have..cons.idercd; the iiy• tefests ,of their mates. Defendants Were 'fined' ssl, and the Chairman added • that understood that if another ease similar to their 3 carne before the Court a heavier fine would be imposed. Mr. W. P. 'Rylands and Mr. Frank Berry acted as assessors for the employers and the men respectively.</p>
        <p>The Irish P.C. P.C. O'Leary: " More on there and don't be obstructing the thoroughfare • Interested Spectator: " Vrot 'arm am I doin' of?" _</p>
        <p>P.C. O'LearY "Sure if every wan was to stand in the wan place, how would the rest go by ?" Punch." '</p>
        <p>Just Like a Woman. "Tommy, I'm not at all pleased with the re- port your mother gives dor." me of your conduct to- ; "I knew you Wouldn't be. an' I told her so. But she inane the report. all file same. Just like a wowap, a!n't it, father?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>I OWLS.</head>
        <p>I OWLS.</p>
        <p>A rare struggle was seen at Wigan on Satin.- day, when two Warrington teams, Rylands's and Cemetery Hotel, met hi the second round of the Lancashire County Amateur Association ,Cham- pionship. Rylands's were forced to bow the knee by eight chalks.</p>
        <p>On the first four games .Rylaii&amp;'.3 • were thirteen ahead. J. Gleave was "all over " J. tEhton, and allowed him fo score but two, while Cottam also played splendidly, .antl accounted for A. Forster 21-8. W. Roughley.-aild S. Massey' went down to W. Massey and G. Ball, jun., by 8—,21 and 15-21 respectively •Although T. Humphreys did not play his usual game, he accounted for R. Antrbbus by 21-17.</p>
        <p>• G. Monks is in grand form just at presenc, and easily accounted for J. Plant by 21-9. 'These two players peeled at eight, but so well did Monks afterwards bowl that the Cemetery man could only add- another one. J. Woods was only beaten .20-21-by, J. Bibby, but H. Haslara, against R. Warburton, went down 7-21, and T. Platt against F. Dale, 11-21.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of the gal:oe,, I. Newton and T. Clare (captain) tried their utmost to pull the game out of the fire, but they could not do -7,0. Newton going down 17-21, and Clare 16-21. The Cemetery thus won amidst great enthusi- asm..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>RYLANDS'&amp;v. CEMETERY.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'&amp;v. CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>The individual scores in ,the Rylands's v. Cemetery Match were:—J. Gleave (Rylands's)•2l, J. Ashton 2; W. Cottam 21, A. Forster 8; W. Roughley 8, W. Massey 21; S: Massey .15, G. Ball,. inn., 21;. T. -Platt 14, F. Dale 21; T. Humphreys 21, R. Antrobus 17; G. Monks 21, J. Plant 9; R. ;Tilling 15, C. Smith 21; J. Woods 20; J. Bibby,. 21; H. Haslam 7, R. Warburton 21; L. Newton,l7, W. Vatterworth 21; T. Clara (captain)-.16,.C. Jordan 21.</p>
      </div>
