        <p>APPRENTICE Wanted to the plumbing trade.—Apply Edward de Prez, Moore, near Warrington. BAKER and CONFECTIONER Wanted, male or female; good wages for reliable person.— Peake, Baker, Nantwich. CAR—T-ER and SALESMAN (good) Wanted; one well used to fruit and potato business; no oible man for the Army need apply; must be trustworthy.—Latham, 26, Earle-street, Crewe. CGERGYMAN'S Daughter, 20, Requires post in school or private family. as Governess or Companion-Help; capable; specialist in ,drawing, painting, dancing.—Cordon, 24.' Merridale-road, Wolverhampton.</p>
        <p>TNDER-BARMAN Wanted.—Apply Crown Hotel, Colne, Lancashire. yotrNG LADY Wanted for Grocer's Office; state references and salary required.—Box 25, " Examiner" Office, Warrington.</p>
        <p>MEN and WOMEN Wanted to study 2,000 Electrical Engineering by Hawkins 's Guide Method. The cheapest and most perfect system known. Total cost only Two pounds Ten shillings, in instalments of Five shillings per month.' Grand opportunity.—Edwin A. Mansfield and Co.. Electrical Engineers. New Brighton.</p>
        <p>Shops, Businesses, Sze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>Grand</head>
        <p>Grand</p>
        <p>. ,i,.....,"iii,.., ;' '.. • . '. '. .'. ... ' ".• ..' 1 . ! ,*: ~+. I;.(ty'P ; , . •r„f. • ii., . . s IPt. ‘‘) ..i.l. 41' ./..• '''' , . 4. lit 1.44,.. ,ixt. • . , ';'; 't ..4st. ~.4( 1+.4 • • '''' 'dt,', '</p>
        <p>_ toria-road, Widnes; -, „ sheet, St. Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the EXAMINER General Printing Work:=, Dewe3--Areet, Warrington; also Published bY them at the ExikiNEß Offices, 2, vie 3 GeorTe-- aster; and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the County of Chester. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earketown, all in the County of Lan,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>EXAMINERS SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINERS SATURDAY, MAY 27, 1916.</p>
        <p>THE TRIBUNALS.</p>
        <p>MARRIED MEN APPEAL FOR EXEMPTION.</p>
        <p>Business And Domestic Reasons Receive Considerate Treatment.</p>
        <p>The Borough Tribunal on Tuesday morning was engaged for three hours in hearing appeals by married men wanting exemption on business and domestic grounds. Dr. G. W. Joseph pre- sided. Two appeals were heard in private, and the result was not announced. The decision in two appeals was adjourned, and in the re- mainder conditional and temporary exemption was given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>NOT FIT FOR A WOMAN.</head>
        <p>NOT FIT FOR A WOMAN.</p>
        <p>A married man, aged 36, with three children, who carries on business as a hardware dealer and general furnishing business, appealed. He said his business entaiTed a good deal of labour, as he had to go out and buy second-hand furni- ture and do it up to make it saleable. His wife assisted him with the business, but he did not think the removing of furniture was a fit job for a woman.—He was given exemption till August 31st, and was asked to join the Volunteer Corps.</p>
        <p>PUBLICAN'S TEMPORARY EXEMPTION. A publican, aged 32, married, with two chil- dren, appealed on business grounds. The Chairman pointed out that the hours of public-houses were shorter now, but appellant said although this was the case, he had to get through the business in the best way possible.-- Granted exemption till August 31st. Mr. Bradbury said such a healthy athlete-like appellant should join the Volunteer Corps. Appellant said he would attach himself to the Corps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>ONLY 4FT. 11IN.</head>
        <p>ONLY 4FT. 11IN.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Ackers, Whitley and Co., coal mer- chants, appealed for A carter, married, aged 36, with four children. The . firm stated that the man was engaged as a bagger, in addition to which he had a coal round, which he was main- taining for a man who had joined the National Reserves, and who had fought in the Boer War. They did not think the man was fit for Army service; he was only 4ft. llin. with his clogs on. The application was adjourned to enable thc.! man to be medically examined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>BUILDING SOCIETY SECRETARY EXEMPTED.</head>
        <p>BUILDING SOCIETY SECRETARY EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>An appeal on business grounds was made by an auctioneer, estate agent and valuer. The business was started 13 years ago. He took con- trol three years ago, on the death of the former principal. He had only one man in his employ; at the Commencement of the war his staff con- sisted of five men of military age. The bulk of his business was held on personal grounds, and would not be retained, if he had to join the Army. He acted as secretary to a local build- ing society and a limited company.—Conditional exemption was given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>SUPPLIED WORKPEOPLE'S DINNERS.</head>
        <p>SUPPLIED WORKPEOPLE'S DINNERS.</p>
        <p>A tripe, fish and chip potato dealer, married, (35), with. two children, was given exemption till August 31st. He said he supplied a large number of dinners to workpeople.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>"BEST APPEAL YET."</head>
        <p>"BEST APPEAL YET."</p>
        <p>A cinema hall manager, who had put in an aPpeal, now expressed a desire to withdraw it. He said he had joined the Navy as•a signaller, and an undertaking had beeu given that he would not be called up till July 18th. He held an Army certificate for signalling. Dr. Joseph That is the best appeal we have heard yet. We wish you success, and hope you will come back a Lord High Admiral, or some- thing of the sort. • The Tribunal agreed to the appeal being with- drawn. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>INQUIRIES TO BE MADE.</head>
        <p>INQUIRIES TO BE MADE.</p>
        <p>Messrs. W. Almond and Co., tripe dressers, appealed on behalf of a works manager, aged 36, married, and two married oil refiners, aged 33 and 38 respectively In reply to Captain Mathams, the repre- sentative said there was no possible means of men being supplied from the headquarters at Blackburn. They were short-handed at Black- burn, and boys were chiefly employed. it would be prohibitive to send perishable goods from that town to Warrington. The Tribunal adjourned the appeals for a fort- night to enable inquiries to be made as to the conditions prevailing at Blackburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>BOOTS FOR SOLDIERS' FAMILIES.</head>
        <p>BOOTS FOR SOLDIERS' FAMILIES.</p>
        <p>A master hoot and shoe repairer, aged 30, married, with two children, said his only assistant was his father, aged 60, who could not do a great deal of work owing' to infirmity. Most of his work was for soldiers' families. The Clerk: how do you know that? Appellant: Well, how do I know it? Most of the men our way are soldiers.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: They don't stamp it on the soles of their boOts, do they? .(Laughter.l Exemption till the end of August was given.</p>
        <p>WAR-TIME FRUIT SHOP.</p>
        <p>fruit and vegetPble dealer was given till August 31st. He said he had two shops, one of which he had had eighteen months.</p>
        <p>ThP Clerk: Wasn't it a risk to open a shop in war-time? Appellant said his father-in-law was looking after that shop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>TRIBUNAL'S FINAL.</head>
        <p>TRIBUNAL'S FINAL.</p>
        <p>An insurance agent (35), married, with four children, said he had tried to get someone to take over his book, but had failed. He asked for three months' exemption to enable him to look round. He was given till July 31st, and was informed that the decision of the Tribunal was final.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>POSTING MANAGER EXEMPTED.</head>
        <p>POSTING MANAGER EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Maginn, cab proprietor, appealed for his general manager-clerk, aged 36, married, with two children. Mr. Maginn said he was also contractor for his Majesty's mails. He had failed to get suitable men to drive.— Exemption till the end of July was given,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>LADY WINDOW CLEANERS.</head>
        <p>LADY WINDOW CLEANERS.</p>
        <p>Will They Be A Success in Warrington? When a window cleaner and carpet beater applied for exemption before the Tribunal at the, Town Hall on Thursday, he was asked whether ladies were not proving good substi- tutes for men. • Applicant: From my experience, I am afraid they won't be much of a success. The Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway): Some of them were in trousers the other day and looked very nice. They were cleaning the windows of the Town Hall. Applicant: I don't think it is the sort of work for a woman. Exempted until 30th June.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>BUSY COFFIN-MAKERS.</head>
        <p>BUSY COFFIN-MAKERS.</p>
        <p>In applying for a son, a coffin-maker men- tioned that it was work which men were loth to do, as it was not pleasant. In the last seven- teen months they had Made 570 coffins. The case was adjourned for a week, when the appeal of another son will be heard. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the applicant.</p>
        <p>HELPS THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Mainwaring applied for his manag- ing clerk, remarking flat besides having an ex- tensive business as an auctioneer and valuer, he held certain .appointments under the Board of Agriculture. One of the duties Was to send a monthly return regarding the crops in an area including over 200 townships. Some of this expert work was done by the clerk. Mr. Main-</p>
        <p>waring also spoke of the depletion his staff had undergone. His managing clerk was absolutely essential to the business. Conditional exemption.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>FOR DUBLIN BUT NOT BERLIN.</head>
        <p>FOR DUBLIN BUT NOT BERLIN.</p>
        <p>The Wigan Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. appealed for their Warrington agent and wharf manager, 34 years of age and married. In reply to the Chairman, the agent said he had only been passed for home service because of his eyes. When he was at Chester Castle they said to him that he was not fit for Berlin, but only for Dublin.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Is that what they marked on your .paper? (Laughter.) The Deputy-Mayor: You could see a Sein Feiner, but not a German. (Renewed laughter.) Conditional exemption'.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S STAFF GREATLY DEPLETgD.</p>
        <p>•-- - - - Mr. T. Leslie, secretary of Messrs. Rylands Bros., appealed for five clerks. Prior to the war, lie said, they had an officelstaff of fifty, and of these 38 were of military age. They had lost 18 through enlisting, and had been obliged to fill their places as far as possible with females. The manufactures they were engaged upon had considerably increased their work, thus necessi- tating the retention of a number of experienced workers who had not been " starred " as indis- pensable. With one exception, and he was medically unfit, all the five had agreed to join the Volunteer Training Corps. Conditional exemption was granted to three of the clerks, these being married, and the other two were exempted until the end of Jdne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>MANAGER FOR HIS FATHER.</head>
        <p>MANAGER FOR HIS FATHER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Andrew Rathbone, iron and steel mer- chant, gained conditional exemption for his son, who manages his business.</p>
        <p>PROVISIONS.</p>
        <p>A provision dealer, with a business of his own in Market-place, was granted conditional exemp- tion. He is married, but has no children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>MOSTLY BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>MOSTLY BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Finding Out The indispensables</p>
        <p>In Local Firms.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph presided at yesterday's sitting of the Borough Tribunal, when a number of appeals, chiefly on business grounds, were re- * viewed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>ONE LEFT OUT OF THREE.</head>
        <p>ONE LEFT OUT OF THREE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Porshaw appeared in support of appeals put in by two brothers, both married, who were partners in a wholesale and retail fish business. Mr. Forshaw said another brother, also a part- ner, had joined the Army. One brother, who had no family had now agreed to withdraw his appeal and join. The Tribunal granted conditional exemption to the remaining brother. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>TEMPORARY EXEMPTION.</head>
        <p>TEMPORARY EXEMPTION.</p>
        <p>A motor cycle and cycle agent and repairer, aged 30, married, with three children, appealed. He was represented by Mr. Steel. He purchased the business, twelve months ago. Exempted, until August 31st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>KINEMA APPEAL.</head>
        <p>A KINEMA APPEAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Connor, proprietor of' the Star 'Cinema, appealed on behalf of an engineman-electrician. Mrs. Lightburn informed the Tribunal that the manager was called up at the outbreak of war. Three other employes had joined, and another man was called up for Monday. At present there were fourteen employes, including one wounded soldier and two pensioners. The rest were females. Application had been made to a number of big firms for an operator, but they were unable to supply one. Exempted until August 31st.</p>
        <p>GROCERS CONDITIONALLY EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>c,,,rs. J. Richardson and Sons, wholesale grocers, appealed on behalf of their foreman, a married man, • and he was given conditional exemption. A junior partner in the firm was given conditional exemption. The senior part- ner said they had 28 employes left, which in- cluded nine females. Only two men had been certified as fit for general service. Five had already joined and another was called up. Similar treatment was given a shoemaker and clogger, aged 32, married, with four children. Messrs. Houghton, wheelwrights and black- smiths, appealed for the only tradesman they had left. They had been advertising many months for workmen, but had failed to get replies.—Conditionally exempted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>R.S:P.C.A. CLAIM FOR INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>R.S:P.C.A. CLAIM FOR INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel, representing the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to. Animals, appealed on behalf of the local inspector. Mr. Steel explained that the society had lost 120 out of 220 inspectors, and those who re- mained worked two districts. The society sug- gested to the Tribunal that the work done by their inspectors was of national importance. The Board of Agriculture had issued regulations for the preservation of live stock, which the inspectors of the society carried out. If more inspectors were taken away it would be impos- sible to replace them,' as they were trained in veterinary work. Mr. Steel added that the local inspector was not shirking his duty; the appeal had been put in by the society.—Conditionally exempted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>LOOMS STANDING.</head>
        <p>LOOMS STANDING.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Silcock appealed on behalf of a fire- man, Mr. W. B. Forshaw representing the firm. It was stated that six men out of 25 had joined the colours. They had been unable to replace them. They had five looms standing out of ten, owing to having no men to work them.—Conditionally exempted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>INSURANCE AGENT GIVEN TIME</head>
        <p>INSURANCE AGENT GIVEN TIME</p>
        <p>An insurance agent, aged 33, married, asketi for two months' exemption to enable him to train a man to look after his book.—The Tribunal agreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>ACCOUNTANT'S APPEAL.</head>
        <p>ACCOUNTANT'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. L. A. Voisey, chartered accountant, ap- pealed on behalf of a chartered accountant, aged 36, and married. Mr. Voisey said it would be impossible at the present time to fill the man's place. He had half-yearly audits coming</p>
        <p>on, and it would be a great inconvenience if he were taken. He had three other employes of military age, but two of them had been described as medically unfit. He thought the man could bo relieved by the end of July.—The Tribunal agreed to exempt him until then.</p>
        <p>"THE CASE OF CHARLES DUKES."</p>
        <p>Question in House Of Commons In the House Of Commons on Wednesday, Mr. Snowden asked the President of the Local Gov- ernment Board if he could obtain a hearing before the Central Tribunal of the case of Charles Dukes, of Warrington, district organiser for the Gasworkers and General Labourers' Union, secretary of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Trades Councils, secretary of the Warrington Trades Council, member of the War- rington Munitions Tribunal, member of the Advisory Committee, of the Labour Exchanges (Liverpool district), his appeals to the local and county tribunals having failed, and as hardship was likely to result to the workmen whose in- terests are in the hands of Mr. Dukes, whether he would have this case referred to the Central Tribunal in order' to have an authoritative deci- ismiopnorotnancae with the matterit whic Was likely to be of calling up for military ser- vice of married men.</p>
        <p>Mr. Long said he was making inquiries into this case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>• . . , . . . . . . .. • „ . , . .., ~..; 8 3 ~ " „... 1 1 . . ~...,. , ni.....„.....,.....;.zws ' ~,,-, . , . 'i; , :".' C 4 4-; 'c' ',. II•th 1 fik., `j,'' ‘ii,' . , : , .i. . e ~ ~ . ..t; ..!, 411. . ..., ,</head>
        <p>• . . , . . . . . . .. • „ . , . .., ~..; 8 3 ~ " „... 1 1 . . ~...,. , ni.....„.....,.....;.zws ' ~,,-, . , . 'i; , :".' C 4 4-; 'c' ',. II•th 1 fik., `j,'' ‘ii,' . , : , .i. . e ~ ~ . ..t; ..!, 411. . ..., ,</p>
        <p>EX-PUBLIC OFFICIAL</p>
        <p>Who Became Farm Manager</p>
        <p>And Teamsman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>FATHER QUESTIONED.</head>
        <p>FATHER QUESTIONED.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furness, the military representative, at Wednesday's sitting of the Warrington Dis- trict Tribunal, put some pointed questions to a Newton-le-Willows farmer, who appealed for his son, aged 27 years, described as a farm manager and teamsman, who, previous to January last was a public official. Mr. Furness said that last January appellant's son was desirous of joining the Army. Efforts were made to get him a commission, without success.</p>
        <p>The appellant agreed that when his son wanted to join the Army he was a deputy. registrar.</p>
        <p>Furness said a good deal of comment had been occasioned in the district respecting appellant's son. He wanted to know the reason •for the man leaving his position as deputy- registrar to go on the farm, and if the con- ditions on the farm had altered considerably since January last. The appellant said the conditions had changed considera:bly. 'When he knew that his head man was to be called up he said his son would have to come to manage the farm affairs. He had lost several men since January, and he now had only his son and another man. Mr. Furness: You had a farm bailiff ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Is it a fact that_he_was discharged?—No.</p>
        <p>Appellant explained that his farm bailiff had been ill for six months. He was now back with him, but he was unfit for heavy work. Conditions had so altered, and he was so short- handed that if his son went away he (appellant) would have to give up the business he intended to do.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted one month's exemp- tion, and appellant expressed regret that he would have to come before the Tribunal again. A Member: You will be able to •get plenty of Irishmen in a month's time.</p>
        <p>Appellant: Irishmen cannot manage a farm. CATTLEMAN'S EXEMPTION UNTIL SEPTEMBER 30th.</p>
        <p>A Padgate farmer, appealing on behalf of his cattleman, said he had been trying to get men for weeks. He had advertised frequently, but had not got one application. Part of the man's duties was to drive cattle into the town. Aged men and women could not do this work. Cattle took some managing in a busy town. Exempted till end of September.</p>
        <p>VICTIM OF MURDEROUS ASSAULT. •</p>
        <p>A Fearnhead farmer, appealing for his son, aged 22 years, said he had full control of the farm. He (appellant) was unable to take an active part in the management as he was the victim of a murderous assault some years ago, and two men were sent to penal servitude for it. He had two sons in the Army and another likely to be called up. Exemption till end of September was given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>FARM EMPLOYES' EXEMPTIONS.</head>
        <p>FARM EMPLOYES' EXEMPTIONS.</p>
        <p>Several farm employes were given conditional and temporary exemption. In one case a farmer said he did darting for a District Council, and his son, for whom he appealed, assisted him. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said a man who did carting for a District Council was not indis- pensable to the farm. Another member declared that carting for a' District Council was not profitable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>KINEMAS.</head>
        <p>THE KINEMAS.</p>
        <p>Picture House.</p>
        <p>"His Real Character" and "A Desperate Leap" are the titles of two fine dramas which have been presented at the Picture House this week, the former during the first half and the latter during the second. Both were brimful of excitement.</p>
        <p>Next week "Sing of a Great City" for th first half, and "The Beast" for the latter half will be the "star" pictures.</p>
        <p>Star K i nema.</p>
        <p>There is now showing at the Star Kinema Henry Arthur Jones's fine play, " Masquer- adors." In the first part of. next week " The Voice of the Fog "—a Lasky film—will be shown, and in the second half, "The Foundling," where Miss Mary Pickford, said to be "the world's sweetheart," will shine.</p>
        <p>Special coming attractions are: Pauline Frederick in "Bella Donna"; and Hall Caine's masterpiece, The Christian," with Derwent Hall Caine as Father Storm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>WI'CLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>WI'CLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, pre- sided over by the Rev. T. T. James, who gave a welcome to Mr. J. H. J. Hampson, who gave the address upon "Fight the good fight." He said it was our supreme duty to fight for God, our country and our homes. He did not think and believe, tls some people did, that this was a war because of our national sins. Someone was to blame, and he laid that at the door of the Kaiser and his leaders. We were fighting for peace and righteousness, and should honour God by good lives, as well as being alert at all times for our country and looking after our homes. Mr. Yearsley and Mr. W. Beswick took part in the meeting. The President welcomed Mr. McConville, who was over on leave, being engaged in a transport section, and the Secretary referred to a message from. Mr. G. T. Yeates he had received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>Sabbath Services.</head>
        <p>Sabbath Services.</p>
        <p>The following services and preachers are announced for to-morrow :</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges WesleYan Sunday School anniversary services; morning service in chapel, preacher Rev. Thomas Waugh; evening servie° in Parr Hall. In the afternoon the Parr Hall will be used for a musical service.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Church, Legh-street, Choir Festival; morning, Rev. A. J. Chessuln; evening, Mr. J. Upson; afternoon, at 2.30, ren- dering of Stainer's cantata "Daughter 01 Jairus," by the choir; soloists, Mrs. C. Worthington, Mr. T. Goostrey and Mr. it' Dakin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Boys' Dangerous Experiment With</head>
        <p>Boys' Dangerous Experiment With</p>
        <p>Gunpowder.</p>
        <p>BARKER'S BIG BARGAINS.</p>
        <p>Curiosity led to a rather serious accident 011 Monday evening., with the result that two thirteen-year-old boy,—Jack Seed, of Padgate- lane, and Arthur Podmore, 4, Steel-sfreet—rr cowed severe burns, which necessitated the!r removal to the infirmary. Seed's condition 15 not so serious, but Podmore is suffering from extensive burns to the hands, arms and faa- It appears that Seed, who is an employe of the Cheshire Lines Railway Company, is fond et experimenting. He invited Podmore to a be at his home, where they. were making use a e' quantity of gunpowder. Podmore was some wax, when either the match or the meltee; wax dropped on to the powder, causing al loua explosion, with the,above results. A neighbour applied oil to the burns and the boys Were immediately taken to the Infirmary, where they are progressing as well as can be expected-</p>
      </div>
