        <p>of volunteers. In the event of an invasion the immediate danger would not be from the Kaiser's soldiers, but from the aliens who are ' masquerading as friends in our midst, who, , in the absence of a force to keep them in order, would secretly attack our railways and our works. Volunteers would be very valu- able as guards—perhaps more valuable than Regulars, as they know their own districts well. It is a small thing for those who escape from active service to decide to help in the work of defending their own homes. Better treatment is now being accorded the Volun- teers, and there should be a better response to the solicitations of those who bare calling for recruits.</p>
        <p>WINS D.C.M. BEAMONT SCHOOL OLD COY • Jubilation Yesterday. . • Only 18 years of age, Private Joseph Heesom, of the Royal Welsh Fh4liers. ~‘,l of Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Heesom, 31, Orford-avenue, has won the D.C.M., for valyble services rendered during a bombing expedition -a few weeks ago. It was in a letter dated May 11th that Private Heesom first mentioned the bombing exploit, when he wrote :—" I am still having good luck, , but I shall never forget the other night. I went oht with a party and raided the German ' trenches, and we did give it them. I. shall never forget when we were coming back. again, either. But don't , worry, I am quite all right. We got commended by the .Divisional Com- mander and also by the Brigadier-General. The Colonel asked me how old I was, and when I</p>
