        <p>MR. A. ,H. POLLEN. From Grand Fleet To Royal Court Theatre Next Week. Mr. A. H. Pollen, the great naval expert, was to have been at the Royal Court Theatre to-day, but an invitation to view the Grand Fleet im- pelled him to cancel the engagement, and he is now having a wonderful experience at sea. He will return towards the end of the week, and on Saturday, June 3rd, come to the Royal Court and tell us all about it. We are, indeed, for- ; tunate. We shall get our Navy news quite hot [ and straight from the horse's mouth, as a popu- lar phrase has it. All England knows Mr. Pollen as a writer and lecturer. In his first ,capacity we are continu- ally judging of his merits and awarding him the palm for his knowledge and lucidity. As a lecturer, he is equally successful, and his plat- form expositions have frequently attracted Mr. Balfour, in fact, that gentleman recently took the chair at one of his lectures and paid him a high tribute. The Difference. A Dublin office boy, returning to his employ- ment after a fortnight's absence, informed his employer that he had been fighting and a prisoner; whereas, of course, in similar circum- stances, an English lad would have contented himself with explaining that he had merely been taking the letters to the post.—" Punch." The Last Straw. Arthur: "They say, dear, that people who live together get to look alike." Kate: "Then you must consider my refusal as final."</p>
